  { id:'opqb-t6-224', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A recurrent bilateral conjunctivitis occurring with the onset of **hot weather** in young boys with symptoms of burning, itching, and lacrimation with **large flat topped cobble stone papillae** raised areas in the palpebral conjunctiva is:',
    options:['Trachoma','Phlyctenular conjunctivitis','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','Vernal keratoconjunctivitis'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Every clause of this stem is a diagnostic criterion for vernal keratoconjunctivitis, and the last one — flat-topped cobblestone papillae — is what makes it unambiguous.** This is the topic's anchor entry for VKC; the fourteen other VKC questions in this bank point back here rather than repeat the tables.\n\n**Why each clause counts.** *Recurrent* and *with the onset of hot weather*: book p.88 makes VKC \"seasonally recurrent (hence the name **vernal** meaning **springtime**)\" and attributes its high incidence in hot regions to \"a higher level of pollution by pollens and various other allergens\". *Bilateral*: the book prints it as typically bilateral. *Young boys*: \"The majority of VKC occurs in patients between the **ages of 5-25 years old** with an average age of onset between **10-12 years old**\", resolving spontaneously \"in the **late second decade of life**\" — which is why this bank's VKC vignettes are all boys of ten to twelve (`opqb-t6-228`, `opqb-t6-280`). *Burning, itching, lacrimation*: the book heads the symptom list with a named quotation, \" The morning Misery (1988 by Buckley, R.J.) \", and lists severe itching, photophobia, foreign body sensation and mucous ropy discharge.\n\n**The signs of VKC — the table this bank asks from every side.** Book p.88 divides them three ways, and `L6) Conjunctiva` photographs each division without naming any of it:\n\n| Division | Signs | Where this bank asks it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Conjunctival** | Diffuse conjunctival injection; **tarsal giant papillae**, flat-topped, cobblestone, >1 mm | here, `opqb-t6-272`, `opqb-t6-280`, `opqb-t6-286` |\n| **Limbal** | Limbal papillae bearing whitish dots (**Tranta spots**) of degenerated eosinophils; limbal stem cell deficiency → corneal neovascularization | `opqb-t6-228`, `opqb-t6-231`, `opqb-t6-236`, `opqb-t6-271` |\n| **Corneal** | Punctate epithelial erosions; **shield ulcer** in the upper 1/3; **pseudo-gerontoxon**; keratoconus | `opqb-t6-264`, `opqb-t6-286` |\n\n`L6` slide 38 is titled \"Vernal KeratoConjunctivitis\" over a close-up of an everted upper lid \"covered by LARGE, FLAT-TOPPED, POLYGONAL GIANT PAPILLAE packed edge to edge in a cobblestone mosaic\", and slide 14, titled only \"Papillae\", shows the same architecture with the detail that each elevation is \"red at its centre — a central vessel core\". That vascular core is the structural definition: book p.86 says papillae \"have a **fibrovascular core** (in contrast to follicles)\", and giant papillae are those \"**more than 1mm in diameter**\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it would have shown instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Trachoma** | **Follicles**, not papillae — pale, rounded, avascular-topped, with vessels running *around* rather than into them, and maximal on the **upper** tarsus. Trachoma is a chronic chlamydial infection of poverty and overcrowding, not a seasonal itch. Anchor entry `opqb-t6-230`. |\n| **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** | A small raised nodule on the bulbar conjunctiva or limbus with a **localised leash of vessels** running to it, and redness confined to that sector — `L6` slides 46–48. Not bilateral, not seasonal, no papillae. See `opqb-t6-239`. |\n| **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | **Mucopurulent discharge** and lids matted on waking, with papillae that are fine and velvety, not giant. The dominant symptom is grittiness, not itching. See `opqb-t6-229`. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The single most discriminating symptom in the whole topic is **itch**: book p.94's three-column grid rates itching *mild* in viral and bacterial disease and **intense** in allergic disease, and pairs it with mucoid ropy discharge and cobblestone papillae. Hot weather, a boy, intense itch, ropy discharge, cobblestones — the diagnosis is made before the slit lamp is switched on.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is the seasonal, bilateral, intensely itchy conjunctivitis of boys aged 5–25 whose defining sign is giant flat-topped cobblestone papillae on the upper tarsal conjunctiva.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q224), key p.41' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-225', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A 12-year-old boy receiving **long term treatment for spring catarrh** developed defective vision in both eyes. The likely cause is:',
    options:['Posterior subcapsular cataract','Retinopathy of prematurity','Optic neuritis','Vitreous hemorrhage'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The drug is never named in the stem, and that is the whole question: \"long term treatment for spring catarrh\" means long-term topical corticosteroid, and the lens change that topical steroid causes is a posterior subcapsular cataract.** Bilateral, painless, gradual loss of vision in a child on chronic steroid drops is that cataract until proved otherwise.\n\n**The material warns about the drug without naming the complication.** Book p.89's VKC treatment list allows topical \"steroid to control acute attacks but **avoid long-term use**\", and the AKC list on the same page says corticosteroids \"should be **restricted to controlling the acute** symptoms **to avoid the complications associated with prolonged use**\". The bank's own printed box at `opqb-t6-287` repeats it: \"Topical steroid to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use.\" ⚠️ **None of those sources says what the complications are.** Book ch.6's own not-covered list records it explicitly — steroid \"complications are alluded to… but never named — no glaucoma, no cataract, no herpes reactivation\" — and `L6) Conjunctiva` contains no pharmacology at all (steroid = 0 hits in all 66 slides). *That the complication is a posterior subcapsular cataract, and that steroid-induced glaucoma is its companion, is therefore **not taken from the course material**; it is standard, uncontested pharmacology, and it is what this question is testing.*\n\n**Why posterior subcapsular, and why it blurs vision early.** The opacity forms as a plaque immediately in front of the posterior capsule — on the visual axis, at the node of the optical system — so a very small opacity costs a great deal of acuity, far more than a peripheral cortical change of the same size. It is worse in bright light and for near work, because a constricted pupil is masked by it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Retinopathy of prematurity** | A disease of the **premature neonate's** developing retinal vasculature. It cannot begin at twelve years of age, and nothing in the stem mentions prematurity. |\n| **Optic neuritis** | Typically **unilateral**, of subacute onset over days, with **pain on eye movement**, a relative afferent pupillary defect and dyschromatopsia. Steroids treat it; they do not cause it. |\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage** | Sudden, usually unilateral, with floaters and a lost red reflex. It has no association with topical steroid or with spring catarrh. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Two things must be checked at every review of a child on topical steroid for VKC — **the lens and the intraocular pressure** — because both complications are silent until they are advanced, and the child will not complain. That is precisely why book p.89 offers **cyclosporine and tacrolimus** as \"**steroid-sparing agents**\" for severe cases, and why `opqb-t6-287` keys systemic corticosteroids as the one thing management must *not* include.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Cataract — the lesion itself is a lens opacity, but the question is testing the complication of treating a conjunctival disease.)*",
    objective:'Long-term topical corticosteroid for spring catarrh causes bilateral posterior subcapsular cataract — and steroid-induced glaucoma alongside it; the material warns against prolonged use but never names either complication.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q225), key p.41' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-226', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A young child suffering from **fever and sore throat** began to complain of lacrimation. On examination, **follicles** were found in the **lower** palpebral conjunctiva with **tender preauricular lymph nodes**. The most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Trachoma','Staphylococcal conjunctivitis','Adenoviral conjunctivitis','Phlyctenular conjunctivitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Fever plus sore throat plus a follicular conjunctivitis plus tender preauricular nodes is pharyngoconjunctival fever, and pharyngoconjunctival fever is adenovirus.** The bank never uses the phrase; it prints the three components and expects the name.\n\n**Each finding is a printed one.** Book p.90 makes adenovirus \"the most frequent causative agent (**90% of cases**)\" of viral conjunctivitis, itself \"the **most common external ocular infection**\", and names **Pharyngoconjunctival fever** as one of its five clinical presentations, \"Caused by adenovirus serovars **3 , 4 and 7**\". Its signs list gives \"Conjunctival **follicles** (mainly in the **lower** tarsal conjunctiva)\" — the stem's *lower* palpebral conjunctiva is not decoration, it is the discriminator against trachoma. **Preauricular lymphadenopathy** appears twice: in the general clinical-features list on p.86 (viral, chlamydial, severe bacterial) and again in the viral signs list on p.90. And the accompanying illness is printed too: the non-specific acute follicular form comes with \"**Sore throat** or an accompanying common cold\".\n\n**Follicle position is the cheapest sign in this topic:**\n\n| Follicles maximal in the… | Think |\n|---|---|\n| **LOWER** tarsus and fornix | **Viral** (adenoviral) conjunctivitis |\n| **UPPER** tarsus | **Trachoma** — book p.92 makes active trachoma \"**Upper tarsal** follicular conjunctivitis\" |\n| **Upper AND lower** tarsus | **Adult chlamydial (inclusion)** conjunctivitis, book p.91 |\n\n`L6) Conjunctiva` teaches the same contrast entirely by photograph: slide 34, titled \"Viral Conjunctivitis\", is an everted **lower** lid with pale glistening follicles, while slide 25, \"Chlamydial Conjunctivitis (Trachoma)\", is an everted **upper** lid.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Trachoma** | Follicles on the **upper** tarsus, chronic rather than acute, with mucopurulent discharge, and no fever or pharyngitis. It is a disease of \"poverty, overcrowding and poor hygiene\" (book p.92) with re-infection cycles, not a two-day febrile illness. |\n| **Staphylococcal conjunctivitis** | Bacterial disease gives **papillae**, not follicles, and **mucopurulent or purulent** discharge rather than watering — book p.94's grid puts *Papillae* in the bacterial column and *Follicles + preauricular lymphadenopathy* in the viral one. Preauricular nodes in bacterial disease are essentially confined to gonococcal and meningococcal infection. |\n| **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** | A localised nodule with a sectoral vessel leash (`opqb-t6-239`), no follicles, no fever, no nodes. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Say the diagnosis and then say the public-health sentence with it: book p.90 calls adenoviral disease \"**highly contagious**\", spread \"by contact with respiratory or ocular secretions\", and resolving spontaneously in **2–3 weeks** with no specific treatment — so what the family needs is hand hygiene, separate towels and time, not an antibiotic. This bank keys the same organism to an Egyptian epidemic at `opqb-t6-273`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Fever, sore throat, LOWER tarsal follicles and tender preauricular nodes is pharyngoconjunctival fever — adenovirus; follicle position separates viral (lower) from trachomatous (upper) disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q226), key p.41' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-227', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Patching** of the eye is **contraindicated** in:',
    options:['Corneal abrasion','Bacterial corneal ulcer','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','After glaucoma surgery'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A pad over a discharging eye turns the conjunctival sac into a warm, closed, undrained incubator — so the one condition on this list in which patching is forbidden is the one that is producing discharge.** Book p.91 gives the instruction as the first line of the treatment of acute bacterial conjunctivitis: \"**Avoid eye patching** and stop the use of contact lenses by the patient.\"\n\n**This bank asks the same principle three times in one topic**, which is a fair signal that it is examinable:\n\n| Question | Form of the question | Key |\n|---|---|---|\n| this one | Patching contraindicated in… | **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** |\n| `opqb-t6-238` | Treatment of MPC, all true EXCEPT… | \"Eye bandages are used to avoid photophobia\" |\n| `opqb-t6-247` | Tarsorrhaphy contraindicated in… | **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** |\n\nA pad, a bandage and a tarsorrhaphy differ only in how long the lids stay shut; the objection is identical. Retained pus macerates the epithelium and the commonest serious consequence is a corneal ulcer — which is exactly what this bank keys at `opqb-t6-260`.\n\n**Why patching is at worst acceptable in the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not the contraindication |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal abrasion** | A clean epithelial defect with no infection. A pad splints the lid off the healing edge and relieves pain; it is the traditional treatment, and although modern practice often prefers a bandage contact lens or no patch at all, it is not *contraindicated*. |\n| **Bacterial corneal ulcer** | The strongest distractor, because an infected cornea also should not be padded — but note the difference in mechanism: here the objection is that a pad hides a progressing infection from hourly review and cannot be given with the hourly fortified drops the ulcer needs. The bank keys the discharging conjunctivitis, and against a discharging conjunctivitis the objection is absolute. |\n| **After glaucoma surgery** | A pad and shield are **routine** immediately after intraocular surgery, to protect the bleb and the wound. Patching here is the standard of care, not a contraindication. |\n\n*(The abrasion, ulcer and post-operative statements above are **not taken from the course material** — book ch.6 prints only the four-word instruction \"Avoid eye patching\", `L6) Conjunctiva` contains no management line of any kind, and neither source discusses patching in any other disease. They are standard, uncontested practice, given because the bank offers all four as options.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The rule is about **drainage, not comfort**. A patient with a sticky, photophobic eye will ask for a pad and it is the wrong thing to give: the discharge must be able to leave the sac, be washed away and be sampled. Book p.91's other instruction in the same line — **stop the contact lenses** — obeys the same logic, and `opqb-t6-234` shows what prolonged lens wear does to the tarsal conjunctiva.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Never patch a discharging eye: book p.91 says “Avoid eye patching” in bacterial conjunctivitis, because a closed sac retains pus and the commonest serious consequence is a corneal ulcer.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q227), key p.41' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-228', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A ten-year-old boy complains of **itching**. On examination, there are **mucoid nodules with smooth rounded surface on the limbus**, and mucous white **ropy** conjunctival discharge. He most probably suffers from:',
    options:['Trachoma','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','Bulbar spring catarrh','Purulent conjunctivitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Gelatinous nodules sitting on the limbus, in an itching ten-year-old with ropy discharge, are the LIMBAL form of vernal keratoconjunctivitis — the \"bulbar\" spring catarrh this bank distinguishes from the palpebral form.** The clinical picture, the age band and the signs table are set out in full at `opqb-t6-224`; this entry covers only what separates the bulbar type from the palpebral one.\n\n**The two forms, as this bank uses the words:**\n\n| Form | Where the disease sits | What you see |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Palpebral** spring catarrh | Upper **tarsal** conjunctiva | Giant flat-topped **cobblestone papillae** — `opqb-t6-272`, `opqb-t6-280` |\n| **Bulbar (limbal)** spring catarrh | The **limbus**, 360° or in sectors | Raised **gelatinous** thickening bearing chalky **white dots** — here, `opqb-t6-231`, `opqb-t6-271` |\n\nBook p.88's limbal-signs row prints the substance of the nodule and the dots on it: \"Limbal papillae with whitish dots (**Tranta spots**) that consist of degenerated **eosinophils**\", and warns that \"Limbal disease can result in a **limbal stem cell deficiency** which can lead to *corneal neovascularization*\" — the reason the bulbar form is the more sight-threatening of the two.\n\n**`L6) Conjunctiva` devotes four consecutive slides to exactly this appearance and names none of it.** Slide 39 photographs both eyes with \"raised, gelatinous, greyish-pink thickened swellings carrying small discrete WHITE DOTS\" at the limbus, with a black arrow printed on the picture pointing straight at the dots and no caption. Slide 40 is the slit-lamp close-up: \"a row of discrete, chalky WHITE DOTS… on the crest of a raised, thickened, gelatinous limbal band\". Slide 41 shows the band \"thick, opaque, creamy yellow-white\" with corneal haze spreading down from it, and slide 42 shows a \"creamy tan-yellow GELATINOUS COLLAR\" encircling the cornea **360°**. Measured across all 66 slides, the word *Trantas* returns **zero hits** — the sign is photographed four times, arrowed once, and never named.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Trachoma** | Upper tarsal **follicles** and mucopurulent discharge, not limbal gelatinous nodules; and trachoma does not itch. Its own limbal follicles resolve into **Herbert's pits** (`opqb-t6-288`), which are depressions, not raised nodules. |\n| **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | **Mucopurulent** discharge and grittiness, lids matted on waking (`opqb-t6-270`); itching is mild and there are no limbal masses. |\n| **Purulent conjunctivitis** | Frank **pus**, gross lid oedema, an acutely ill eye and a real risk of corneal perforation — `L6` slides 18–21 photograph it in a neonate. Nothing in this stem is acute or purulent. |\n\n**⚠️ A defect in the stem, recorded and not corrected.** It prints \"mucous white ropy **mucopurulent** conjunctival discharge\", calling the same discharge both mucoid-ropy and mucopurulent in one phrase. Book p.94's grid assigns **mucoid (ropy)** discharge to allergic disease and **mucopurulent/purulent** to bacterial disease, so the two halves of the phrase point at different diagnoses. The itch, the age and the limbal nodules settle it; the key stands as printed at C.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Raised gelatinous limbal nodules bearing chalky white Tranta spots, in an itching child with ropy discharge, are the bulbar (limbal) form of spring catarrh — the form that threatens the limbal stem cells.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q228), key p.41' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-229', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In **viral epidemic kerato-conjunctivitis** characteristically there is usually:',
    options:['Copious purulent discharge','Copious muco-purulent discharge','Excessive watery lacrimation','Mucoid ropy white discharge'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Discharge is the single most useful sign in this whole topic, because each of the four kinds belongs to a different disease — and viral disease gives WATERING.** This is the anchor entry for the discharge tables; the other discharge questions in the topic point back here.\n\n**Book p.85 prints the four-column table this question is built on:**\n\n| **Watery** | **Mucoid** | **Mucopurulent** | **Purulent** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Viral** | Allergic | Chlamydial | Acute bacterial infection |\n| Allergic conjunctivitis | Toxic (chemical) & dry eye | Acute bacterial infection | Severe purulent discharge suggests **gonococcal** |\n\nand book p.94's three-column discriminating grid says the same thing from the other side:\n\n| | Viral | Bacterial | Allergic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Itching** | Mild | Mild | **Intense** |\n| **Discharge** | **Watery** | Mucopurulent / purulent | Mucoid (ropy) |\n| **Signs** | Follicles + preauricular lymphadenopathy | Papillae | Papillae (cobblestone) |\n\n**Why epidemic keratoconjunctivitis in particular.** Book p.90 makes EKC one of the five adenoviral presentations, \"Caused by adenovirus serovars **8 , 19 and 37**\", and \"**The most severe** ocular adenoviral infection\" — \"Keratitis and consequently **photophobia** are marked\" and \"Cornea may become chronically affected.\" The *kerato-* half of the name is earned by the subepithelial infiltrates, which book p.86 explains as lymphocytic infiltration \"deep to the corneal epithelium… as in epidemic keratoconjunctivitis caused by **Adenovirus** infection\". `L6` slide 36 is a slit-lamp photograph of exactly those infiltrates — \"multiple small, round, discrete, WHITE-GREY subepithelial corneal OPACITIES\" — with no caption on the slide.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Whose discharge it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Copious purulent discharge** | **Acute bacterial**, and copious purulent means **gonococcal** until disproved — book p.85's own N.B. `L6` slides 18–20 photograph it. |\n| **Copious muco-purulent discharge** | **Chlamydial** disease and acute bacterial infection. The trap, because EKC's conjunctival sac is wet and the untrained eye calls any wetness discharge. |\n| **Mucoid ropy white discharge** | **Allergic** disease — book p.88 makes \"Mucous **ropy**\" discharge a VKC symptom, and `L6` prints no discharge vocabulary at all beyond its slide title \"Discharge\". Keyed at `opqb-t6-280`. |\n\n**⚠️ This question is printed twice in this topic.** The second printing, six pages later on p.39 as Q277, has the same four options in the same order and the same key; it differs only in dropping \"usually\" from the stem and in two option spellings. Both printings are kept because both are printed.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Ask *what comes out of the eye* before anything else. Watery and follicular with a tender node in front of the ear — viral. Sticky and papillary with the lids gummed on waking — bacterial. Ropy and itchy with cobblestones — allergic. Torrential and purulent in a neonate or a sexually active adult — gonococcal, and that one is an emergency.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Watery discharge is viral, mucoid ropy is allergic, mucopurulent is chlamydial or bacterial, and copious purulent is gonococcal — epidemic keratoconjunctivitis is adenoviral and therefore waters.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q229), key p.41 — and REPRINTED at p.39 (Q277), key p.42, with the same key and no option replaced, only respelled (\"muco-purulent\"/\"muco purulent\", \"Mucoid\"/\"Mucous ropy white\"). Held once, per the within-bank reprint rule' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-230', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Corneal **Herbert's rosettes** are found in:",
    options:['Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis','Active trachoma','Spring catarrh'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Cicatricial trachoma:**\nIt is prevalent in middle age.\n1. Linear or stellate conjunctival scars in mild cases, or broad confluent scars (Arlt' s line) in severe disease. Scarring is most prominent on the upper tarsal plate.\n2. **Superior limbal follicles may resolve to leave a row of shallow depressions (Herbert pits).**\n3. Post- trachomatous concretions (PTCs) cause chronic irritation and foreign body sensation.\n4. Trichiasis, corneal vascularization and cicatricial entropion\n5. Severe corneal opacification.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim including \"Arlt' s line\" with its stray space. The notes below are added for study. This is the topic's anchor entry for trachoma; the other trachoma questions point back here.)*\n\n**⚠️ The box answers a different question from the one asked, and the difference is worth holding on to.** The stem asks about **rosettes**; the box describes **cicatricial** trachoma and mentions rosettes nowhere. The two are the same structure at two ages:\n\n| Stage | The limbal lesion | Named |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Active** | **Herbert's rosettes** — superior limbal **follicles**, raised, pale, arranged along the upper limbus | not in either source |\n| **Cicatricial** | **Herbert's pits** — \"a row of **shallow depressions**\" left when those follicles resolve | book p.92 |\n\nSo the follicle is *active* and infectious, and the pit it leaves behind is *cicatricial* and not. The key is **Active trachoma** and it is correct: the rosette is the live follicle. *(The word \"rosette\" appears in no cached source — zero hits across all 36 ophthalmology decks and all 12 book chapters. That Herbert's rosettes are the active superior limbal follicles which later become Herbert's pits is **not taken from the course material**; it is the standard usage, and the bank's own key requires it.)*\n\n**The trachoma table this topic asks from eight directions.** Book p.92 divides the disease and grades it:\n\n| | **Active trachoma** | **Cicatricial trachoma** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who | \"most common in **preschool children**\" | \"prevalent in **middle age**\" |\n| Conjunctiva | **Upper tarsal follicular** conjunctivitis + mucopurulent discharge; papillary reaction may be present | Linear/stellate scars → broad confluent **Arlt's line**, worst on the upper tarsal plate |\n| Limbus | **Herbert's rosettes** | **Herbert's pits** |\n| Cornea | Superior epithelial keratitis; **pannus** (superficial vascularization + cellular infiltration) | Severe corneal opacification |\n| Lid | — | **Trichiasis**, **cicatricial entropion** |\n| Other | — | **PTCs**, causing chronic irritation and foreign body sensation |\n| Infectious? | **Yes** — `opqb-t6-233` | No |\n\n**WHO grading**, book p.93, five grades and the only grading system in the module (MacCallan is recorded absent by both sources):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Threshold |\n|---|---|---|\n| **TF** | Trachomatous inflammation, follicular | **5 or more** follicles **>0.5 mm** on the superior tarsal plate |\n| **TI** | Trachomatous inflammation, intense | tarsal conjunctiva obscuring **50 % or more** of the deep tarsal vessels |\n| **TS** | Trachomatous conjunctival scarring | easily visible fibrous white tarsal bands |\n| **TT** | Trachomatous trichiasis | **at least one lash** touching the globe |\n| **CO** | Corneal opacity | enough to blur part of the pupillary margin |\n\n**Management — the SAFE strategy** (book p.93): **S**urgery for trichiasis, **A**ntibiotics for active disease, **F**acial hygiene, **E**nvironmental improvement. Drugs: a single dose of **azithromycin 20 mg/kg up to 1 g** is the treatment of choice; alternatives **erythromycin 500 mg bd × 14 days** or **doxycycline 100 mg bd × 10 days** (tetracyclines contraindicated in pregnancy, breastfeeding and **children under 12**); topical 1 % tetracycline ointment is less effective than oral. Antibiotics go to the patient **and all family members**, and communities may need **annual** treatment.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** **mucopurulent conjunctivitis** produces papillae and discharge, no limbal follicles; **phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis** produces a single raised nodule with a sectoral vessel leash, not a row (`opqb-t6-239`); **spring catarrh** produces limbal papillae bearing **Tranta spots** — raised chalky dots, not follicles and not depressions — which is the trap this bank sets deliberately at `opqb-t6-288`.\n\n⚠️ `L6) Conjunctiva` devotes nine slides to trachoma (25–33) and names **not one** of these signs: *Herbert* = 0 hits, *Arlt* = 0, *WHO*/*TF*/*TI*/*TS*/*TT*/*CO* = 0, *MacCallan* = 0, and no organism, drug or dose anywhere in its 66 slides. It titles \"PTCs\" on slide 15 and never expands the abbreviation. All of the above is the book's.",
    objective:'Herbert’s rosettes are the ACTIVE superior limbal follicles of trachoma; they resolve into Herbert’s pits, which are cicatricial. Active = follicles, pannus, infectious; cicatricial = Arlt’s line, pits, PTCs, trichiasis, entropion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q230), key p.41 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-231', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"**Trant's spots** are noticed in cases of:",
    options:['Active trachoma','Bulbar spring catarrh','Corneal phlycten','Vitamin A deficiency'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Signs of VKC:**\n1. Conjunctival signs: Diffuse conjunctival injection and Tarsal giant papillae\n2. Limbal signs:\n- **Limbal papillae with whitish dots (Tranta spots)** that eosinophils consist of degenerated\n- Limbal disease can result in a limbal stem cell deficiency which can lead to corneal neovascularization\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. Its second sentence is scrambled as printed — book p.88, from which it is copied, reads \"that consist of degenerated **eosinophils**\". The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Trantas dots are the chalky white specks that sit on the crest of the gelatinous limbal papillae of vernal keratoconjunctivitis, and they are collections of degenerated eosinophils.** That composition is why `opqb-t6-236` keys eosinophils as the characteristic cell of vernal catarrh: the sign and the cytology are the same fact.\n\n**⚠️ THIS BANK PRINTS THE EPONYM FOUR DIFFERENT WAYS INSIDE THIS ONE TOPIC**, and a reader who learns only one spelling will not recognise the others:\n\n| Where | As printed |\n|---|---|\n| this stem | **Trant's spots** |\n| this box, four lines lower | **Tranta spots** |\n| `opqb-t6-261` option B | **Taranta's spots** |\n| `opqb-t6-288` option B | **Trantas dots** |\n\nBook p.88 prints \"**Tranta spots**\" and labels its Fig 6-4 the same way. The usual textbook form is *Horner–Trantas dots*, which occurs nowhere in this module.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture deck photographs the sign four times and never names it.** `L6) Conjunctiva` slides 39–42 are all the limbal form of VKC: slide 39 has \"a single black ARROW… pointing to the white dots at the upper limbus\" with no caption; slide 40 is the slit-lamp close-up of \"a row of discrete, chalky WHITE DOTS… on the crest of a raised, thickened, gelatinous limbal band\"; slide 41 shows the band overhanging the cornea with haze spreading down from it; slide 42 shows a **360°** gelatinous collar. Measured over all 66 slides, *Trantas* = **zero hits**. The name is the book's alone.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What sits at its limbus instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Active trachoma** | **Herbert's rosettes** — superior limbal *follicles*, which resolve into the *depressions* called Herbert's pits (`opqb-t6-230`). Raised pale follicles, not chalky dots on a gelatinous band. |\n| **Corneal phlycten** | A single small raised nodule at or near the limbus with a **leash of dilated vessels** running to it — `L6` slides 46–48 photograph three of them. One lesion, one vessel bundle, not a row of dots. |\n| **Vitamin A deficiency** | A **Bitôt spot** — `L6` slide 62 photographs it: a triangular patch of \"CHALKY GREYISH-WHITE material with a dry, FOAMY, BUBBLY, CHEESY surface\" heaped on a dry, lustreless, wrinkled conjunctiva. Genuinely chalky and genuinely white, which is why it is the second-best distractor here — but it lies **on the bulbar conjunctiva in the interpalpebral zone**, the surrounding conjunctiva is xerotic, and there is no gelatinous limbal papilla underneath it. ⚠️ The deck titles the slide \"Bitôt Spot\" and says nothing more; *vitamin A*, *xerosis*, *measles* and *malnutrition* all return zero across all 66 slides, and book ch.6 does not mention Bitôt spots at all. *(That Bitôt spots are the conjunctival sign of vitamin A deficiency is therefore **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Chalky white at the limbus has two answers and the background settles which: **on a raised gelatinous band in an itching child** it is Trantas and the disease is VKC; **on a dry wrinkled lustreless conjunctiva** it is a Bitôt spot and the child needs vitamin A.",
    objective:'Trantas dots are chalky collections of degenerated eosinophils on the limbal papillae of vernal keratoconjunctivitis — this bank spells the eponym four different ways in one topic.',
    source:"ophthalmology qb.pdf p.33 (Q231), key p.41 (explanation box printed)" },

  { id:'opqb-t6-232', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A **painful, tender, non-itchy localized** redness of the conjunctiva can be due to:',
    options:['Bulbar spring catarrh.','Episcleritis.','Vascular pterygium.','Phlyctenular conjunctivitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Four adjectives, and between them they exclude every option but one.** *Localized* rules out the diffuse conjunctivitides; *non-itchy* rules out allergy; and *painful and tender* is the pair that separates episcleritis from the other localised lesions.\n\n**Tenderness is the printed sign.** Book ch.7's episcleritis/scleritis comparison lists the episcleritis symptoms as \"**Red eye**, acute onset (simple episcleritis) or gradual onset (nodular); **Irritation**: discomfort, photophobia; **Tenderness**; **No visual affection**\", and its signs include \"**Tender** episcleral nodules that can be easily mobilized over the sclera\". *Mobile over the sclera* is the bedside test that also distinguishes episcleritis from scleritis, where the deeper vessels do not move.\n\n**How the localised red eyes separate:**\n\n| Lesion | Painful? | Tender? | Itchy? | The give-away |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Episcleritis** | Yes, aching | **Yes** | No | Sectoral flush; the nodule **moves** over the sclera on a cotton bud |\n| **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** | Irritable, gritty, photophobic | Not characteristically | No | A discrete **nodule with a vessel leash** running to it |\n| **Bulbar spring catarrh** | No | No | **Intensely** | Gelatinous limbal band with **chalky white dots** |\n| **Vascular pterygium** | No | No | No | A **triangular** fold with a base at the canthus and an apex on the cornea |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n- **Bulbar spring catarrh** is defeated by one word: *non-itchy*. Itch is the cardinal allergic symptom — book p.94's grid grades it **intense** in allergic disease against *mild* in viral and bacterial. See `opqb-t6-228`.\n- **Vascular pterygium** is localised and it is red — book p.95 calls it \"Fibrovascular, triangular, wing-like encroachment\" and `L6` slide 50 photographs \"straight, prominent RED VESSELS\" running within it — but it is characteristically **painless**, its complaints are cosmetic, gritty or astigmatic, and it does not present as an acutely tender spot. It is the second-best distractor, since a chronically inflamed pterygium is one of the book's own indications for excision (p.95).\n- **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** is the closest miss and the reason this question is worth doing. It is genuinely localised — `L6` slide 46 records that \"The injection is localised to the sectors carrying the nodules — it is not the diffuse redness of the earlier conjunctivitis pictures\" — and it is genuinely uncomfortable and photophobic. What it is not is *tender to touch*, and it is not primarily painful. This bank keys phlycten to the localised-redness stem at `opqb-t6-245`, where the words *painful* and *tender* are absent; the two questions are a deliberate pair and the adjectives are the whole difference between them.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A sector of red eye is a small differential, and it is settled by touching it through the closed lid and by moving the vessels with a cotton bud. *(The mobility test and the episcleritis-versus-scleritis vessel-blanching manoeuvre are **not taken from the course material** beyond book ch.7's phrase \"easily mobilized over the sclera\"; the rest is standard bedside practice.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Sclera — episcleritis is taught in book ch.7 and belongs to the scleral chapter, but the question is testing the differential diagnosis of a localised red conjunctiva.)*",
    objective:'Painful, tender, non-itchy, localised redness is episcleritis; localised and non-tender with a vessel leash is a phlycten; localised and intensely itchy is bulbar spring catarrh.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q232), key p.41' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-233', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In **trachoma** the patient is **infectious** when there is:',
    options:["Arlt's line","Herbert's pits",'Post-trachomatous concretions.','Follicles and papillae in the palpebral conjunctiva.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three of these four options are scars. Only one is a live inflammation, and infectivity belongs to the live disease.** Book p.92 divides trachoma into an \"'**active**' inflammatory stage\" and a \"'**cicatricial**' chronic stage\", and defines the active stage as \"**Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis** associated with mucopurulent discharge\" with a papillary reaction that \"can also be present\". Follicles and papillae on the palpebral conjunctiva is that definition, word for word.\n\n**Why activity means infectivity.** The organism is *Chlamydia trachomatis* serovars **A, B, Ba and C** (book p.92), and it is the discharge from an actively inflamed conjunctiva that carries it — book p.92 attributes trachoma's visual morbidity to \"**re-infection cycles within communities**\" and relates the disease to \"poverty, overcrowding and poor hygiene\". Two of the four arms of the **SAFE** strategy exist purely to break that transmission: **F**acial hygiene and **E**nvironmental improvement, which is exactly what `opqb-t6-252` keys. Once the conjunctiva has scarred, the chlamydia has gone; what remains is mechanical damage done by the scar, and it is not transmissible.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is a cicatricial sign:**\n\n| Option | What it is | Stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Arlt's line** | \"Broad confluent scars\" of severe disease, \"most prominent on the upper tarsal plate\" (book p.92); mild cases give linear or stellate scars instead | Cicatricial |\n| **Herbert's pits** | \"Superior limbal follicles may resolve to leave a **row of shallow depressions**\" (book p.92) — the fossil of an active follicle | Cicatricial |\n| **Post-trachomatous concretions (PTCs)** | Chalky deposits in the scarred tarsus that \"cause chronic irritation and foreign body sensation\" (book p.93); `L6` slide 15 photographs \"a dense row of small, discrete, CREAMY-YELLOW/WHITE rounded nodules\" and titles it only \"PTCs\", never expanding the abbreviation | Cicatricial |\n\nThe full active-versus-cicatricial table, the WHO five-grade system and the SAFE strategy are set out once at `opqb-t6-230` and are not repeated here.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The practical form of this question is the public-health one: when do you treat the whole family? When the disease is **active**. Book p.93 is explicit — \"Antibiotics should be administered to those affected and to **all family members**\", with a single dose of **azithromycin 20 mg/kg up to 1 g** as the treatment of choice, and whole communities may need **annual** treatment because one course \"is not always effective in eliminating infection in an individual\". A patient with Arlt's line and trichiasis needs **surgery**, not azithromycin.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Trachoma is infectious in its ACTIVE stage — upper tarsal follicles and papillae with mucopurulent discharge; Arlt’s line, Herbert’s pits and PTCs are cicatricial scars and carry no organism.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q233), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-234', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A female patient 18 years old, who is **contact lens wearer since two years**, is complaining of redness, lacrimation and foreign body sensation of both eyes. On examination, visual acuity was **6/6** with **negative fluorescein test**. The expected diagnosis can be:',
    options:['Acute anterior uveitis.','Giant papillary conjunctivitis.','Bacterial corneal ulcer.','Acute congestive glaucoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Two years of contact lens wear plus a normal acuity plus an intact epithelium leaves only one diagnosis on this list — and the two negatives are doing as much work as the history.**\n\n**What the negatives exclude.** **6/6** means the visual axis is clear and the eye is optically and neurologically intact; **a negative fluorescein test** means there is no epithelial defect anywhere on the cornea. Book p.94 heads its whole differential of conjunctivitis with the five findings that put a red eye on the conjunctival side of the line: \"**Normal VA, Normal pupil, No photophobia, Clear cornea, Normal AC**.\" This patient has them.\n\n**Why prolonged lens wear does this.** Book p.86 gives giant papillae — \"**Cobble stone appearance** i.e., they are flat-topped… **more than 1mm in diameter**\" — as the product of \"prolonged inflammation and irritation\", and lists the three causes:\n\n| Cause of giant papillae | book p.86 |\n|---|---|\n| **Allergic conjunctivitis especially in vernal catarrh** | 1 |\n| **Prolonged contact lens wear** | 2 |\n| **Foreign body (as in artificial eye users)** | 3 |\n\nThe same three are reprinted as this bank's own box at `opqb-t6-268`. The mechanism is chronic mechanical and antigenic irritation of the upper tarsus by the lens edge and its deposits; the symptoms are exactly the stem's — redness, watering, foreign body sensation, and lens intolerance that builds over months.\n\n**⚠️ Note what the book does NOT do:** ch.6 records that \"Giant papillary conjunctivitis as a named diagnosis with its own section: **absent**. GPC appears only as a figure caption on printed 86 and as a cause-list entry; the objectives flowchart does not list it.\" So the diagnosis is carried by a caption and a list, and by `L6` not at all — its 66 slides return zero for \"giant papillary\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What would be abnormal that is not |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute anterior uveitis** | **Reduced** vision, **ciliary (circumcorneal) injection**, photophobia, a **small irregular pupil**, cells and flare in the anterior chamber. `L6` slide 11, titled \"Ciliary Injection\", photographs precisely the deep circumcorneal flush this patient does not have. |\n| **Bacterial corneal ulcer** | A **positive fluorescein** test by definition — an ulcer is an epithelial defect — plus a white stromal infiltrate and usually reduced acuity. The negative fluorescein excludes it outright, and lens wear is a genuine risk factor, which is what makes it the strongest distractor. |\n| **Acute congestive glaucoma** | A **painful, hard, red eye** with a hazy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, haloes and markedly reduced vision, typically **unilateral** in a hypermetropic older patient. Nothing here fits an 18-year-old with 6/6 in both eyes. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Evert the upper lid — book ch.6 records that lid eversion technique is absent from the text although a dozen figures show everted lids, and `L6` slide 4 teaches it in four wordless panels — because the diagnosis is on the tarsal surface and nowhere else. Management is to stop or change the lens, and `opqb-t6-227`'s rule applies: book p.91 pairs \"Avoid eye patching\" with \"**stop the use of contact lenses**\".\n\n**⚠️ THE BANK PRINTS THIS EXACT QUESTION TWICE — on book p.34 and again on book p.38 as Q269** — the same stem, the same four options in the same order, the same key. The only difference in the entire item is a hyphen in \"18-year-old\" and a full stop on option D. **The two printings are held here as one entry**, per the within-bank reprint rule; the second is not a separate question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Two years of contact lens wear with 6/6 and a negative fluorescein test is giant papillary conjunctivitis — prolonged lens wear is one of the book’s three causes of giant papillae.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q234), key p.42 — and REPRINTED WORD FOR WORD at p.38 (Q269), key p.42, differing only by a hyphen in \"18-year-old\" and a full stop on option D. Held once, per the within-bank reprint rule' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-235', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Organisms causing **angular conjunctivitis** are:',
    options:['Moraxella Axenfeld bacilli','Pneumococci','Gonococci','Adenovirus'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Angular conjunctivitis is inflammation concentrated at the canthi rather than spread over the eye, and the organism attached to it is the Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus, *Moraxella lacunata*.** The bank prints the name as \"Moraxella Axenfeld bacilli\"; the usual form is Morax–Axenfeld.\n\n**The appearance, which is the part the material does teach.** `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 24 is titled \"**Bacterial Conjunctivitis (Angular)**\" and photographs it: the picture is \"centred on the MEDIAL CANTHUS (the angle) of an eye. The skin at the inner canthus is **red, macerated, excoriated and fissured**, with a crust of yellow discharge at the angle itself; the adjacent bulbar conjunctiva is only **moderately injected** and the redness is concentrated **AT THE ANGLE** rather than diffusely over the eye.\" That distribution is the diagnosis — a conjunctivitis that is worst where the lids meet, with macerated skin outside the eye as well as inflammation inside it.\n\n**⚠️ Neither source names the organism, and the absence is precise.** `L6` slide 24 gives the title and the photograph and stops; measured across all 66 slides the deck names **not one organism anywhere** — *Morax/Moraxella* = 0, *Staphyl* = 0, *Streptoc* = 0, *Haemophil* = 0, *Corynebact* = 0, *adenovirus* = 0. Book ch.6's own not-covered list records \"Chronic conjunctivitis as an entity, and **angular conjunctivitis (Moraxella lacunata)**: absent\" — the book names the organism only in the act of recording that it does not teach it. Elsewhere in the module *Moraxella* survives once, in book p.91's list of the commonest organisms of acute bacterial conjunctivitis (\"**H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, S. aureus and Moraxella catarrhalis**\"), which is a different species and a different disease. *(That angular conjunctivitis is caused by the Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus, that the organism digests the skin lipids and so macerates the canthal skin, and that zinc — which inhibits its proteolytic enzyme — is the traditional topical treatment, are **not taken from the course material**; they are standard, uncontested microbiology, and the bank asks for the first of them.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually causes here |\n|---|---|\n| **Pneumococci** | *S. pneumoniae* is one of book p.91's four commonest causes of **acute bacterial (mucopurulent)** conjunctivitis — diffuse, not angular — and it is the type this bank associates with **petechial haemorrhages** at `opqb-t6-250`. |\n| **Gonococci** | *N. gonorrhoeae* causes **hyperacute copious purulent** conjunctivitis with lid oedema, corneal ulceration and preauricular nodes (book p.91), and it is the dangerous organism of ophthalmia neonatorum (`opqb-t6-265`). Book p.91 notes \"Very few cases\" of adult bacterial conjunctivitis are gonococcal. Nothing about it is confined to the angle. |\n| **Adenovirus** | Viral, watery, follicular, bilateral, with tender preauricular nodes (`opqb-t6-226`). Not a bacterium and not angular. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Look at the **skin at the canthi**, not only at the conjunctiva. Excoriated, fissured, crusted angles with a comparatively quiet globe is a different diagnosis from a diffusely red eye, and it is one of the few conjunctivitides in which the lid skin is part of the picture.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Angular conjunctivitis — inflammation and skin maceration concentrated at the canthi — is caused by the Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus; the deck titles and photographs the disease but names no organism anywhere.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q235), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-236', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In **vernal catarrh**, the characteristic cells are:',
    options:['Macrophage','Eosinophils','Neutrophils','Epithelioid cells'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is an allergic disease, and the cell of allergy on the ocular surface is the eosinophil.** The material states it in one line, and states it as the composition of a sign rather than as cytology: book p.88's limbal-signs row reads \"Limbal papillae with whitish dots (**Tranta spots**) that consist of degenerated **eosinophils**\". The bank reprints that same sentence as its own box at `opqb-t6-231`, which is why these two questions answer each other.\n\n**So the sign IS the cytology.** The chalky white dots you see at the limbus in this disease are heaps of dead eosinophils. That is the whole of what the course material says about the cell, and it is enough for this question.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is the characteristic cell of something else:**\n\n| Cell | Where it is the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Macrophage** | Chronic non-granulomatous inflammation generally; not characteristic of any conjunctivitis in this material. |\n| **Neutrophils** | **Acute bacterial** infection — the cell that makes discharge purulent. The trap for anyone who reads \"catarrh\" as \"infection\"; *vernal catarrh* is the old name for spring catarrh and it is allergic, not infective. |\n| **Epithelioid cells** | **Granulomatous** inflammation — tuberculosis, sarcoid, and the granuloma of a chalazion. They are the cells of a delayed (type IV) reaction, which is what makes them the second-best distractor here: **phlyctenulosis** is the type-IV conjunctival disease (`opqb-t6-239`), not VKC. |\n\n*(The assignment of neutrophils to acute bacterial infection and of epithelioid cells to granulomatous inflammation is **not taken from the course material**. Book ch.6 records that it prints no immunology at all — \"The hypersensitivity type (I / IV) underlying any of the allergic forms: absent. No immunology at all — no IgE, no mast-cell mediator list beyond the phrase 'mast cell degranulation'\" — and `L6) Conjunctiva` returns zero for *eosinophil*, *hypersensitiv*, *type I*, *type IV* and *IgE* across all 66 slides. Only the eosinophil, and only via the Tranta-spot sentence, is sourced.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The one mechanistic sentence the book does print explains the treatment: book p.89 tells the patient to \"**avoid eye rubbing** as much as possible (result in **mast cell degranulation** and leads to a vicious circle of more itching and eye rubbing)\", and the bank reprints it as item 1 of its box at `opqb-t6-287`. Mast cells release the mediators, eosinophils arrive and accumulate, and the treatment ladder — mast cell stabilizers, antihistamines, steroid for acute attacks — is aimed at that sequence.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The characteristic cell of vernal catarrh is the eosinophil — the book teaches it only as the composition of Tranta spots, “degenerated eosinophils”.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q236), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-237', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**True membrane** formation occurs in the following conditions:',
    options:['Diphtheritic conjunctivitis.','Allergic conjunctivitis.','Staphylococcal blepharitis','Phlyctenular conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Book p.85 prints a two-column table for exactly this, and it names one cause on each side.** This is the topic's anchor entry for membranes; `opqb-t6-280` and `opqb-t6-286` point back here.\n\n| **True membrane** | **Pseudo-membrane** |\n|---|---|\n| Necrotic and granulation tissue **infiltrates** the conjunctival epithelium (**firm adherence**) | Consists of **coagulated exudates on the surface** of the inflamed **intact** epithelium |\n| Results in **bleeding** when peeling is attempted, due to epithelial destruction and exposure of the underlying stroma | **No bleeding** when peeled |\n| **Cause: *Corynebacterium diphtheriae*** [membranous] conjunctivitis | **Cause:** severe **gonococcal** infection |\n\n⚠️ **Two of those four cells are tutorial-marked in the book and two are not**, and the book ch.6 cache records which: the definitions-by-adherence and the gonococcal cause each end \"→ [T]\", while **the peel-bleeding discriminator carries no marker and is core**. So the sentence to learn for the exam is the middle row.\n\n**The mechanism is the whole distinction.** A true membrane is not lying *on* the conjunctiva, it is *part of* it — necrotic tissue and fibrin have grown into a destroyed epithelium, so stripping it tears the surface and the bed bleeds. A pseudomembrane is coagulated exudate resting on an intact epithelium and lifts away cleanly.\n\n**`L6) Conjunctiva` teaches the same pair as two photographs placed back to back and says nothing.** Slide 22, \"Bacterial Conjunctivitis (**Membranous**)\", shows \"a thick, CREAMY-YELLOW, adherent MEMBRANE with a raised, rolled edge… Fine red vessels run over the surface of the membrane where it is attached, and there is **bright red haemorrhagic staining at its lower edge** — the membrane is incorporated into the epithelium and its removal bleeds.\" Slide 23, \"(**Pseudomembranous**)\", shows \"a thin, WHITISH-GREY, **loosely lying** sheet… flat, dull, without a rolled edge, and **without the bleeding** seen on slide 22.\" The deck names no organism on either slide — *diphther* and *Corynebact* both return zero across all 66 slides.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it produces instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Allergic conjunctivitis** | **Papillae** and mucoid ropy discharge. No membrane of either kind; the surface epithelium is intact and there is no necrosis. |\n| **Staphylococcal blepharitis** | Lid-margin disease — crusting, collarettes, madarosis, recurrent styes, and, per book ch.5, \"Chronic conjunctivitis, and **phlyctens**\" as complications. Not a membrane. |\n| **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** | A discrete raised **nodule** with a vessel leash (`opqb-t6-239`), which may ulcerate at the limbus. A nodule is not a sheet. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Membrane in a child, especially an unimmunised one, means think **diphtheria** — and, in this material's other membrane cause, **gonococcus** means think of the cornea, since book p.91 lists corneal ulceration among the signs of gonococcal and meningococcal infection and adds that systemic antibiotics are needed for both. Peeling a membrane is not a bedside curiosity: if it bleeds, the epithelium underneath has already been destroyed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'True membranes are infiltrated into a destroyed epithelium, adhere firmly and BLEED on peeling — the cause is Corynebacterium diphtheriae; pseudomembranes lie on intact epithelium, peel without bleeding, and are classically gonococcal.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q237), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-238', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In treatment of **mucopurulent conjunctivitis**, all are true **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Frequent washing with boric acid 4%.','Eye bandages are used to avoid photophobia.','Local antibiotic eye drops are used.','Sulphonamide is effective for Hemophilus Aegyptus.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The one thing you must not do to a discharging eye is close it, and that is what option B proposes.** Book p.91 heads the treatment of acute bacterial conjunctivitis with the instruction: \"**Avoid eye patching** and stop the use of contact lenses by the patient.\" A bandage over a sac full of mucopus keeps the pus in, warms it, and stops it draining; the commonest serious consequence is a corneal ulcer, which this bank keys at `opqb-t6-260`. Photophobia in a simple mucopurulent conjunctivitis is mild and is better handled with dark glasses than with a pad — and if the photophobia is severe, the cornea is involved and closing the eye is worse still.\n\nThe same principle is keyed twice more in this topic: `opqb-t6-227` (patching contraindicated in mucopurulent conjunctivitis) and `opqb-t6-247` (tarsorrhaphy contraindicated in the same). A pad, a bandage and a tarsorrhaphy are the same objection at three durations.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Why it stands |\n|---|---|\n| **Frequent washing with boric acid 4 %** | Lavage removes discharge mechanically — the opposite of retaining it — and boric acid lotion is the traditional mild antiseptic irrigant for this purpose. Book p.91's treatment list is only four lines long (avoid patching, strict hygiene, topical antibiotics, systemic antibiotics for gonococcal/meningococcal/H. influenzae) and names no irrigant; *boric* returns zero across all 12 cached book chapters and appears in the whole ophthalmology deck cache only in `L26) Trauma II`. ⚠️ *That boric acid lotion is used as an eye wash is therefore **not taken from the course material**; it is traditional practice, and this bank keys it as true again at `opqb-t6-276`.* |\n| **Local antibiotic eye drops are used** | Book p.91: \"**Topical antibiotics**\", and book p.94's DD grid gives the bacterial column \"Topical antibiotics (Drops and ointment)\" as the whole treatment. ⚠️ Note that **no antibiotic is ever named** — book ch.6 records \"Any named topical antibiotic anywhere in the chapter: **absent**.\" |\n| **Sulphonamide is effective for Hemophilus Aegyptus** | *Haemophilus aegyptius* is the Koch–Weeks bacillus, the classic cause of epidemic mucopurulent conjunctivitis in hot climates, and sulphonamides are its traditional treatment. ⚠️ *This is entirely outside the material — \"aegypt\" returns **zero hits** across all 36 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 cached book chapters, and the closest the book comes is naming \"**H. influenzae**\" among the four commonest organisms of acute bacterial conjunctivitis (p.91) and requiring **systemic** antibiotics for it. **Not taken from the course material.*** The bank spells it \"Hemophilus Aegyptus\" and calls the same organism \"Koch Week's bacilli\" at `opqb-t6-250`. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Mucopurulent conjunctivitis is treated by **getting the discharge out and keeping it out** — lavage, hygiene, topical antibiotic, no pad, no lens — and by asking one question before you relax: *is the cornea clear?* Book p.94's five negatives (normal VA, normal pupil, no photophobia, clear cornea, normal AC) are what let you call a red eye a conjunctivitis at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Never bandage a discharging eye — book p.91 says “Avoid eye patching”; lavage, topical antibiotics and hygiene are the treatment of mucopurulent conjunctivitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q238), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-239', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'The antigen in **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** could be all **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Intestinal parasites','Tubercula – protein','Staphylococcal blepharitis.','Pollens'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE KEY IS AGAINST THE COURSE MATERIAL, AND THE CONTRADICTION IS IN THE SAME BOOK. The key is C as printed and it is not disputed here — but be aware of what the material says, because an examiner working from it may mark differently.**\n\n**What the book prints.** Book ch.5 (Eyelids), listing the complications of **staphylococcal anterior blepharitis**, includes: \"Madarosis, trichiasis and poliosis · Recurrent styes · **Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens**.\" That is the book stating, in its own complication list, that staphylococcal blepharitis produces phlyctens — which makes option C a genuine antigen source, not the exception. The option the material would exclude is **D, pollens**: pollen is the type-I allergen of seasonal and vernal disease (book p.87, \"The allergen (usually **pollen**) varies with the geographic location\"), and it has no part in phlyctenulosis, which is a delayed hypersensitivity to a **microbial protein**.\n\n**Recorded, not corrected.** Per this project's standing rule the `answer` is always the bank's printed key, and the key here is C. The discrepancy is written down and quoted; it is not taken to the user as a dispute.\n\n**What phlyctenulosis actually is.** A **type IV (delayed) hypersensitivity nodule** on the conjunctiva or at the limbus, provoked by a microbial antigen rather than by the organism itself being present in the eye. The classical antigens are **tuberculoprotein** (the bank's \"Tubercula – protein\"), **staphylococcal** antigen from chronic blepharitis, and, in endemic areas, **intestinal parasites** — three of this question's four options. *(The type-IV mechanism and this antigen list are **not taken from the course material**: book ch.6 records phlyctenular conjunctivitis among the entities it does not cover, and its not-covered list also records that the chapter prints \"**No immunology at all**\". `L6) Conjunctiva` titles the disease but prints no cause, no mechanism and no treatment — *tuberc* returns zero across all 66 slides. The bank asks about the antigen anyway, which is why the list is given.)*\n\n**What the material DOES teach — the appearance, and it teaches it well.** `L6) Conjunctiva` gives phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis **four consecutive slides**, 46–49, and slide 49 is \"the lecturer's OWN HAND-DRAWN diagram\", the only hand-drawn figure in the deck, captioned in his handwriting \"Phlyctenular K.C.\". The photographs show \"small, round, DISCRETE, PINKISH-WHITE RAISED NODULES… each nodule has a **LEASH OF DILATED, BRIGHT RED CONJUNCTIVAL VESSELS** running to it and converging on it, while the sclera between and beyond the nodules is white\", and slide 46 records the point that `opqb-t6-245` keys: \"The injection is **localised to the sectors carrying the nodules** — it is not the diffuse redness of the earlier conjunctivitis pictures.\" The hand drawing adds what the photographs cannot: it contrasts a nodule out on the bulbar conjunctiva, a nodule sitting **on** the limbus, and elongated green-marked (i.e. stained, ulcerated) lesions **creeping over the limbus onto the cornea**, each led by its own vessel leash — which is the *kerato*- half of the name and the reason the disease scars.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A phlycten is a signpost, not a diagnosis. Find and treat what is provoking it — the lid margins first, since staphylococcal blepharitis is the commonest source and its treatment (book ch.5: lid hygiene, warm compresses, lid scrubs, topical fusidic acid or azithromycin, systemic tetracyclines) is straightforward. A recurrent phlycten in an endemic setting is a reason to think about tuberculosis and about worms.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phlyctenulosis is a type IV hypersensitivity nodule to microbial protein — tuberculoprotein, staphylococcal antigen, intestinal parasites; pollens are type I allergens and play no part, yet this bank keys staphylococcal blepharitis as the exception, against its own ch.5 complication list.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q239), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-240', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Purulent conjunctivitis** has the following characters **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Discharge of pus','Acute suppurative inflammation of conjunctiva','No tendency for corneal involvement','Fever and malaise'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Purulent conjunctivitis is the one conjunctivitis that blinds people quickly, and it does so through the cornea — so \"no tendency for corneal involvement\" is exactly backwards.**\n\n**The book says it twice.** Book p.91's signs list for acute bacterial conjunctivitis ends with \"**Corneal ulceration** in **gonococcal** and **meningococcal** infections\", and its p.85 discharge table carries the N.B. that \"Severe **purulent** discharge is suggestive of **gonococcal** conjunctivitis\" — so *purulent* in this material means *gonococcal until proved otherwise*, and gonococcal means the cornea is at risk. Book ch.16 (Paediatric), teaching ophthalmia neonatorum, prints the endpoint in its complications box: \"Corneal opacification, ulceration, **perforation**, endophthalmitis, **loss of eye, and blindness**.\"\n\n**`L6) Conjunctiva` teaches the whole sequence in four wordless photographs.** Slide 18, titled \"Bacterial Conjunctivitis (**Purulent**)\", is a **newborn** with \"both lids grossly swollen, tense and shiny\" and thick yellow-cream pus escaping between them — the deck's own note says \"the age of the patient is the whole point and it is carried only by the photograph\". Slide 19 is a gloved examiner parting those lids with \"copious yellow-cream pus welling out\". Slide 20 re-shows the fornix full of pus. Slide 21 is the end: a child's eye held open by a wire speculum showing \"a large, dense, white-grey opaque area occupying most of the cornea with a raised, irregular central bulge and surrounding scarring — **the end-result of the untreated purulent infection of the preceding slides**\". That is what \"tendency for corneal involvement\" looks like.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Why it stands |\n|---|---|\n| **Discharge of pus** | The definition. Book p.85's fourth discharge column is headed *Purulent* and is filled by acute bacterial infection. |\n| **Acute suppurative inflammation of conjunctiva** | Also the definition, in pathological terms — an acute neutrophil-rich inflammation producing pus. |\n| **Fever and malaise** | Book p.91: \"Associated **systemic symptoms** of the causative organism\", and its lymphadenopathy line names \"**Pre-auricular and submandibular lymphadenopathy** in gonococcal and meningococcal infections\". Meningococcal conjunctivitis in particular is a systemic illness. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A hyperacute copious purulent discharge is an **emergency**, and the two actions are urgent: book p.91's investigations are \"**Conjunctival swabs for urgent gram staining** to exclude gonococcal and meningococcal infections\" plus culture, and its treatment adds \"**Systemic** antibiotics… in gonococcal and meningococcal and H influenzae infections\" on top of topical. In the newborn, book ch.16 names the drugs: **ceftriaxone** systemically plus topical antibiotic for gonococcal disease, oral **erythromycin** for chlamydial.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Purulent (gonococcal) conjunctivitis has a strong tendency to corneal ulceration and perforation — it is the conjunctivitis that blinds, and it needs urgent Gram stain and systemic antibiotics.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q240), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-241', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Papillae** of spring catarrh have the following characteristics **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Large papillae','Always involving the fornix','Flat-topped','Ropy discharge'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Papillae cannot form in the fornix, and the reason is anatomical rather than clinical.** Book p.83: \"The **palpebral conjunctiva** is **firmly adherent to the tarsal plates** in the lids\", whereas the conjunctiva reaching the fornix is loose and redundant. A papilla is a fold of hyperplastic epithelium raised over a fibrovascular core and tethered at its base by septa running down to that adherent layer — so papillae are confined to the tarsal plate, where the conjunctiva is bound down, and stop where the tarsus stops. Follicles, which are lymphoid aggregates in the loose subepithelial adenoid layer, do the opposite: they are most numerous **towards the fornix**, which `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 13 records in its photograph (\"most numerous towards the fornix end of the plate\").\n\n**So follicle-versus-papilla is answered by position as well as by structure:**\n\n| | **Papilla** | **Follicle** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | **Fibrovascular** — a central vessel; `L6` slide 14: \"Each elevation is **red at its centre** — a central vessel core\" | **Lymphocytic**; book p.86: \"leucocytic infiltration (mainly **lymphocytes**)\" |\n| Vessels | Run **up into** the centre | Run **over and around**, sparing the top |\n| Top | **Flat**, pale-clefted | **Rounded**, pale, domed |\n| Where | **Tarsal plate only** — never the fornix | Maximal **towards the fornix** |\n| Size for \"giant\" | **>1 mm** (book p.86) | — |\n\nThis bank asks the same point from the other side at `opqb-t6-263`, where \"papillae in the lower fornix\" is keyed as the sign *not* found in vernal disease.\n\n**Why the other three are true of spring catarrh:**\n\n| Option | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Large papillae** | Book p.86 defines giant papillae as \">1 mm in diameter\" and lists \"Allergic conjunctivitis especially in **vernal catarrh**\" first among their causes; book p.88's conjunctival-signs row prints \"Tarsal **giant papillae**\". |\n| **Flat-topped** | Book p.86: \"Giant papillae (**Cobble stone appearance** i.e., they are **flat-topped**)\". `L6` slide 38 photographs them \"LARGE, FLAT-TOPPED, POLYGONAL… packed edge to edge in a cobblestone mosaic\". |\n| **Ropy discharge** | Book p.88's symptom list: \"**Mucous 'ropy'** discharge\", with a figure captioned \"ropy discharge\" showing a strand being drawn out of the lower fornix. Strictly this is a characteristic of the **disease** rather than of the papillae, which is a looseness in the question — but it is true of spring catarrh and so is not the exception. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** When you evert a lid, note **where** the bumps stop. Bumps that end at the edge of the tarsal plate are papillae and point to allergy, chronic irritation or bacterial disease; bumps that carry on into the fornix are follicles and point to viral or chlamydial disease. The causes of each are printed once, as this bank's own box, at `opqb-t6-268`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Papillae are confined to the tarsal plate because the palpebral conjunctiva is bound down there — they never involve the fornix; follicles, being lymphoid and subepithelial, are maximal towards the fornix.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.34 (Q241), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-242', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Complications of trachoma** include:',
    options:['Corneal descemetocele','Subconjunctival hemorrhage','Enlarged preauricular lymph nodes','Cicatricial ectropion'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE KEYED OPTION MISNAMES THE COMPLICATION, AND THIS BANK'S OWN BOX CONTRADICTS IT. The key is D as printed and it is not disputed — but the lid deformity of trachoma is cicatricial ENTROPION, not ectropion.**\n\n**What the sources print.** Book p.93, listing cicatricial trachoma: \"Trichiasis, corneal vascularization and **cicatricial entropion**\". This bank's own printed box at `opqb-t6-230` reprints that line word for word as its item 4 — *entropion*. And `opqb-t6-246`, four questions later on the facing page, offers \"**Cicatricial entropion.**\" correctly spelt as one of its own options. So the bank prints the word correctly twice and incorrectly once.\n\n**Why it must be entropion.** The scarring of trachoma is on the **conjunctival (posterior) surface of the tarsal plate** — book p.92: \"Scarring is most prominent on the **upper tarsal plate**\". A scar contracts. A contracting scar on the *back* of the lid shortens the posterior lamella and rolls the margin **inwards**, towards the globe. That in-turning drags the lashes onto the cornea, which is **trichiasis**, and lashes on the cornea abrade it, which is why the WHO grade **TT** (\"at least one lash touching the globe\") sits immediately before **CO** (corneal opacity) in the five-grade sequence, and why **S**urgery for trichiasis is the S of the **SAFE** strategy. An *ectropion* — the margin rolling **outwards** — is caused by anterior-lamella shortening, by age-related laxity or by a facial palsy, and it would move the lashes *away* from the cornea, which would end the sequence rather than drive it.\n\n**On the printed key.** Since none of the other three options is a complication of trachoma at all, D is the only answer the question admits, and it is clearly meant to be the lid-scarring complication; the error is the direction word. Answered D. *(The anterior-versus-posterior lamella mechanism above is **not taken from the course material** — neither book ch.6 nor `L6) Conjunctiva` explains why the lid turns in, and *entropion* returns zero across all 66 slides of `L6`. It is standard, uncontested lid anatomy.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal descemetocele** | A bulge of bare Descemet's membrane through a cornea that has melted almost through — the last step before perforation. It belongs to **suppurative keratitis** and to severe corneal melts. Trachoma's corneal damage is **pannus** and then **opacification** (book p.92–93), a scarring process rather than a perforating one. |\n| **Subconjunctival hemorrhage** | Idiopathic, or from trauma, Valsalva, bleeding disorders, hypertension and anticoagulation (book p.95). Book p.85 does list conjunctival haemorrhages in \"some types of **viral** conjunctivitis and severe **bacterial** conjunctivitis\" — chlamydial disease is not among them. See `opqb-t6-248`. |\n| **Enlarged preauricular lymph nodes** | A real finding in **adult chlamydial (inclusion) conjunctivitis** — book p.91 lists \"**Tender preauricular lymph nodes**\" among its signs — and in viral and gonococcal disease. But it is a feature of **active infection**, not a *complication*, and book p.92's active-trachoma description does not include it. The strongest distractor. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The complications of trachoma are the cicatricial ones, and they are mechanical: scar → in-turned lid → lashes on cornea → corneal opacity → blindness. That chain is why book p.92 calls trachoma \"the world's **leading cause of preventable irreversible blindness**\" and why the surgery in SAFE is lid surgery, not eye surgery.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Lid — the keyed complication is a lid deformity, but the question is testing the sequelae of a conjunctival infection.)*",
    objective:'Trachoma scars the posterior tarsal surface and turns the lid IN — cicatricial ENTROPION with trichiasis; this bank’s option prints “ectropion”, against book p.93 and against its own printed box.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q242), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-243', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Staphylococcal** infections in ophthalmology do **NOT** include:',
    options:['Blepharitis','Corneal ulcer','Giant papillary conjunctivitis','Endophthalmitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Giant papillary conjunctivitis is not an infection at all. It is a mechanical and immunological response to something chronically rubbing the tarsal conjunctiva, and no organism is involved.**\n\n**Book p.86 gives the three causes and none of them is a bacterium:**\n\n1. **Allergic conjunctivitis especially in vernal catarrh**\n2. **Prolonged contact lens wear**\n3. **Foreign body (as in artificial eye users)**\n\npreceded by the mechanism — giant papillae \"develop with **prolonged inflammation and irritation**\". This bank reprints that same list as its own box at `opqb-t6-268` and keys the contact-lens form twice — `opqb-t6-234`, which the bank prints on both p.34 and p.38.\n\n**Watch the wording of the same page, though**, because it is the reason this question is not trivial: book p.86's *ordinary* papillae sentence says \"They develop with **bacterial conjunctivitis** and **chronic blepharitis**\", and book p.94's grid puts *Papillae* in the bacterial column. So bacteria do cause **papillae**. What they do not cause is **giant** papillae — the >1 mm cobblestone form, which needs prolonged mechanical irritation. The word *giant* is the whole question.\n\n**Why the other three genuinely are staphylococcal:**\n\n| Option | Where the material puts *Staphylococcus* |\n|---|---|\n| **Blepharitis** | Book ch.5 teaches **staphylococcal anterior blepharitis** as an entity, with madarosis, trichiasis, poliosis, recurrent styes and \"Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens\" as its complications, treated by lid hygiene, topical fusidic acid or azithromycin and systemic tetracyclines. Book p.89 also links **Staph. blepharitis** to atopic keratoconjunctivitis. |\n| **Corneal ulcer** | Book p.91 names **S. aureus** among the four commonest organisms of acute bacterial conjunctivitis, and *Staphylococcus* is one of the standard causes of bacterial keratitis. |\n| **Endophthalmitis** | Intraocular infection after surgery or penetrating trauma; coagulase-negative staphylococci and *S. aureus* are its commonest organisms. |\n\n*(The specific attribution of bacterial keratitis and of post-operative endophthalmitis to staphylococci is **not taken from the course material** — book ch.6 names *S. aureus* only in its conjunctivitis organism list, and `L6) Conjunctiva` names **no organism anywhere** in its 66 slides. Both attributions are standard, uncontested microbiology, given because the bank offers them as options.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** \"Giant papillary conjunctivitis\" in a lens wearer is not treated with an antibiotic. It is treated by **removing the stimulus** — stop or change the lens, replace the prosthesis, remove the exposed suture — with a mast cell stabilizer or antihistamine for symptoms. Reaching for a topical antibiotic here treats nobody and delays the one intervention that works.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Giant papillary conjunctivitis is a mechanical/allergic response to prolonged irritation — contact lenses, prostheses, vernal disease — and is not an infection; ordinary papillae, by contrast, DO occur with bacterial conjunctivitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q243), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-244', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A **clear zone between the lesion and the limbus** is **NOT** present in:',
    options:['Arcus senilis','Catarrhal corneal ulcer','Trachomatous pannus',"Fleischer's ring"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A pannus is not near the limbus, it comes OUT of the limbus — so there is nothing to leave a clear zone.** The other three lesions all sit a short distance inside the corneal margin with unaffected cornea between them and the limbus; that gap is the *lucid interval*, and its presence or absence is the single sign this question tests.\n\n**Why a pannus has no gap.** Book p.92 defines it inside the active-trachoma row: \"**pannus** formation (**superficial corneal vascularization** and cellular infiltration)\" following superior epithelial keratitis. Vessels that vascularise a cornea have to come from somewhere, and the only vessels available are the limbal ones — so a pannus grows continuously across the limbus into the cornea. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 28, titled \"Trachomatous Pannus\", photographs exactly that continuity: \"A sheet of fine, straight, **RADIAL** blood vessels is seen **crossing the limbus** from the **SUPERIOR** conjunctiva and invading the upper cornea, with a hazy grey infiltration of the cornea at their advancing edge… **the fact that the vessels come from ABOVE is carried by the picture alone**.\" Slide 29, \"Trachomatous Nebula ex Pannus\", is the sequel — a band of grey veil-like haze across the upper cornea after the vessels have regressed.\n\n**Why the other three do have a clear zone:**\n\n| Option | What it is | The gap |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Arcus senilis** | A grey-white lipid ring in the peripheral corneal stroma | Separated from the limbus by a narrow band of clear cornea — the classical **lucid interval of Vogt**. `L6` slide 54 photographs \"a broad white peripheral ring (arcus)\" on an elderly eye. |\n| **Catarrhal (marginal) corneal ulcer** | A hypersensitivity infiltrate to staphylococcal antigen, in the peripheral cornea, characteristically under a lid margin | Classically separated from the limbus by a clear zone — the immune complexes deposit just inside the arcade, not at it. |\n| **Fleischer's ring** | An iron line encircling the base of a **keratoconus** cone | Not a peripheral lesion at all: it lies around the cone in the **central or paracentral** cornea, so the whole peripheral cornea between it and the limbus is clear. The weakest distractor once its position is remembered. |\n\n**⚠️ How much of this is in the material.** *Pannus* is in both sources and is well taught. The other three are not: **arcus senilis** appears in this module only as an incidental describer's word on `L6` slide 54; **catarrhal/marginal ulcer** is named nowhere in book ch.6 (which records marginal keratitis appearing only in a ch.5 figure caption); **Fleischer's ring** returns zero across the cached ophthalmology sources. *That arcus and a marginal ulcer are separated from the limbus by a lucid interval, and that Fleischer's ring encircles a keratoconus cone, are **not taken from the course material** — they are standard, uncontested corneal signs, given because the bank offers all four as options.*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The lucid interval is a **peripheral-cornea triage sign**. A peripheral opacity with clear cornea between it and the limbus is degenerative or immune and usually needs no urgent action; a lesion continuous with the limbus is **vascular or infiltrative** and means the limbal vessels are actively involved — trachomatous pannus above, or a limbal stem cell problem such as the one book p.88 warns about in vernal disease.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Cornea — three of the four lesions are corneal, but the question is asked here because the lesion it keys is trachomatous.)*",
    objective:'A trachomatous pannus grows continuously across the limbus from the superior conjunctival vessels, so it has no lucid interval; arcus senilis, a marginal ulcer and Fleischer’s ring are all separated from the limbus by clear cornea.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q244), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-245', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Causes of **localized** conjunctival redness include:',
    options:['Acute viral conjunctivitis','Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','Hay fever'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are diffuse diseases of the whole conjunctival sac. Only the phlycten produces a sector of redness with white conjunctiva on either side of it.**\n\n**The material states it, and states it as an observation rather than a rule.** `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 46, titled \"Phlyctenular KeratoConjunctivitis\", photographs two nodules on white sclera and records: \"each nodule has a **LEASH OF DILATED, BRIGHT RED CONJUNCTIVAL VESSELS** running to it and converging on it, while the **sclera between and beyond the nodules is white**… The injection is **localised to the sectors carrying the nodules — it is not the diffuse redness of the earlier conjunctivitis pictures**.\" Slide 47 shows \"a WEDGE of dilated pink vessels\" running from the canthal side toward a single nodule \"and stops at it\". The vessels are recruited to feed one lesion, so they light up one sector.\n\n**Why the other three are diffuse:**\n\n| Option | Its redness |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute viral conjunctivitis** | Diffuse bilateral injection with watering, lower tarsal follicles and a tender preauricular node (book p.90). `L6` slides 9–10 photograph the pattern: \"BRIGHT RED, made of individually visible, tortuous, superficial conjunctival vessels… heaviest **PERIPHERALLY** and in the fornices, **FADING** as it approaches the limbus\". |\n| **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | Diffuse injection of bulbar conjunctiva **and** lid margins with mucopurulent crusting — `L6` slide 17. Book p.85 calls conjunctival injection \"*sparing the limbal area*\", which is the pattern, not a sector. |\n| **Hay fever** | Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis: book p.87 gives \"**Diffuse redness**\" in the tutorial box's clinical-picture list, plus itching, painless tearing and mild papillary reaction, **bilateral** by definition. |\n\n**⚠️ Compare `opqb-t6-232`, which is the same stem with three adjectives added.** There the redness is *painful, tender and non-itchy*, and the key is **episcleritis**, not phlycten. The two questions are a deliberate pair, and the difference between them is tenderness:\n\n| | Localised? | Tender to touch? | Nodule? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Phlycten** | Yes | Not characteristically | Yes, with a vessel **leash** |\n| **Episcleritis** | Yes | **Yes** — book ch.7, \"Tender episcleral nodules that can be easily mobilized over the sclera\" | Nodular form only |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Sectoral redness is a short differential and a physical one: look for the **lesion at the apex of the vessels**. If a bundle of vessels converges on something, that something is the diagnosis — a phlycten, an episcleral nodule, a foreign body, an inflamed pterygium. Diffuse redness with no focus is a conjunctivitis, and then the discharge decides which one (`opqb-t6-229`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A phlycten produces localised, sectoral redness — a leash of vessels converging on one nodule with white conjunctiva either side; viral, mucopurulent and hay-fever conjunctivitis are all diffuse.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q245), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-246', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Signs of activity** of trachoma include:',
    options:["Herbert's pits of the upper limbus.",'Scarring of the palpebral conjunctiva.','Follicles in the upper palpebral conjunctiva.','Cicatricial entropion.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Book p.92 defines active trachoma in one clause and this option repeats it: \"Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis associated with mucopurulent discharge.\"** The other three options are all end-stage scars, and a scar is not an activity.\n\n**The active picture, complete:**\n\n- **Upper tarsal follicles** — the sign here, and the sign the WHO grades as **TF** (\"**5 or more** follicles, **more than 0.5 mm** in diameter, on the superior tarsal plate\", book p.93)\n- **Mucopurulent discharge**\n- A **papillary reaction**, which \"can also be present\" — and when it is dense enough to obscure \"**50 % or more** of the deep tarsal vessels\" the grade is **TI**\n- **Cornea:** superior epithelial keratitis and **pannus** — superficial vascularization and cellular infiltration advancing from the **upper** limbus (`L6) Conjunctiva` slide 28)\n- It is \"most common in **preschool children**\", against cicatricial disease being \"prevalent in **middle age**\"\n\n**Why the other three are cicatricial:**\n\n| Option | Book p.92–93 |\n|---|---|\n| **Herbert's pits of the upper limbus** | \"Superior limbal follicles may **resolve** to leave a row of shallow **depressions**\" — the word *resolve* is the whole point. The active version is the follicle (Herbert's rosette, `opqb-t6-230`); the pit is what is left when it has gone. |\n| **Scarring of the palpebral conjunctiva** | \"Linear or stellate conjunctival scars\" in mild cases, broad confluent **Arlt's line** in severe disease, worst on the upper tarsal plate; WHO grade **TS**. |\n| **Cicatricial entropion** | Listed under cicatricial trachoma with trichiasis and corneal vascularization; WHO grade **TT** covers the lash touching the globe. ⚠️ Note that `opqb-t6-242`, on this same page, prints the same complication as \"cicatricial **ectropion**\" and keys it. |\n\n**⚠️ Why the distinction has consequences.** Active means **infectious** (`opqb-t6-233`) and active means **antibiotics** — book p.93: a single dose of **azithromycin 20 mg/kg up to 1 g** for the patient and \"**all family members**\", with communities needing **annual** treatment. Cicatricial means **surgery**: the **S** of SAFE is \"Surgery for trichiasis and complications\", and no antibiotic will unroll a scarred lid. Grading a patient TF/TI versus TS/TT/CO is therefore a treatment decision, not a bookkeeping exercise.\n\n**⚠️ None of this is in the lecture deck.** `L6) Conjunctiva` gives trachoma nine slides (25–33) and shows the whole natural history in photographs — follicles, pannus, nebula, PTCs, early reticular scarring, dense confluent scar — while naming **no grade, no stage, no organism and no drug**: *WHO*, *TF*, *TI*, *TS*, *TT*, *CO*, *MacCallan*, *grading* and *stage* all return zero across its 66 slides. The active/cicatricial division and the WHO codes are the book's alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Active trachoma = upper tarsal follicles + mucopurulent discharge (± papillae, pannus); Herbert’s pits, conjunctival scarring and cicatricial entropion are all cicatricial, and the difference decides antibiotics versus surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q246), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-247', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A **tarsorrhaphy** is **contraindicated** in:',
    options:['Neuroparalytic keratitis.','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis.','Paralytic ectropion.','Malignant exophthalmos'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A tarsorrhaphy sews the lids together. Do that over a conjunctival sac that is producing mucopus and you have built a closed abscess against the cornea.** Book p.91 gives the principle in four words at the head of the treatment of acute bacterial conjunctivitis — \"**Avoid eye patching**\" — and a tarsorrhaphy is a patch that cannot be taken off.\n\nThis is the third printing of the same rule in one topic, which is how you know it is examinable:\n\n| Question | The lid closure proposed | Key |\n|---|---|---|\n| `opqb-t6-227` | Patching | contraindicated in **mucopurulent conjunctivitis** |\n| `opqb-t6-238` | \"Eye bandages… to avoid photophobia\" | the false statement in the EXCEPT list |\n| this one | Tarsorrhaphy | contraindicated in **mucopurulent conjunctivitis** |\n\nThe objection is drainage, and it is the same objection each time. Retained pus macerates the epithelium, and this bank keys the consequence at `opqb-t6-260`: the commonest serious complication of mucopurulent conjunctivitis is a **corneal ulcer**.\n\n**Why a tarsorrhaphy is the right operation in the other three.** All three are diseases of **corneal exposure**, and closing the lids is precisely the treatment:\n\n| Option | Why the cornea is at risk | Why closure helps |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Neuroparalytic keratitis** | A **trigeminal (V1)** lesion: the cornea is anaesthetic, so it neither feels an injury nor blinks reflexly, and the epithelium breaks down. | Covering the surface substitutes mechanically for the blink the nerve can no longer produce. |\n| **Paralytic ectropion** | A **facial (VII)** palsy: orbicularis cannot close the lids, the lower lid falls away and the eye is left open — **lagophthalmos** with exposure keratitis. | Narrowing the palpebral aperture restores cover. A lateral tarsorrhaphy is the classic temporising measure. |\n| **Malignant exophthalmos** | Severe thyroid eye disease: the globe is pushed so far forward that the lids cannot meet over it, and the cornea dries, ulcerates and can perforate. | Closure protects the cornea while the orbital disease is treated or decompressed. |\n\n*(Neuroparalytic keratitis, paralytic ectropion and malignant exophthalmos are all outside book ch.6 and outside `L6) Conjunctiva`, and *tarsorrhaphy* itself appears in the cached ophthalmology sources only in book ch.4, book ch.5 and the cornea deck — never in the conjunctiva sources. The three indications above are therefore **not taken from the conjunctiva material**; they are standard, uncontested practice, given because the bank offers them as options.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The rule generalises cleanly. **Close the lids to protect a cornea that is drying; never close them over a cornea that is bathed in pus.** Ask which problem you are solving before reaching for the sutures.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Lid — tarsorrhaphy is a lid operation and three of the four options are lid or orbit diseases, but the contraindication being keyed is a conjunctival one.)*",
    objective:'Tarsorrhaphy protects a drying, exposed cornea — neuroparalytic keratitis, paralytic ectropion, malignant exophthalmos — but is contraindicated over a discharging eye, because it converts the conjunctival sac into a closed abscess.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q247), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-248', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A **subconjunctival hemorrhage**:',
    options:['May cause blood to pass into the cornea.','It is usually associated with reduced vision.','It is always associated with some form of eye discomfort.','Usually disappears in a couple of weeks.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Book p.95 gives the natural history as a number: a subconjunctival haemorrhage \"usually resolves spontaneously in **2-3 weeks**\".** \"A couple of weeks\" is that figure, and the other three options are each contradicted by the book's own definition on the same page.\n\n**The definition is a list of negatives**, and it is worth learning as one — book p.95: \"Presence of **blood under the conjunctiva**, otherwise **asymptomatic**\", and \"It is **not associated with any significant pain**, **does not affect vision** and **no discharge**.\" *(That second sentence is tutorial-marked in the book, form (c). The definition line above it is not.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **May cause blood to pass into the cornea** | Anatomically impossible from this space. Book p.83: the bulbar conjunctiva is loosely attached to the sclera and Tenon's capsule \"**except at the limbus (corneoscleral junction) where it is firmly attached**\". That firm limbal attachment is a dam — subconjunctival blood spreads freely in the loose plane and stops dead at the limbus. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 65 photographs the result four times: a \"flat, sharply demarcated, HOMOGENEOUS BRIGHT RED sheet of blood… running right up to the **LIMBUS**\" with a \"**knife-sharp border**\". |\n| **Usually associated with reduced vision** | The book says the opposite: \"does not affect vision\". The cornea, the media and the retina are untouched; the blood is on the outside of the eye. |\n| **Always associated with some form of eye discomfort** | \"Otherwise **asymptomatic**\", and \"not associated with any significant pain\". The word *always* would be fatal even without the quotation — most patients discover it in a mirror or are told about it by someone else. |\n\n**Causes, since the bank expects them elsewhere.** Book p.95: **idiopathic**, or associated with **trauma**, **Valsalva manoeuvre** (coughing, sneezing, straining — the examples are tutorial-marked, the manoeuvre itself is core), **bleeding disorders**, **hypertension**, and **anticoagulation therapy**. ⚠️ `L6` slide 65 shows the four photographs, including a traumatic one with periorbital bruising, and gives **no cause at all** — *Valsalva*, *hypertens*, *anticoagul* and *leuk* all return zero across its 66 slides.\n\n**Management, and the one thing that is not reassurance.** Book p.95: assurance if there are no other ocular findings, **blood pressure measurement** and systemic workup to find a cause, and — the sentence that matters — \"A **large area** of subconjunctival haemorrhage with a history of **trauma** should arouse high suspicion for **eye globe rupture**\", especially where the posterior extent is obscured by the blood. That is precisely the situation this bank builds at `opqb-t6-283`. Its tutorial side box adds: artificial tears for discomfort, check the **INR** if the patient is anticoagulated, and investigate a **recurrent** haemorrhage for a bleeding disorder.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A flat, sharply bounded, painless red sheet on a white eye with normal vision needs a blood pressure cuff and a calendar, not a prescription. What changes that is **trauma**, **recurrence**, or **any abnormality of vision, pupil or cornea** — and then it is not a simple subconjunctival haemorrhage any more.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A subconjunctival haemorrhage is painless, does not affect vision, cannot cross the firm limbal attachment into the cornea, and resolves spontaneously in 2–3 weeks.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q248), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-249', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A 30-year-old male presented with a **triangular encroachment of the conjunctiva on the cornea** with small opacities on the cornea **anterior to the apex** of the lesion. He observed it one year ago and states that it is **progressively enlarging**; no previous surgery has been done.',
    options:['Visual deterioration may be due to induced myopia.','Diplopia with limitation of eye movement may occur.','It is better not to operate.','Can be treated by excision with conjunctival auto graft.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**This is a progressive pterygium in a young man, and the operation that suits it is excision with a conjunctival autograft.** This is the topic's anchor entry for pterygium; `opqb-t6-264`, `opqb-t6-267`, `opqb-t6-281` and `opqb-t6-284` point back here.\n\n**What a pterygium is.** Book p.95: \"**Fibrovascular, triangular, wing-like encroachment** of epithelial tissue **onto the cornea**\", which \"may **decrease vision** if it induces **astigmatism** or covers the **pupillary area**\". The chapter prints an Arabic marginal gloss beside the heading — تيجي على الكورنيا, \"it comes onto the cornea\" — as the mnemonic separating it from pinguecula, which spares the cornea. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 50 photographs the fleshy form: \"a triangular, **FLESHY, VASCULAR FOLD** of conjunctiva with a wide base at the canthus and an **APEX that has crossed the limbus**… Straight, prominent RED VESSELS run within its body in a fan, all pointing toward the apex.\"\n\n**Book p.95's management, complete:**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Indications for surgery** | chronic inflammation · **threat to the visual axis** · **induced astigmatism** · cosmesis |\n| **Operation** | \"**Simple excision with bare sclera**\" |\n| **Recurrence** | \"**One-third recur** after excision\" |\n| **Lower recurrence with** | conjunctival **autograft** — \"**only 5 % recurrence rate**\" — or **mitomycin-C** after simple excision |\n| **Symptomatic** | lubricating drops for irritation |\n\nThe autograft's harvest site is taught only in a figure: the book's uncaptioned diagram labels a \"**Harvested area**\" at the **superior bulbar conjunctiva** and a \"Grafted area\" at the bare nasal site. `L6` slide 57 is the operative photograph — \"a pink-red flap of conjunctival tissue has been laid into the defect and secured by a row of fine BLACK INTERRUPTED SUTURES\" — and, like everything else in that deck, it is unlabelled.\n\n**Why the stem earns an operation.** *Progressively enlarging* over a documented year, in a **30-year-old** who will live with it for decades, with **corneal opacities anterior to the apex** showing that the cornea is already being affected. Take the fold off and you have a one-third chance of it coming straight back; take it off and cover the bare sclera with the patient's own conjunctiva and that falls to about one in twenty.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Visual deterioration may be due to induced myopia** | Book p.95 says vision falls when the pterygium \"induces **astigmatism**\" or covers the pupil — and the astigmatism is characteristically **with-the-rule flattening of the horizontal meridian** as the fold tugs the nasal cornea flat, not a spherical myopic shift. `opqb-t6-281` keys a pterygium corrected by a **cylinder**, which is the same fact from the other side. |\n| **Diplopia with limitation of eye movement may occur** | Not a feature of a primary pterygium, which is a superficial fold of conjunctiva. Restriction and diplopia are a complication of **recurrent** pterygium with symblepharon formation or of aggressive adjunctive therapy, not of a first, untreated lesion. |\n| **It is better not to operate** | Reasonable for a **static** pterygium away from the axis — and it is what `opqb-t6-281` keys — but this one is documented as progressive and is already producing corneal opacity, which is book p.95's own indication. |\n\n*(The mechanism of pterygium-induced astigmatism, and the association of restricted motility with recurrent rather than primary disease, are **not taken from the course material**: book p.95 names \"induced astigmatism\" without explaining it, and `L6` gives pterygium eight slides with **no cause, no indication and no recurrence figure** — *UV*, *dust*, *treatment*, *excision*, *graft* and *recurrence rate* all return zero across its 66 slides.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A progressive pterygium threatening the cornea is excised, and excision with a conjunctival autograft cuts recurrence from one-third to about 5 %.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q249), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-250', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A 5-year-old male child presented with an **acute onset of redness, mucopurulent discharge and conjunctival hyperemia** associated with **mild lid edema**.',
    options:["Koch Week's bacilli is the causative organism of such cases in winter.",'Associated central corneal ulcers should be excluded.','Culture & sensitivity testing are done routinely.','Petechial hemorrhages are more common in the pneumococcal type.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**This is acute mucopurulent bacterial conjunctivitis in a child, and the one true statement offered is the association of subconjunctival petechial haemorrhages with the pneumococcal type.**\n\n**The material supports the association without naming it.** Book p.85's clinical-features list, item 4, is \"**Conjunctival haemorrhages** (In some types of viral conjunctivitis and **severe bacterial conjunctivitis**)\", and book p.91 names **S. pneumoniae** as one of the four commonest organisms of acute bacterial conjunctivitis (with *H. influenzae*, *S. aureus* and *Moraxella catarrhalis*). *That the petechial form is characteristically pneumococcal is **not taken from the course material** — neither source assigns haemorrhages to a named organism, and `L6) Conjunctiva` names no organism at all in its 66 slides. It is standard, uncontested teaching, and it is what the bank keys.*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Koch–Weeks in winter** | Two faults in one line. *Koch–Weeks bacillus* is **Haemophilus aegyptius**, and it causes **epidemic** mucopurulent conjunctivitis in **hot, dry weather** — the summer epidemics of warm climates — not in winter. The bank itself calls the same organism \"Hemophilus Aegyptus\" at `opqb-t6-238`. ⚠️ *aegypt* returns **zero hits** across all 36 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 cached book chapters; the closest source statement is book p.91's \"H. influenzae\". **Not taken from the course material.** |\n| **Associated central corneal ulcers should be excluded** | The wrong adjective, and it matters. Book p.91 puts corneal ulceration under **gonococcal and meningococcal** infection, and the ulcers to exclude in ordinary bacterial conjunctivitis are the **peripheral / marginal** ones — the catarrhal ulcers of `opqb-t6-244`, which are hypersensitivity infiltrates near the limbus. A **central** ulcer in a child with mucopurulent discharge is not part of this picture; it is a separate, sight-threatening diagnosis. Still, this is the strongest distractor, because excluding corneal involvement in any red eye is right — book p.94's five negatives include \"**Clear cornea**\". |\n| **Culture & sensitivity testing are done routinely** | Not routine. Book p.91's investigations are \"**Conjunctival swabs for urgent gram staining** to **exclude gonococcal and meningococcal** infections\" and \"**Culture (if possible)**\" — the Gram stain is urgent and targeted, and the culture is qualified. Most acute bacterial conjunctivitis is treated empirically with a topical antibiotic; the swab is for the hyperacute, purulent or neonatal case. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a child with a sticky red eye the diagnosis is usually made at the door, and the questions that change management are: **is the discharge frankly purulent** (think gonococcal, swab urgently, add systemic antibiotics), **is the cornea clear**, and **is the vision normal**? If the answer to the last two is yes, this is book p.91's four-line treatment — no patch, no contact lenses, strict hygiene, topical antibiotics.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Petechial subconjunctival haemorrhages accompany pneumococcal conjunctivitis; Koch–Weeks (H. aegyptius) epidemics occur in hot weather, corneal ulcers here are marginal not central, and culture is not routine.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.35 (Q250), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-251', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Concerning **adenoviral conjunctivitis**:',
    options:['It is highly contagious.','It is characterized by follicles occurring in the palpebral conjunctiva.','Subconjunctival hemorrhage is a common finding.','Topical antivirals are supportaive.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS MORE THAN ONE TRUE OPTION AS THE BOOK PRINTS IT. The key is C and it is not disputed; what follows is what the sources actually say about each line, because two of them are quoted from the book almost word for word.**\n\n**The keyed option is supported.** Book p.85's clinical-features list, item 4: \"**Conjunctival haemorrhages** (In **some types of viral conjunctivitis** and severe bacterial conjunctivitis)\". `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 35 photographs it inside the viral sequence and the deck's own note records why the slide exists: the picture shows \"several flat, bright-red, sharply-edged patches of **SUBCONJUNCTIVAL HAEMORRHAGE**… and, in the inferior fornix below, a row of pale rounded **FOLLICLES**\", and \"**the haemorrhagic element of the viral picture is taught only by this photograph**\". So haemorrhage-with-follicles is a taught adenoviral appearance, and among these four options it is the one that is *distinctively* adenoviral rather than generic.\n\n**⚠️ But A and B are printed as true by the same book.** Book p.90 on viral conjunctivitis: \"**It is highly contagious** and may appear as epidemics\" — that is option A verbatim — and its signs list gives \"Conjunctival **follicles** (mainly in the lower tarsal conjunctiva)\", which is option B. Both statements are therefore correct, and a candidate who has read the book will find three defensible answers under a stem whose only instruction is an underlined colon. Recorded, not corrected.\n\n**Option D is the one that is genuinely false**, and it is worth saying why. Book p.90's treatment of adenoviral disease is: \"**Spontaneous resolution** of adenovirus infection occurs within **2-3 weeks** with **no specific treatment necessary**\"; strict hygiene to restrict transmission; topical steroids with gradual tapering for severe symptoms; topical antibiotics only if secondary bacterial infection is suspected; topical antihistamines and vasoconstrictors for itch. **No antiviral appears anywhere** — and that is correct, because there is no effective topical antiviral against adenovirus. Aciclovir and its relatives work on herpes simplex, which book p.90 lists as a *separate* presentation (\"Herpes simplex virus follicular conjunctivitis (blepharoconjunctivitis)\").\n\n**Adenovirus, for completeness** — book p.90:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Share of viral conjunctivitis | **90 %** |\n| Transmission | contact with respiratory or ocular secretions |\n| **Pharyngoconjunctival fever** | serovars **3, 4, 7** — keyed at `opqb-t6-226` |\n| **Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis** | serovars **8, 19, 37**; the most severe form, marked keratitis and photophobia — keyed at `opqb-t6-229` (printed twice, p.33 and p.39) and `opqb-t6-273` |\n| Signs | injection · **lower** tarsal follicles · **pseudomembranes** may be present · preauricular lymphadenopathy · lid oedema · keratitis with subepithelial infiltrates |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** What an adenoviral conjunctivitis needs is **containment, not a drug**: separate towels, no sharing of pillows or eye drops, hands washed, and — since it is the commonest external ocular infection and spreads through clinics — the same discipline from the examiner. Time does the rest, in two to three weeks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Adenoviral conjunctivitis is highly contagious, follicular and self-limiting in 2–3 weeks with no effective topical antiviral; subconjunctival haemorrhage is a taught part of its picture — but this question prints three true options and keys only one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.36 (Q251), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-252', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Concerning **trachoma**:',
    options:['Its incidence declined due to use of clean household water.','It is caused by chlamydia species D, B, F.','T1 is characterized by the presence of small raised upper tarsal conjunctival follicles.','Conjunctival scarring is pathogenomic of trachoma.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Trachoma is a disease of water, faces and flies, and the fall in its incidence has tracked water supply and hygiene rather than any drug.** Book p.92: it \"is related to **poverty, overcrowding and poor hygiene**\", and \"Its visual morbidity is the consequence of **re-infection cycles within communities**\". Book p.93's **SAFE** strategy puts two of its four arms on exactly this — **F**acial hygiene and **E**nvironmental improvement, alongside **S**urgery for trichiasis and **A**ntibiotics for active disease. Clean household water is what makes facial hygiene possible, and it interrupts the re-infection cycle that the antibiotics alone cannot (\"communities may need to receive **annual** treatment to suppress infection\").\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and each is wrong in an instructive way:**\n\n| Option | The error | What the book prints |\n|---|---|---|\n| **\"chlamydia species D, B, F\"** | Wrong serovars, and they are the *other* disease's | Book p.92: trachoma is \"serovars **A, B, Ba and C**\" of *Chlamydia trachomatis*. **D → K** is **adult chlamydial (inclusion) conjunctivitis** and **ophthalmia neonatorum** (book p.91's flow figure). ⚠️ The book contradicts itself by a letter: the prose gives \"A, B, **Ba** and C\" and the figure gives \"A, B, & C\" without Ba. Both are recorded and neither is corrected. |\n| **\"T1\" for the follicular grade** | Right finding, wrong code | The WHO code is **TF** — *trachomatous inflammation, follicular* — defined as \"**5 or more follicles** [more than **0.5 mm** in diameter] on the superior tarsal plate\" (book p.93). There is no grade called T1 in the five-grade system (TF, TI, TS, TT, CO). Apart from the code, the description in the option is accurate, which is what makes it the strongest distractor. |\n| **Scarring \"pathogenomic\"** | Overclaim, and a misspelling of *pathognomonic* | Conjunctival scarring has many causes — chemical burns, ocular mucous membrane pemphigoid, Stevens–Johnson syndrome, and, as `opqb-t6-260` keys, severe mucopurulent conjunctivitis. What is *characteristic* of trachoma is the **pattern**: linear or stellate scars becoming the broad confluent **Arlt's line**, \"most prominent on the **upper tarsal plate**\", with **Herbert's pits** at the superior limbus. ⚠️ Book ch.6 records the non-trachomatous cicatrising conjunctivitides as absent from the chapter entirely. |\n\n**⚠️ None of the grading is in the lecture deck.** Measured over all 66 slides of `L6) Conjunctiva`: *WHO* = 0, *grading* = 0, *TF*/*TI*/*TS*/*TT*/*CO* = 0, *MacCallan* = 0, and the whole public-health vocabulary — *SAFE strategy*, *face washing*, *hygiene*, *fly/flies*, *mass treatment*, *notifiable* — returns zero as well, in the deck's **largest** disease block. Everything in this question comes from the book.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** When you are asked how to control trachoma, the answer is not a prescription. It is **SAFE**, and the two letters that do the long-term work are the two about water and faces.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Trachoma is a disease of poverty, overcrowding and poor hygiene; its control is the SAFE strategy, its serovars are A, B, Ba and C, its follicular WHO grade is TF, and conjunctival scarring is characteristic but not pathognomonic.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.36 (Q252), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-253', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'All of the following are true regarding **Vernal keratoconjunctivitis** **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['It is probably due to exposure to UV rays & dust.','Hot compresses are beneficial.','Topical steroids may be needed for short intervals.','The bulbar type is characterized by gelatinous masses which start medially & laterally.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Heat is the trigger, so heat is not the treatment. The material says COLD, twice, and this bank's own printed box says it a third time.**\n\n**Book p.89's VKC treatment list, in order:** avoid eye rubbing (\"result in **mast cell degranulation** and leads to a vicious circle of more itching and eye rubbing\") · \"**Dark glasses and cold compresses**\" · then topical mast cell stabilizers, antihistamines, vasoconstrictor/anti-histaminic drops during the attack only, steroid for acute attacks but avoiding long-term use, and cyclosporine or tacrolimus for severe cases as steroid-sparing agents. Book p.87 gives the same first line for seasonal allergic disease: \"**Cold compresses to relieve itching**\", and book p.94's grid puts *Cold compresses* at the head of both the viral and the allergic treatment columns. This bank's box at `opqb-t6-287` reprints it as item 2: \"Dark glasses and **cold compresses**.\" `opqb-t6-276` keys cold compresses again in a different disease.\n\nWarming an eye that is already itching from mast-cell mediators increases the itch and increases the rubbing, and rubbing is the one thing the book tells the patient to stop.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **UV rays & dust** | Not stated of VKC directly, but book p.88 attributes its incidence in **hot** regions to \"a higher level of pollution by **pollens and various other allergens**\", makes it a disease of \"**hot and dry climates**\" — West Africa, the Mediterranean basin, the Middle East — and this bank's own box at `opqb-t6-264` gives UV, dust and chronic irritation as pterygium's associations. ⚠️ *That UV and dust drive VKC specifically is **not taken from the course material**; the book's stated driver is airborne allergen in a hot climate. The option is not the exception because it is broadly consistent with the epidemiology the book does print.* |\n| **Topical steroids for short intervals** | Book p.89: \"**steroid to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use**\"; book p.94's allergic column: \"**Short course** of topical steroid provides symptomatic relief\". The word *short* is doing the work — `opqb-t6-225` keys what happens when it is not short. |\n| **Bulbar type with gelatinous masses medially & laterally** | Book p.88's limbal-signs row: \"**Limbal papillae** with whitish dots (Tranta spots)\". `L6) Conjunctiva` slides 39–42 photograph the gelatinous limbal thickening — slide 39 shows it \"at the upper and lower limbal margins\" of both eyes, slide 42 a collar encircling the cornea **360°**, \"widest at the **nasal and temporal** sides\". The interpalpebral (medial and lateral) quadrants are where it is widest, which is what the option describes. Keyed as the diagnosis at `opqb-t6-228` and `opqb-t6-271`. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The whole of first-line VKC treatment is **cold, dark and hands off**: cold compresses, dark glasses, no rubbing — then a mast cell stabilizer for maintenance, an antihistamine for breakthrough, and steroid only in short courses for acute attacks, with the lens and the pressure checked every time.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Cold compresses, not hot, are first-line in vernal keratoconjunctivitis — the book prints “Cold compresses” for allergic disease three times and never prints a warm one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.36 (Q253), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-254', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Which of the following helps in the confirmation of diagnosis of a patient with **mucopurulent discharge** as in the photo?\n\n*(Figure: a colour clinical photograph of both eyes of an elderly patient, viewed from the front. The bank prints no caption and no figure number.)*',
    options:['Bilaterality','Glued eye upon wakening','Glued eye at the ened of the day','All of the above'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Discharge accumulates while the eyes are shut, so the symptom that confirms it is finding the lids stuck together on WAKING — not at the end of the day.** Book p.91's symptom list for acute bacterial conjunctivitis prints it as a heading in its own right: \"**Matting of the eyelids** and lashes **on awakening** with difficulty in opening the eye.\"\n\nThe mechanism is simply arithmetic. During the day the eye blinks and waters and the discharge is washed onto the lid margin and wiped away; overnight the lids are closed for hours, tear turnover falls, and the mucopus dries between the lashes into a crust that gums them together. By evening the eye is sticky; by morning it is sealed. This bank keys the same history as a complete diagnosis at `opqb-t6-270`, where a mother reports that her child's lids \"were stuck together on waking up in the morning\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Bilaterality** | Not confirmatory, and often not even true at onset. Book p.91 prints \"*Initially presents **unilaterally**. However, the second eye is often affected shortly thereafter*\" (tutorial-marked). Bilaterality is more characteristic of **allergic** disease, which book p.87 lists as \"**Bilateral** eye involvement\" in its clinical-picture panel. |\n| **Glued eye at the ened of the day** | The inversion of the correct answer, and the reason the question exists. The eye is uncomfortable and sticky by evening but it is not glued shut; the gluing needs a night of closure. *(\"ened\" is the bank's own misprint for \"end\", kept as printed.)* |\n| **All of the above** | Excluded once A and C are excluded. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the reprinted photograph.** The identical picture is printed again three questions later as `opqb-t6-257`, where the bank asks what causes it and keys \"Bacterial\". The two questions are consistent with each other — a mucopurulent bacterial conjunctivitis producing overnight matting — and the reuse appears deliberate.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a red eye, the two history questions that separate the three great causes take five seconds each: *does it itch?* (allergic if intensely) and *were the lids stuck this morning?* (bacterial if yes). Book p.94's three-column grid is built on that pair plus the discharge, and `opqb-t6-229` sets it out in full.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Lids matted together ON WAKING is the confirmatory history of mucopurulent bacterial conjunctivitis; bilaterality is not confirmatory and the eye is sticky, not glued, by the end of the day.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.36 (Q254), key p.42 — figure printed with the stem, no caption' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-255', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'The sign in the photograph below is caused by:\n\n*(Figure: a close colour photograph of a conjunctival surface, the lid drawn away from the globe. The bank prints no caption, no figure number and no arrow.)*',
    options:['Allergy','Trachoma','Virus','All of the above'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The photograph shows discrete chalky yellow-white deposits scattered over a red conjunctival surface, and the bank keys them to trachoma — which makes them post-trachomatous concretions.**\n\n**PTCs are a cicatricial trachoma sign.** Book p.93 prints them as the last row of the cicatricial table: \"**Post-trachomatous concretions (PTCs)**: cause chronic irritation and foreign body sensation\", illustrated by \"an everted lid **studded with yellow-white concretions**\", captioned Fig 6-6. This bank's own box at `opqb-t6-230` reprints the line as item 3. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 15 is titled simply \"**PTCs**\" — the abbreviation is never expanded anywhere on the slide — over an everted upper lid showing \"a dense row of small, discrete, **CREAMY-YELLOW/WHITE rounded nodules**, clustered mainly along the upper part of the plate near the fornix\", and slide 30 re-shows the same photograph **inside the trachoma sequence**, which is the deck's way of naming the association without writing a word.\n\nThe concretions themselves are inspissated epithelial and mucous debris trapped in the scarred, distorted tarsal conjunctiva; they are hard, they project, and they scratch the cornea on every blink, which is exactly the \"chronic irritation and foreign body sensation\" the book describes. *(That composition is **not taken from the course material** — book ch.6 records \"Concretions in general (non-trachomatous)… absent (PTCs appear only as a trachoma sequela)\", and `L6` gives the abbreviation and nothing else. Removal with a needle under topical anaesthetic, likewise, is standard practice and not in either source.)*\n\n**⚠️ A DIVERGENCE INSIDE THIS TOPIC, RECORDED AND NOT CORRECTED.** Three questions later, `opqb-t6-258` prints a photograph of the same visual family — chalky white dots scattered over a red conjunctival surface — and keys it to **antihistamine**, i.e. to **allergy**. Both crops were looked at side by side before this was written. The two lesions are genuinely different diseases and the appearances are genuinely close:\n\n| | **PTCs** (this question) | **Trantas dots** (`opqb-t6-258`, `opqb-t6-231`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Where | **Tarsal** conjunctiva, in a **scarred** plate | **Limbus**, on a raised **gelatinous** band |\n| What they are | Inspissated epithelial/mucous debris | Degenerated **eosinophils** (book p.88) |\n| Background | Pale, scarred, avascular conjunctiva; Arlt's line may be visible | Thickened, gelatinous, injected limbal papillae |\n| Symptom | Foreign body sensation, irritation | **Itch** |\n| Disease | Cicatricial **trachoma** | **Vernal keratoconjunctivitis** |\n\nSo the discriminator is **where the dots sit and what they sit on**, not their colour.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** **allergy** gives Trantas dots at the limbus on a gelatinous band, in an itching child, with cobblestone papillae on the tarsus — see the table above; **virus** gives pale rounded **follicles** in the lower fornix with watering, and no chalky deposit of any kind; **all of the above** cannot stand once two of the three are excluded.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chalky yellow-white concretions studding a scarred tarsal conjunctiva are post-trachomatous concretions — a cicatricial trachoma sign causing foreign body sensation; Trantas dots look similar but sit on a gelatinous LIMBAL band and are allergic.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.36 (Q255), key p.42 — figure printed with the stem, no caption' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-256', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'The organism responsible for the sign shown below is:\n\n*(Figure: a close colour photograph of an eyelid and its lashes with the conjunctival surface exposed. The bank prints no caption and no figure number.)*',
    options:['Chlamydia trachomatous','Staphylococcus aures','Streptococcus','Moraxella'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The photograph shows a tarsal conjunctiva whose normal red vascular surface has been replaced by a dense confluent whitish sheet — the end-stage scarring of trachoma — and the organism of trachoma is *Chlamydia trachomatis*, serovars A, B, Ba and C.**\n\n**Book p.92 names it once and exactly:** \"Trachoma is associated principally with infection by serovars **A, B, Ba and C** of ***Chlamydia trachomatis***.\" The other end of the same genus is printed as a flow figure on book p.91:\n\n| Serovars | Disease |\n|---|---|\n| **A, B, Ba, C** | **Trachoma** |\n| **D → K** | Adult chlamydial (inclusion) conjunctivitis · **Ophthalmia neonatorum** |\n\n⚠️ The book contradicts itself by one letter across those two pages — the prose gives \"A, B, **Ba** and C\", the figure gives \"A, B, & C\" without Ba. Both are recorded as printed and neither is corrected.\n\n**What the picture is showing.** The tarsal plate is where trachoma does its worst work: book p.92 says \"Scarring is **most prominent on the upper tarsal plate**\", progressing from \"Linear or stellate conjunctival scars\" in mild disease to \"**Broad confluent scars (Arlt's line)**\" in severe disease. `L6) Conjunctiva` photographs the whole progression across four slides — slide 16 (\"Scarring\") shows a tarsus that \"has lost its normal vascular pattern and is replaced by a smooth, **PALE, avascular sheet** with fine whitish linear/stellate streaks\"; slide 31 shows \"a dense fine white **FIBROUS reticulum**\"; and slide 33, the deck's most advanced picture, shows the tarsal conjunctiva \"almost entirely replaced by **DENSE WHITE SCAR** — a broad, confluent, chalky-white sheet occupying the centre of the tarsal plate, with the residual pink conjunctiva pushed to the edges\". Slide 32 re-shows slide 16's photograph inside the trachoma sequence, which is the deck's only way of attributing it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it causes |\n|---|---|\n| **Staphylococcus aureus** | **Anterior blepharitis** (book ch.5), acute bacterial conjunctivitis with papillae and mucopurulent discharge (book p.91), corneal ulcer, endophthalmitis — and it is the antigen source of a **phlycten**. It does not scar the tarsus into a white sheet. |\n| **Streptococcus** | Acute bacterial conjunctivitis; *S. pneumoniae* is one of book p.91's four commonest organisms and is this bank's petechial-haemorrhage type at `opqb-t6-250`. Acute, not cicatrising. |\n| **Moraxella** | *M. catarrhalis* in book p.91's list, and the Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus of **angular** conjunctivitis (`opqb-t6-235`), where the inflammation sits at the canthi and macerates the skin. Nothing about it produces tarsal scarring. |\n\n**⚠️ The organism is in the book alone.** `L6) Conjunctiva` gives trachoma nine slides and titles one of them \"Chlamydial Conjunctivitis (Trachoma)\" — that adjective is the closest the whole 66-slide deck comes to naming an organism. Measured over the deck: *\"Chlamydia trachomatis\"* = 0, *Staphyl* = 0, *Streptoc* = 0, *Morax* = 0.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A scarred upper tarsus is a permanent record of an infection that has already ended. What it predicts is what comes next — **trichiasis**, **cicatricial entropion**, corneal opacification — so the examination that matters is of the lid margin and the cornea, and the treatment is surgical, not antibiotic (`opqb-t6-246`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Trachoma is Chlamydia trachomatis serovars A, B, Ba and C, and its cicatricial hallmark is dense confluent scarring of the UPPER tarsal plate — Arlt’s line at its most advanced.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.37 (Q256), key p.42 — figure printed with the stem, no caption' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-257', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'The picture below is caused by:\n\n*(Figure: a colour clinical photograph of both eyes of an elderly patient, viewed from the front — the same photograph the bank prints at `opqb-t6-254`. No caption, no figure number.)*',
    options:['Virus','Bacterial','Allergy','None of the above'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**⚠️ This is the same photograph the bank printed three questions earlier at `opqb-t6-254`** — verified by looking at both crops side by side: the same elderly face, the same crusted discharge on the left lower lashes and at the medial canthus, the same framing. There it was captioned \"a patient with **mucopurulent discharge**\" and the question was how to confirm the diagnosis; here the diagnosis itself is asked, and the answer follows from the discharge.\n\n**Mucopurulent, crusted, matting the lashes = bacterial.** Book p.94's three-column grid settles it in one row:\n\n| | Viral | **Bacterial** | Allergic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Itching** | Mild | Mild | Intense |\n| **Discharge** | Watery | **Mucopurulent / purulent** | Mucoid (ropy) |\n| **Signs** | Follicles + preauricular lymphadenopathy | **Papillae** | Papillae (cobblestone) |\n| **Treatment** | Cold compresses, artificial tears, ± short topical steroid | **Topical antibiotics (drops and ointment)** | Cold compresses, topical antihistamine, mast cell stabilizer, ± short topical steroid |\n\nBook p.91's symptom list adds the visible detail this photograph carries: \"**Matting of the eyelids** and lashes on awakening with difficulty in opening the eye\", and its signs list gives \"Oedema and erythema of the eyelids\" and \"**Mucopurulent or purulent discharge**\". `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 17, titled \"Bacterial Conjunctivitis (**Mucopurulent**)\", photographs the same appearance: \"Yellow-white **crusting and stringy mucopurulent material cling to the lashes and the medial canthus**\", with the cornea clear and the pupil round.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What the photograph would have shown |\n|---|---|\n| **Virus** | A **watery** eye, not a crusted one, with lower tarsal follicles and a tender preauricular node; often unilateral first and then bilateral. `L6` slide 34 shows the follicles; the discharge in the viral pictures is not crusted onto the lashes. |\n| **Allergy** | **Bilateral** and symmetrical with lid oedema, chemosis and **ropy mucus** — a strand you can lift out of the fornix, not a dried crust on the lid margin. The dominant complaint would be itch. |\n| **None of the above** | Excluded the moment the discharge is identified. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The asymmetry in this photograph is itself informative: book p.91 records that bacterial conjunctivitis \"*Initially presents **unilaterally**. However, the second eye is often affected shortly thereafter*\", which is exactly the stage this patient is at. The treatment is topical antibiotic, strict hygiene, no patch and no contact lenses (`opqb-t6-227`, `opqb-t6-238`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Crusted mucopurulent discharge matting the lashes and medial canthus is bacterial conjunctivitis — the discharge, not the redness, names the cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.37 (Q257), key p.42 — figure printed with the stem, no caption; the same photograph is printed at Q254 on p.36' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-258', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'What is the treatment for the condition shown below?\n\n*(Figure: a close colour photograph of an everted lid, its margin running diagonally across the frame. The bank prints no caption and no figure number.)*',
    options:['Antibiotic','Antiviral','Antihistamine','All of the above'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The photograph shows chalky white dots on a red, velvety, thickened conjunctival surface, and the bank keys the treatment to an antihistamine — so the disease it intends is allergic, and the dots are Trantas dots on the limbal papillae of vernal keratoconjunctivitis.**\n\n**Why an antihistamine.** Book p.89's VKC treatment list names \"**Medications: → Topical:** mast cell stabilizers · **antihistamines** · vasoconstrictor and anti-histaminic drops (during the attack only) · steroid to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use · cyclosporine or topical tacrolimus… as steroid-sparing agents\". Book p.94's allergic column gives \"Cold compresses · **Topical antihistamine drops** · Topical mast cell stabilizer · Short course of topical steroid\". Book p.89 names the only two anti-allergy drugs in the chapter — **Ketotifen** and **olopatadine** — and names them under atopic keratoconjunctivitis rather than under VKC. The bank's own printed box at `opqb-t6-287` is the fullest version of the same list.\n\nAn antihistamine blocks the H1 receptor and so relieves the itch quickly; a mast cell stabilizer prevents the degranulation but takes days to work, which is why the two are usually prescribed together and why book p.89 lists them side by side.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | When it would be right |\n|---|---|\n| **Antibiotic** | Bacterial conjunctivitis — mucopurulent or purulent discharge, papillae, lids matted on waking (book p.94). Not an itching eye with limbal deposits. |\n| **Antiviral** | Herpes simplex disease. It has no role in adenoviral conjunctivitis, which book p.90 treats with hygiene and time, and none at all in allergy. See `opqb-t6-251`. |\n| **All of the above** | Excluded once two of the three are. It is also the wrong instinct clinically: an itching allergic eye given an antibiotic gets no better and may get a toxic conjunctivitis on top, which book p.84 lists as a cause in its own right (\"Some topical medications e.g., **Brimonidine**\"). |\n\n**⚠️ A DIVERGENCE INSIDE THIS TOPIC, RECORDED AND NOT CORRECTED.** `opqb-t6-255`, three questions earlier, prints a photograph of the same visual family — discrete chalky white deposits on a red conjunctival surface — and keys it to **trachoma**, i.e. to post-trachomatous concretions. Both crops were looked at side by side. The full discriminating table is written once, at `opqb-t6-255`; in one line: **Trantas dots sit at the LIMBUS on a raised gelatinous band in an itching eye; PTCs sit on a SCARRED TARSUS in an eye with foreign-body sensation.** The bank offers no way to tell which it means except the answer options, and here the options name a treatment rather than a disease.\n\n**⚠️ The deck photographs this sign four times and never names it.** `L6) Conjunctiva` slides 39–42 are all limbal VKC, slide 39 with a black arrow printed on the picture pointing at the dots and no caption; *Trantas* returns **zero hits** across all 66 slides, and so does every drug name — the deck contains no pharmacology whatever (*antihistamin* = 0, *steroid* = 0, *cromoglyc* = 0, *olopatadine* = 0).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chalky limbal Trantas dots on a red gelatinous surface are vernal keratoconjunctivitis, treated topically with antihistamines and mast cell stabilizers — not antibiotics and not antivirals.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.37 (Q258), key p.42 — figure printed with the stem, no caption' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-259', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 50-year-old female with **rheumatoid arthritis** complained of red eye, tearing, and burning sensation for the past several years. She also complained that her vision is not as clear as before and **improved with blinking**. The most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Allergic conjunctivitis.','Dry eye.','Trachoma.','Chronic dacryocystitis.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Rheumatoid arthritis → commonly associated with **secondary Sjögren's syndrome** → dry eye.\nChronic red eye, burning, foreign body sensation → hallmark of **keratoconjunctivitis sicca**.\n**Blurred vision improves with blinking** → classic sign of tear film **instability (dry eye)**.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim including its three arrows. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Every element of the vignette is a dry-eye element, and the systemic disease is the one that gives it away before the eye is examined.** Rheumatoid arthritis is the commonest cause of **secondary Sjögren syndrome**, in which lymphocytic infiltration destroys the lacrimal and salivary glands; the ocular result is aqueous-deficient dry eye — *keratoconjunctivitis sicca*.\n\n**Why a dry eye WATERS, which is the part students misread.** Two mechanisms run together. An unstable tear film breaks up between blinks and exposes the corneal epithelium, which is richly innervated; the irritation triggers **reflex** lacrimation, so the patient complains of watering while the basal secretion is deficient. Book ch.4 records this as \"**paradoxical watering**\". The burning and grittiness are the primary complaint; the tearing is the eye's answer to it.\n\n**Why vision improves with blinking.** The tear film is the eye's **first refracting surface**, and it is a smoother one than the epithelium beneath it. As it breaks up, the surface becomes optically irregular and the image degrades; a blink resurfaces it and the vision clears — for a few seconds, until it breaks up again. That transient clearing is close to pathognomonic of an unstable film, and it is what the box calls \"a classic sign of tear film instability\".\n\n**⚠️ The same symptom is given to a different disease elsewhere in this material, recorded and not corrected.** Book p.91 lists, among the symptoms of **acute bacterial conjunctivitis**, \"*Blurred vision that improves with blinking (suggests a **discharge** on the ocular surface)*\" — tutorial-marked, form (b). So the book attributes blink-dependent blurring to discharge and this bank's box attributes it to tear-film instability. Both readings are correct in their own setting, and the discriminator is the history: **several years** of burning in a rheumatoid patient is not an acute bacterial conjunctivitis.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Allergic conjunctivitis** | The cardinal symptom would be **intense itching** (book p.94's grid), with ropy mucoid discharge and papillae, and it is typically episodic and seasonal rather than a several-year burn. No systemic association with rheumatoid arthritis. |\n| **Trachoma** | A chronic chlamydial infection of poverty and overcrowding, giving upper tarsal follicles then scarring, Arlt's line, trichiasis and entropion. It can *cause* dry eye — `opqb-t6-274` keys exactly that — but it is a disease of endemic communities, not of a 50-year-old with rheumatoid disease, and nothing in the stem is cicatricial. |\n| **Chronic dacryocystitis** | An **excretory** problem: a blocked nasolacrimal duct with a distended, infected sac, giving epiphora, recurrent unilateral conjunctivitis and mucopurulent **regurgitation on pressing the sac**. Typically **unilateral**, with a wet eye rather than a burning one, and there is no mention of discharge or reflux here. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A red, burning, gritty eye of years' duration in a patient with an autoimmune disease is dry eye until measured otherwise, and the two measurements are **TBUT** for stability (book ch.4 makes under 10 seconds abnormal) and the tear-volume test for quantity. In rheumatoid disease the eye is also the place where something more dangerous can appear — the scleritis of book ch.7 — so a red eye that is **painful and tender** in the same patient is a different, urgent diagnosis (`opqb-t6-232`).\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Conjunctiva — the question is printed in the conjunctiva topic and asks a red-eye differential, but the disease it keys is a lacrimal/tear-film one and is taught in book ch.4.)*",
    objective:'Rheumatoid arthritis → secondary Sjögren → aqueous-deficient dry eye: chronic burning and redness with paradoxical watering, and blurring that clears with each blink because the tear film is the eye’s first refracting surface.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.37 (Q259), key p.42 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-260', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'The most commonly serious complication of **muco-purulent conjunctivitis** is:',
    options:['Madarosis.','Conjunctival scarring.','Dry eye.','Corneal ulcer.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A conjunctivitis is a nuisance until it reaches the cornea; then it can blind. The corneal ulcer is both the commonest serious complication and the reason the whole management of a discharging eye is built around drainage.**\n\n**The material supports it from three directions.** Book p.91 lists \"**Corneal ulceration** in gonococcal and meningococcal infections\" among the signs of acute bacterial conjunctivitis, and its Tutorial referral box warns to \"Be aware that may cause **marginal keratitis**\" — reprinted, unmarked, in the DD table on p.94. Book p.94's five negatives that define a red eye as *merely* conjunctival include \"**Clear cornea**\", so a cornea that stops being clear is a conjunctivitis that has stopped being simple. And `L6) Conjunctiva` shows the endpoint without a word: slide 21 photographs a child's eye held open by a speculum with \"a large, dense, white-grey opaque area occupying most of the cornea with a raised, irregular central bulge and surrounding scarring — **the end-result of the untreated purulent infection of the preceding slides**\".\n\n**Why it happens, and why patching makes it worse.** Retained mucopus lies against the cornea, macerates the epithelium and delivers a high bacterial load to any break in it. That is the whole reason book p.91 opens the treatment with \"**Avoid eye patching**\" — this bank keys the same rule three times, at `opqb-t6-227`, `opqb-t6-238` and `opqb-t6-247`. The complication and the contraindication are the same fact told twice.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually causes it |\n|---|---|\n| **Madarosis** | Loss of the lashes — a **lid margin** disease. Book ch.5 lists \"**Madarosis**, trichiasis and poliosis\" among the complications of chronic staphylococcal **blepharitis**, not of conjunctivitis. |\n| **Conjunctival scarring** | Real but uncommon after simple mucopurulent disease, and characteristic of other processes: **trachoma** (Arlt's line, book p.92), and the cicatrising conjunctivitides that book ch.6 records as absent from the chapter — pemphigoid, Stevens–Johnson, chemical burn. Severity, not frequency, is not the issue; frequency is, and this is not the common one. |\n| **Dry eye** | A consequence of scarring that destroys goblet cells and the accessory lacrimal ductules — which is why `opqb-t6-274` keys it as a complication of **trachoma**, where the fornix fibroses. It is a late sequel of a cicatrising disease, not of an acute discharging one. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In every red eye with discharge, the examination that matters takes ten seconds: **check the visual acuity and stain the cornea**. Book p.94's grid tells you what the discharge means; the fluorescein tells you whether it has already cost the patient something. `opqb-t6-278` keys exactly that test in a different setting.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The commonest serious complication of mucopurulent conjunctivitis is corneal ulceration — which is why a discharging eye is never patched and why the cornea is stained at every visit.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.37 (Q260), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-261', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'All of the following may be seen with **trachoma** except:',
    options:['Follicular conjunctivitis.',"Taranta's spots.","Herbert's pits.",'Conjunctival scarring.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Trantas spots belong to vernal keratoconjunctivitis, not to trachoma — they are the one allergic sign in a list of chlamydial ones.** Book p.88 places them precisely: they are \"**Limbal papillae with whitish dots (Tranta spots)** that consist of degenerated **eosinophils**\", printed under the limbal signs of VKC. Eosinophils are allergy's cell; there is no eosinophil in the trachoma story.\n\n**⚠️ THIS BANK SPELLS THE EPONYM FOUR DIFFERENT WAYS IN ONE TOPIC**, and this option is the third of them:\n\n| Where | As printed |\n|---|---|\n| `opqb-t6-231` stem | Trant's spots |\n| `opqb-t6-231` box | Tranta spots |\n| **here, option B** | **Taranta's spots** |\n| `opqb-t6-288` option B | Trantas dots |\n\nBook p.88 and its Fig 6-4 print \"Tranta spots\". The usual textbook form, *Horner–Trantas dots*, occurs nowhere in this module.\n\n**Why the other three genuinely are trachoma:**\n\n| Option | Stage | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Follicular conjunctivitis** | **Active** | Book p.92: \"**Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis** associated with mucopurulent discharge\"; WHO grade **TF** requires 5 or more follicles over 0.5 mm on the superior tarsal plate |\n| **Herbert's pits** | **Cicatricial** | Book p.92: \"Superior limbal follicles may resolve to leave a **row of shallow depressions**\" |\n| **Conjunctival scarring** | **Cicatricial** | Book p.92: linear or stellate scars in mild disease, broad confluent **Arlt's line** in severe disease, worst on the upper tarsal plate; WHO grade **TS** |\n\nThe full active-versus-cicatricial table and the WHO grades are written once, at `opqb-t6-230`.\n\n**⚠️ The trap this question is built on.** Both diseases produce a chalky white sign at the **superior limbus**, and the deck photographs both without naming either:\n\n| | **Trachoma** | **Vernal keratoconjunctivitis** |\n|---|---|---|\n| The limbal lesion | **Herbert's rosettes** → resolve into **pits**, i.e. *depressions* | **Limbal papillae** bearing **Trantas dots**, i.e. *raised* chalky deposits |\n| What the dots are made of | (pits are not deposits) | Degenerated **eosinophils** |\n| Symptom | Irritation, foreign body sensation | **Itch** |\n| `L6` slides | 25–33, never named | 39–42, never named |\n\n**Raised dots on a gelatinous band = Trantas. Depressions in a scarred limbus = Herbert.** `opqb-t6-288` makes that the whole of its question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Trantas spots are limbal papillae topped with degenerated eosinophils and belong to vernal keratoconjunctivitis; trachoma’s limbal sign is Herbert’s rosettes resolving into pits — raised dots versus depressions.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q261), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-262', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'**Lacrimation in neonates** necessitates proper ophthalmic examination to check for the following except:',
    options:['Microphthalmia.','Birth corneal trauma.','Ophthalmia neonatorum.','Nasolacrimal duct obstruction.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A small eye is not a wet eye. Microphthalmia is a developmental failure of globe growth, and it presents as a small or absent-looking eye, poor vision and often other ocular malformations — not as watering.** The other three all put tears on the cheek, each by a different route.\n\n**The three real causes, and the mechanism of each:**\n\n| Cause | Route to a watering eye |\n|---|---|\n| **Nasolacrimal duct obstruction** | **Drainage failure.** The commonest by far — an imperforate membrane at the valve of Hasner, the last part of the drainage system to canalise. Tears are made normally and cannot leave. |\n| **Ophthalmia neonatorum** | **Inflammation.** Book ch.16 (printed 211) defines it as \"a type of **conjunctivitis** that takes place in the neonatal period [1st month of life], and it can have severe complications such as **corneal ulcer and perforation**\", from *N. gonorrhoeae*, *Chlamydia trachomatis* (D→K), herpes simplex type 2, or the non-sexually-transmitted staphylococci, streptococci and *Hemophilus*. |\n| **Birth corneal trauma** | **Reflex hypersecretion.** Forceps injury tears Descemet's membrane; the resulting corneal oedema and epithelial disturbance irritate the richly innervated corneal surface and drive reflex tearing, with photophobia. |\n\n**⚠️ And note the diagnosis that is NOT on the bank's list, because it is the one that must never be missed.** Book ch.16 (printed 212) gives **primary congenital glaucoma** \"the classic '**triad**' of symptoms\" produced by raised pressure and corneal oedema — and the first of them is epiphora, alongside photophobia and blepharospasm. A watering, photophobic neonate with an enlarging, hazy cornea is a surgical emergency, and any list of causes of neonatal watering that omits it is incomplete. The bank omits it here; do not.\n\n**How the three separate at the cot side:** *discharge* points to ophthalmia neonatorum (and copious pus points to gonococcus, which needs an urgent Gram stain and systemic **ceftriaxone**, per book ch.16); *mucoid reflux on pressing the sac* points to NLD obstruction; *a hazy cornea with photophobia* points to birth trauma — or to congenital glaucoma, and the corneal **diameter** is what separates those two.\n\n*(The mechanism of forceps-induced Descemet tears, and the description of microphthalmia, are **not taken from the course material** — book ch.6 does not discuss neonatal watering at all, `L6) Conjunctiva` returns zero for *neonatorum*, and microphthalmia is not in either conjunctiva source. Ophthalmia neonatorum and congenital glaucoma are both from book ch.16.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A watering baby gets three things looked at before anyone reaches for a diagnosis of blocked duct: **the discharge**, **the cornea** (clarity and diameter), and **the sac** on pressure. Congenital NLD obstruction is the commonest answer and the most benign, and it is exactly for that reason that it must be the diagnosis you arrive at, not the one you assume.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Conjunctiva — ophthalmia neonatorum is a conjunctivitis and the question is printed in the conjunctiva topic, but what it tests is the differential of neonatal epiphora.)*",
    objective:'Neonatal watering means drainage failure (NLD obstruction), inflammation (ophthalmia neonatorum) or reflex hypersecretion (birth corneal trauma) — and, though this bank omits it, congenital glaucoma; microphthalmia does not water.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q262), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-263', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'The following sign is **not** found in **vernal** conjunctivitis:',
    options:['Bulbar masses on both sides of the limbus.','Ropy discharge in the fornix','Papillae in the lower fornix.','Ciliary injection.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Papillae cannot occupy a fornix, and the reason is anatomical.** Book p.83: \"The **palpebral conjunctiva** is **firmly adherent to the tarsal plates** in the lids\", while the conjunctiva reaching the fornix is loose and redundant. A papilla is a fold of hyperplastic epithelium raised over a fibrovascular core and tethered at its base to that adherent layer, so papillae are limited to the tarsal plate and stop at its edge. Follicles, being lymphoid aggregates in the loose subepithelial adenoid layer, do the reverse — `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 13 records them as \"most numerous **towards the fornix** end of the plate\". This bank keys the same fact from the other direction at `opqb-t6-241` (\"Always involving the fornix\" is the exception there).\n\n**⚠️ TWO OPTIONS ARE DEFENSIBLE HERE, AND THE KEY IS C. Recorded, not disputed.** Option **D, ciliary injection**, is also not a sign of vernal disease, and the deck is unusually clear about why. `L6) Conjunctiva` teaches the two injection patterns as a photographic pair:\n\n| | **Conjunctival injection** (slides 9–10) | **Ciliary injection** (slide 11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Colour | \"**BRIGHT RED**… individually visible, tortuous, **superficial** conjunctival vessels\" | \"a deep, dusky **pink/violaceous** flush\"; \"individual vessels are **not resolvable**\" |\n| Distribution | \"heaviest **PERIPHERALLY** and in the fornices, **FADING** as it approaches the limbus\" | \"**CIRCUM-CORNEAL** — a ring in the sclera immediately around the limbus, **densest at the limbus** and fading peripherally\" |\n| The eye in the photograph | clear cornea, round pupil | \"cornea is hazy\" with a fluorescein-stained ulcer and white exudate |\n| What it means | Conjunctival disease | **Corneal or intraocular** disease — keratitis, uveitis, acute glaucoma |\n\nBook p.85 says the same in five words: conjunctivitis gives \"Red eye (Conjunctival injection *sparing the limbal area*)\". Vernal disease is conjunctival, so its redness spares the limbus; a circumcorneal flush in a VKC patient means the cornea has been breached — a shield ulcer, for instance — and is a different, more serious finding. **Within this bank's own logic C is the better answer**, because C is the point the bank keys twice and D is a sign the bank does not otherwise discuss; but a candidate who chooses D has not misunderstood the disease.\n\n**Why the other two are found in vernal disease:**\n\n| Option | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Bulbar masses on both sides of the limbus** | The limbal form. `L6` slide 39 photographs \"raised, gelatinous, greyish-pink thickened swellings\" at the limbal margins of both eyes; slide 42 shows a **360°** gelatinous collar \"widest at the **nasal and temporal** sides\" — which is \"both sides of the limbus\". Keyed as the diagnosis at `opqb-t6-228` and `opqb-t6-271`. |\n| **Ropy discharge in the fornix** | Book p.88's symptom list: \"**Mucous 'ropy'** discharge\", illustrated by a figure captioned \"ropy discharge\" in which a clinician draws a strand of mucus \"out of the **lower fornix**\". Book p.94's grid gives allergic disease \"Mucoid (ropy)\" discharge. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the stem's misprint:** it reads \"**vermal** conjunctivitis\", for *vernal*, and is kept as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Papillae are bound to the tarsal plate and never occupy the fornix; and conjunctival injection spares the limbus, so a ciliary (circumcorneal) flush in a vernal eye signals corneal or intraocular disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q263), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-264', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Pterygium** can be a cause of the following **except**:',
    options:['Irregular astigmatism.','Keratoconus.','Dry eye','Corneal vascularization.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Pterygium is A **fibrovascular growth of conjunctiva onto the cornea, usually nasal.**\nAssociated **with UV exposure, dust, and chronic irritation.**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim including its stray capital A. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Keratoconus is a bilateral, progressive, non-inflammatory ectasia in which the corneal stroma thins and cones forward. It is not something a conjunctival fold can produce.** A pterygium is a growth *onto* the surface; it flattens the cornea it pulls on, it does not thin it. Where keratoconus does appear in this material it is attached to a different disease entirely: book p.89 lists **\"Keratoconus is more common in VKC patients as well; possibly due to increased eye rubbing** of chronically irritated patients\", and repeats the association under atopic keratoconjunctivitis (\"Keratoconus is common\"). So the association is with **allergic** disease and with rubbing — which is why book p.89 opens VKC treatment by telling the patient to stop rubbing.\n\n**Why the other three genuinely do follow a pterygium:**\n\n| Option | How the pterygium causes it |\n|---|---|\n| **Irregular astigmatism** | Book p.95: it \"may **decrease vision** if it induces **astigmatism** or covers the pupillary area\", and \"induced astigmatism\" is one of the four printed **indications for surgery**. The fold tugs the nasal cornea flat, distorting the surface — regularly at first and irregularly as it advances. `opqb-t6-281` keys a pterygium whose astigmatism is still regular enough to be corrected by a cylinder. |\n| **Dry eye** | The lesion stands proud of the corneal surface, so the lid cannot spread the tear film evenly over it and a dry spot forms in front of the head. The book prints the same mechanism for the pterygium's cousin — pinguecula \"**May be irritating due to abnormal tear film distribution**\" (p.94) — and prescribes lubricating drops for both. |\n| **Corneal vascularization** | A pterygium is by definition **fibrovascular**, and it carries its vessels onto the cornea with it. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 53 photographs the head \"climbing over the limbus onto the cornea, with **small vessels looping over it onto the corneal surface**\". |\n\n**The full pterygium picture — its cause, its management and its recurrence figures — is written once at `opqb-t6-249`** and is not repeated here. The one line worth carrying from this box is its second sentence, because it also answers `opqb-t6-267`: the association is with **UV exposure, dust and chronic irritation**.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture deck gives pterygium eight slides and no cause.** `L6` slides 50–57 photograph the fleshy form, the thin atrophic form, lesions crossing the limbus against lesions stopping at it, and an operation — and *UV*, *ultraviolet*, *sunlight*, *dust*, *wind*, *treatment*, *excision*, *graft* and *recurrence rate* all return **zero** across its 66 slides. Every fact in this entry that is sourced is the book's.",
    objective:'A pterygium causes irregular astigmatism, tear-film disturbance and corneal vascularization; keratoconus is an ectasia associated with vernal and atopic disease and eye rubbing, not with pterygium.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q264), key p.42 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-265', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In **ophthalmia neonatorum** the most common **dangerous** causative organism is:',
    options:['E. coli','Gonococci.','Xerosis bacilli.','Herpes simplex virus.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The word in the stem is *dangerous*, and the organism that earns it is *Neisseria gonorrhoeae* — because it is the one that perforates a cornea in days.**\n\n**Book ch.16 (printed 211) teaches ophthalmia neonatorum in full**, and it is the only place in the module that does. It defines the condition as \"a type of **conjunctivitis** that takes place in the neonatal period [1st month of life], and it can have severe complications such as **corneal ulcer and perforation**\", caused by \"**infection from the birth canal**\", and divides the organisms:\n\n| | Organisms |\n|---|---|\n| **Sexually transmitted** | **1.** *Neisseria gonorrhoea* — \"**cause purulent conjunctivitis**\" · **2.** *Chlamydia trachomatis* (serotypes **D→K**, \"also known as **Inclusion blennorrhoea** or chlamydia oculogenitalis\") · **3.** Herpes simplex type 2 (genital herpes) |\n| **Non-sexually transmitted** | Staphylococci, streptococci and *Hemophilus* |\n\nand its complications box gives the reason gonococcus is the dangerous one: \"Conjunctival scarring · **Corneal opacification, ulceration, perforation, endophthalmitis, loss of eye, and blindness**.\"\n\n**Why gonococcus specifically.** It is the organism the book links to purulent discharge — book p.85's own N.B. reads \"Severe purulent discharge is suggestive of **gonococcal** conjunctivitis\" — and book p.91 attaches **corneal ulceration** and preauricular/submandibular lymphadenopathy to gonococcal and meningococcal infection and requires **systemic** antibiotics for both. `L6) Conjunctiva` shows the disease without naming it: slide 18, titled \"Bacterial Conjunctivitis (Purulent)\", is \"a **NEWBORN** infant's face… both lids grossly swollen, tense and shiny\" with pus escaping, and the deck's own note says \"**the age of the patient is the whole point and it is carried only by the photograph**\"; slide 21 is the outcome, a cornea replaced by dense white opacity.\n\n**Treatment, from book ch.16:** gonococcal disease \"treated early with **systemic (Ceftriaxone)** and topical antibiotics\"; chlamydial \"treated by oral **Erythromycin**\"; and mothers with suspected STDs \"should be treated **before childbirth**\", with topical erythromycin ointment used in suspected cases.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **E. coli** | A Gram-negative coliform that can cause a neonatal conjunctivitis but is neither the commonest nor the dangerous one, and book ch.16's non-sexually-transmitted list does not include it. |\n| **Xerosis bacilli** | *Corynebacterium xerosis*, a commensal of the conjunctival sac and essentially non-pathogenic. ⚠️ *xerosis* returns **zero hits** across all 36 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 cached book chapters — the term exists in this module only as this distractor. **Not taken from the course material.** |\n| **Herpes simplex virus** | Genuinely on book ch.16's list (type 2, genital herpes) and genuinely serious — but it is uncommon, and it threatens the child through **disseminated systemic infection and encephalitis** rather than by melting a cornea in days. It is not the *most common* dangerous organism. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Any purulent discharge in the first month of life is gonococcal until a Gram stain says otherwise, and it does not wait for the morning list. ⚠️ Note where this is **not** taught: book ch.6, the conjunctiva chapter, mentions ophthalmia neonatorum **once**, inside the serovar figure on p.91, with no organisms, no timing, no prophylaxis and no treatment — and `L6) Conjunctiva` returns **zero** for *neonatorum*. Everything above is book ch.16.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Neisseria gonorrhoeae is the dangerous organism of ophthalmia neonatorum — purulent discharge progressing to corneal ulceration and perforation; treat with systemic ceftriaxone plus topical antibiotic.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q265), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-266', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Vernal conjunctivitis** is **not** treated with:',
    options:['Antibiotics.','Corticosteroids.','Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory.','Mast cell stabilizers.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is an allergic disease. There is no organism, so there is nothing for an antibiotic to do.**\n\n**Book p.89's VKC treatment list, complete, and no antibiotic appears anywhere in it:**\n\n1. Avoid eye rubbing — it \"result in **mast cell degranulation** and leads to a vicious circle of more itching and eye rubbing\"\n2. **Dark glasses and cold compresses**\n3. Topical **mast cell stabilizers** — option D\n4. Topical **antihistamines**\n5. Topical vasoconstrictor and anti-histaminic drops, during the attack only\n6. Topical **steroid** to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use — option B\n7. Topical **cyclosporine or tacrolimus** for severe cases, as steroid-sparing agents\n\nBook p.94's allergic column gives the same shorter list — cold compresses, topical antihistamine drops, topical mast cell stabilizer, short course of topical steroid. This bank reprints the fullest version as its own box at `opqb-t6-287`.\n\n**Why the other three are used:**\n\n| Option | Where it sits |\n|---|---|\n| **Corticosteroids** | Yes, but **topically and briefly** — for acute attacks only, because of the cataract and pressure complications that `opqb-t6-225` keys. `opqb-t6-287` keys the further restriction that **systemic** steroids are not routine. |\n| **Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory** | ⚠️ **Not in the book's list, and not in `L6`.** Topical NSAIDs are used in practice for the itch and irritation of allergic conjunctivitis as a steroid-sparing measure. *Not taken from the course material* — but it is not the exception the bank is keying, since the bank keys antibiotics. |\n| **Mast cell stabilizers** | The maintenance drug — book p.89 names them first among the topicals, and book p.89 names **Ketotifen** and **olopatadine** as the only two anti-allergy drugs in the whole chapter (under AKC, not VKC). |\n\n**When an antibiotic IS reasonable in an allergic eye**, so the rule is not applied blindly: if a secondary bacterial infection supervenes — the discharge changes from ropy mucus to mucopus, the lids start to mat on waking — then treat that infection. Book p.90 makes the same allowance for viral disease (\"Topical antibiotics if 2ry bacterial infection is suspected\"). What is wrong is treating the vernal disease *itself* with an antibiotic.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The two failures of instinct in a sticky, red, itching child's eye are opposite and both common: giving an antibiotic to an allergic eye, and giving a steroid to an infected one. The discriminator is book p.94's grid — **intense itch and ropy mucus with cobblestone papillae is allergic; matted lids and mucopus with plain papillae is bacterial** — and the histories differ before either eye is opened.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is treated with cold compresses, dark glasses, mast cell stabilizers, antihistamines and short courses of topical steroid — never with antibiotics, since no organism is involved.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q266), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-267', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Pterygium** is caused by the following factor:',
    options:['Exposure to infra-red rays.','Exposure to ionic radiation.','Exposure to ultraviolet rays.','Exposure to an allergen.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Ultraviolet light is the printed association, and this bank prints it itself.** Its own box at `opqb-t6-264` reads: \"Pterygium is A fibrovascular growth of conjunctiva onto the cornea, usually nasal. Associated **with UV exposure, dust, and chronic irritation**.\" Book ch.6, printed 94, gives the same association for the pterygium's cousin, pinguecula — \"Associated with **sun and wind exposure**, aging\" — and `L37) Red Eye` states it for the pterygium directly: it \"is associated with **ultraviolet exposure** and occurs more frequently in **tropical climates**\".\n\n**Why UV, and why nasal.** The lesion is an elastotic degeneration of the subepithelial conjunctival collagen driven by chronic actinic damage, which is why pterygium is a disease of outdoor workers in sunny latitudes — this bank makes its patient a **soldier** at `opqb-t6-281`. It sits in the **interpalpebral** zone, the strip of conjunctiva the lids never cover, and is commonest **nasally** because light entering temporally is refracted by the cornea and focused onto the nasal limbus. *(The elastotic-degeneration mechanism and the nasal-focusing explanation are **not taken from the course material** — book ch.6 records \"Pterygium histology/pathogenesis (elastotic degeneration, UV mechanism)… absent\", and `L6) Conjunctiva` gives pterygium eight slides with no cause at all: *UV*, *ultraviolet*, *sunlight*, *dust* and *wind* all return zero across its 66 slides. They are standard, uncontested teaching.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually does to the eye |\n|---|---|\n| **Infra-red rays** | Heat, not photochemical damage. Chronic infra-red exposure is the classical cause of **glassblower's cataract** — a lens injury, not a conjunctival one. |\n| **Ionic radiation** | *(the bank's spelling of \"ionizing\")* Ionizing radiation causes **radiation cataract**, dry eye from lacrimal gland damage, and radiation retinopathy. Book p.84 does list irradiation among the **toxic** causes of conjunctivitis — \"Exposure to smoke, **irradiation**, chemicals, or fumes\" — but a toxic conjunctivitis is an inflammation, not a fibrovascular growth. |\n| **Exposure to an allergen** | The cause of **allergic** conjunctivitis — pollen, house dust mites, animal dander, fungal allergens (book p.87). Allergy produces papillae, itch and ropy mucus; it does not build a triangular fold of fibrovascular tissue over years. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The association is also the prevention, and it is the one thing you can offer a patient with a small pterygium who does not yet need surgery: **UV-blocking sunglasses and a hat**, plus lubricating drops for irritation. The full management — indications for excision, the one-third recurrence after bare-sclera excision, the 5 % after a conjunctival autograft — is written once at `opqb-t6-249`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Pterygium is associated with ultraviolet exposure, dust and chronic irritation — a disease of the sunlit interpalpebral zone, commonest nasally; infra-red causes cataract and ionizing radiation causes cataract and retinopathy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q267), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-268', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Conjunctival follicles** are present in the following **except**:',
    options:['Spring catarrh.','Active trachoma.','Viral conjunctivitis.','Chlamydia conjunctivitis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Causes of Conjunctival follicles:**\n1. **Viral conjunctivitis**\n2. Chlamydia conjunctivitis\n3. Hypersensitivity to topical medications (e.g., Brimonidine)\n**Causes of Conjunctival papillae:**\n1. Allergic conjunctivitis especially in vernal catarrh\n2. Prolonged contact lens wear\n3. Foreign body (as in artificial eye users)\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. It is a near-exact reprint of book p.86, omitting only the tutorial-marked fourth follicle cause, \"Folliculosis in children\". The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Spring catarrh produces PAPILLAE, and the box the bank prints under this question is the whole answer: vernal catarrh heads the papilla list and appears nowhere on the follicle list.** This is the topic's anchor entry for the follicle-versus-papilla distinction; a dozen other questions in this topic turn on it.\n\n**The structural difference, which is what makes the lists behave as they do** (book p.86):\n\n| | **Follicle** | **Papilla** |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it is made of | \"leucocytic infiltration (mainly **lymphocytes**)\" — a lymphoid aggregate | \"a **fibrovascular core**… surrounded by hyperplastic conjunctival epithelium\" |\n| Vessels | Run **over and around** it; the top is pale | Run **up into** the centre; `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 14: \"red at its centre — a central vessel core\" |\n| Shape | Discrete, **rounded**, smooth-domed, even-sized | **Flat-topped**, polygonal, mosaic, separated by pale clefts |\n| Where | Loose conjunctiva — maximal **towards the fornix** | **Tarsal plate only**, where the conjunctiva is bound down |\n| \"Giant\" | — | **>1 mm** diameter, \"**Cobble stone appearance**\" |\n| Means | Viral, chlamydial, drug hypersensitivity | Allergy, chronic mechanical irritation, bacterial disease |\n\nBook p.86 adds that ordinary papillae \"develop with **bacterial conjunctivitis** and **chronic blepharitis**\", which is why book p.94's grid puts *Papillae* in the bacterial column too — the **giant** form is what needs the prolonged irritation of the box's three causes.\n\n**Why the other three do produce follicles:**\n\n| Option | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Active trachoma** | Book p.92: \"**Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis** associated with mucopurulent discharge\"; WHO grade **TF** = 5 or more follicles over 0.5 mm on the superior tarsal plate. `L6` slide 25 photographs it. |\n| **Viral conjunctivitis** | Book p.90: \"Conjunctival **follicles** (mainly in the **lower** tarsal conjunctiva)\". Bold and underlined in the bank's own box. `L6` slide 34. |\n| **Chlamydia conjunctivitis** | Book p.91, adult inclusion conjunctivitis: \"Conjunctival **follicles** (**upper and lower** tarsal)\" with tender preauricular nodes and mucopurulent discharge. |\n\n**And note the third box entry, which is easy to skip:** **hypersensitivity to topical medications**, with **Brimonidine** as the example. A glaucoma patient on long-term drops who develops a follicular conjunctivitis is not necessarily infected — the drops may be the cause, and book p.84 lists topical medication under the **toxic** aetiology as well.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Evert the lid, and ask two questions of what you see: **is the top of the bump pale and round, or flat and red-centred?** and **does it reach the fornix, or stop at the tarsal edge?** Round, pale, reaching the fornix = follicle = viral, chlamydial or drug. Flat-topped, red-cored, stopping at the tarsal edge = papilla = allergy or chronic irritation. Everything else in this topic follows from that.",
    objective:'Follicles are lymphoid, pale-topped, fornix-maximal and mean viral, chlamydial or drug hypersensitivity; papillae are fibrovascular, flat-topped, tarsus-limited and mean allergy or chronic irritation — spring catarrh gives papillae, not follicles.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.38 (Q268), key p.42 (explanation box printed)' },

  /* opqb-t6-269 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     An EXACT within-topic reprint of `opqb-t6-234` (book p.34): same stem, same
     four options in the same order, same key. The only differences in the whole
     item are a hyphen in "18-year-old" and a full stop on option D.
     A within-bank reprint takes NO `alsoIn` -- held once. Neither printing is
     boxed, so the held entry keeps its authored marker. Its explanation now
     records both pages. */
  { id:'opqb-t6-270', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A child 5 years old presented with bilateral red eyes. His mother mentioned that **his lids were stuck together on waking up in the morning**. On examination, vision was **6/6** and the lashes were glued together. The most reliable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Spring catarrh.','Active trachoma.','Muco-purulent conjunctivitis.','Viral conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**One symptom carries this whole question, and the book prints it as a heading.** Book p.91, symptoms of acute bacterial conjunctivitis: \"**Matting of the eyelids** and lashes **on awakening** with difficulty in opening the eye.\" Overnight the lids stay closed for hours, tear turnover falls, and the mucopus dries into a crust that gums the lashes together — so the sign is specific to a discharge that is thick enough to dry, which means mucopurulent or purulent, which means bacterial.\n\nThe stem then confirms it twice: the mother's history (*stuck together on waking*) and the examination (*the lashes were glued together*). And **6/6** does the negative work — book p.94's five findings that keep a red eye conjunctival are \"**Normal VA**, Normal pupil, No photophobia, **Clear cornea**, Normal AC\".\n\n**The same discriminator is keyed at `opqb-t6-254`**, where the bank asks which feature *confirms* mucopurulent conjunctivitis and answers \"glued eye upon wakening\" — explicitly rejecting \"glued eye at the end of the day\". By evening the eye is sticky; by morning it is sealed.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What the history would have been |\n|---|---|\n| **Spring catarrh** | **Intense itching**, seasonal recurrence in hot weather, ropy mucus that can be drawn out in a strand, and giant cobblestone papillae on the upper tarsus. Ropy mucus does not dry into a crust that glues lashes. Book p.94's grid grades allergic itching *intense* against *mild* in bacterial disease, and this mother reports no itching at all. |\n| **Active trachoma** | Chronic, not overnight: upper tarsal follicles with mucopurulent discharge in a child of an endemic community, evolving over months. It genuinely does discharge — but \"the most reliable diagnosis\" for a two-day sticky eye in a 6/6 child is the acute one. |\n| **Viral conjunctivitis** | **Watery**, with lower tarsal follicles and a tender preauricular node; often one eye first. A watery eye may crust faintly at the lid margin but it does not glue the lashes shut. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Two history questions separate the three great causes before the child is even examined: **does it itch?** and **were the lids stuck this morning?** Intense itch → allergic. Lids stuck → bacterial. Neither, with watering and a sore throat → viral. Book p.94's grid is that pair plus the discharge, and `opqb-t6-229` sets it out in full. Treatment here is topical antibiotic, strict hygiene, **no patch** and no shared towels.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Lids and lashes glued together on waking, with normal vision, is mucopurulent bacterial conjunctivitis — the discharge is thick enough to dry overnight, which watery viral and ropy allergic discharge are not.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q270), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-271', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A school teacher has noticed that one of her students was **frequently rubbing his eyes**. On examination, there was conjunctival injection and **gelatinous masses on the limbus**. This boy most probably suffers from:',
    options:['Phlycterular conjunctivitis.','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis.','Bulbar spring catarrh.','Purulent conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Gelatinous masses on the limbus in a schoolboy who cannot stop rubbing his eyes is the limbal — \"bulbar\" — form of vernal keratoconjunctivitis.** The full VKC picture and signs table are written once at `opqb-t6-224`, and the bulbar-versus-palpebral distinction at `opqb-t6-228`; this entry covers what this vignette adds.\n\n**The rubbing is not incidental — it is the sign the book singles out.** Book p.89 opens the treatment of VKC with it: \"Instruct the patient to **avoid eye rubbing** as much as possible (result in **mast cell degranulation** and leads to a vicious circle of more itching and eye rubbing).\" So rubbing is simultaneously the symptom, the mechanism of worsening, and the first thing treatment must stop. It has a second consequence the same page records: \"**Keratoconus** is more common in VKC patients as well; **possibly due to increased eye rubbing** of chronically irritated patients\" — which is why `opqb-t6-264` puts keratoconus with allergy rather than with pterygium.\n\n**What the gelatinous masses are.** Book p.88: \"**Limbal papillae** with whitish dots (**Tranta spots**) that consist of degenerated **eosinophils**\", with the warning that \"Limbal disease can result in a **limbal stem cell deficiency** which can lead to *corneal neovascularization*\". `L6) Conjunctiva` photographs them four times without naming them once — slide 39, both eyes with \"raised, gelatinous, greyish-pink thickened swellings carrying small discrete WHITE DOTS\", an arrow printed on the picture and no caption; slide 41, the band \"thick, opaque, creamy yellow-white… overhangs the corneal edge\" with haze spreading down onto the cornea; slide 42, a **360°** \"creamy tan-yellow GELATINOUS COLLAR\" encircling the cornea. Measured over all 66 slides, *Trantas* = **zero hits**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What would be on the limbus instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Phlyctenular conjunctivitis** | A **single small raised nodule** with a leash of dilated vessels converging on it and white conjunctiva elsewhere (`L6` slides 46–48) — one lesion with one vessel bundle, not a gelatinous band. It is not itchy, and the redness is sectoral rather than diffuse. *(\"Phlycterular\" is the bank's misprint, kept.)* |\n| **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | Diffuse injection with **mucopurulent discharge** and lids matted on waking (`opqb-t6-270`); papillae are fine and tarsal, and the limbus is normal. Itch is mild. |\n| **Purulent conjunctivitis** | Frank pus, gross lid oedema, an acutely ill eye and a real risk of corneal perforation (`L6` slides 18–21). Nothing here is acute or purulent, and this is a well child at school. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The bulbar form is the one that costs vision, because the disease sits on the **limbus** — where the stem cells that resurface the cornea live. A child with a 360° gelatinous collar and Trantas dots needs the itch controlled properly, not intermittently: cold compresses and dark glasses, a mast cell stabilizer as maintenance, an antihistamine for breakthrough, short steroid courses for attacks, and cyclosporine or tacrolimus if the steroid cannot be stopped (`opqb-t6-287`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Gelatinous limbal masses in a child who constantly rubs his eyes is bulbar (limbal) spring catarrh; the rubbing degranulates mast cells, drives the vicious circle, and is itself associated with keratoconus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q271), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-272', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Cobble stone papillae** are pathognomonic of:',
    options:['Trachoma.','Inclusion blennorrhea.','Palpebral spring catarrh.','Adenoviral conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**\"Cobblestone\" is the book's own word for the giant papillae of vernal disease, and it is defined by shape, size and site.** Book p.86: \"**Giant papillae (Cobble stone appearance i.e., they are flat-topped)** are those **more than 1mm in diameter**. They develop with prolonged inflammation and irritation as in: 1. **Allergic conjunctivitis especially in vernal catarrh** 2. Prolonged contact lens wear 3. Foreign body (as in artificial eye users).\" Book p.88's VKC conjunctival-signs row prints \"Tarsal **giant papillae**\", illustrated by two photographs captioned \"Cobblestone papillae\", and book p.94's grid gives the allergic column the sign \"Papillae (**cobblestone**)\".\n\n`L6) Conjunctiva` slide 38, titled \"Vernal KeratoConjunctivitis\", photographs them: \"LARGE, FLAT-TOPPED, POLYGONAL GIANT PAPILLAE packed edge to edge in a **cobblestone mosaic**, each several times the size of an ordinary papilla, separated by pale clefts, on a deep red background\". Slide 44 arrows three of them individually.\n\n**Why the site is in the answer.** The option says *palpebral* spring catarrh, and that is not padding — it is the half of the disease where papillae occur. Papillae are confined to the tarsal plate because book p.83 makes the palpebral conjunctiva \"firmly adherent to the tarsal plates\"; the **bulbar (limbal)** form of the same disease produces gelatinous limbal masses with Trantas dots instead (`opqb-t6-228`, `opqb-t6-271`). Same disease, two distributions, two different signs.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — all three are follicular diseases:**\n\n| Option | What its tarsus shows |\n|---|---|\n| **Trachoma** | **Upper tarsal follicles** in the active stage; a papillary reaction \"can also be present\" (book p.92) but it is fine and velvety, not giant, and the disease is defined by its follicles. |\n| **Inclusion blennorrhea** | The chlamydial D→K disease — book ch.16 names \"**Inclusion blennorrhoea**\" as an alternative name for neonatal chlamydial infection, and book p.91 gives adult inclusion conjunctivitis \"Conjunctival **follicles** (upper and lower tarsal)\" with \"Conjunctival **papillae in chronic cases**\". Chronic papillae, again, are not giant cobblestones. |\n| **Adenoviral conjunctivitis** | \"Conjunctival **follicles** (mainly in the lower tarsal conjunctiva)\" (book p.90). Papillae are not part of the viral picture in book p.94's grid at all. |\n\n**⚠️ On the word \"pathognomonic\".** Strictly it overstates: the book's own list gives contact lens wear and a foreign body as the other two causes of giant papillae, and this bank keys the lens form twice, printing `opqb-t6-234` on both p.34 and p.38. Among the four options offered, however, only one produces them, so C is the answer. The bank spells the word correctly here and misspells it \"pathogenomic\" at `opqb-t6-252`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Cobblestone = giant papillae, flat-topped and over 1 mm, on the TARSAL plate — the palpebral form of spring catarrh; trachoma, chlamydial and adenoviral disease are all follicular.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q272), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-273', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In August 1999 many Egyptian citizens complained of bilateral red watery eyes. On examination, a marked **follicular reaction** was present mainly on the **lower** palpebral conjunctiva, accompanied by **preauricular lymphadenopathy**. The possible diagnosis was:',
    options:['Active trachoma.','Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis.','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis.','Purulent conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**An outbreak, watery eyes, lower tarsal follicles and preauricular nodes is adenoviral epidemic keratoconjunctivitis — and the word doing most of the work is *epidemic*.**\n\n**Book p.90 supplies every element.** Adenovirus is \"the most frequent causative agent (**90 % of cases**)\" of viral conjunctivitis; \"It is **highly contagious** and may appear as **epidemics**\"; it is \"Transmitted by contact with respiratory or ocular secretions\". Its signs are conjunctival injection, \"Conjunctival **follicles** (mainly in the **lower** tarsal conjunctiva)\", possible pseudomembranes, \"**Preauricular lymphadenopathy**\", lid oedema, and keratitis \"In the form of subepithelial infiltrates, punctate epithelial keratitis\". **Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis** specifically is \"Caused by adenovirus serovars **8, 19 and 37**\" and is \"**The most severe** ocular adenoviral infection\", in which \"Keratitis and consequently **photophobia** are marked\" and the \"Cornea may become chronically affected\".\n\n**Follicle position again.** *Lower* palpebral is the discriminator against trachoma, whose follicles are on the **upper** tarsus, and against chlamydial inclusion conjunctivitis, whose follicles are on **both** (book pp.91–92). The full position table is at `opqb-t6-226`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Active trachoma** | Chronic and endemic rather than epidemic — a disease of \"poverty, overcrowding and poor hygiene\" with \"**re-infection cycles**\" (book p.92), not a sudden August outbreak. **Upper** tarsal follicles, mucopurulent discharge, no preauricular node in the book's description. |\n| **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | Bacterial: **papillae** not follicles, **mucopurulent** discharge not watering, lids matted on waking. Book p.94's grid restricts \"Follicles + preauricular lymphadenopathy\" to the viral column. |\n| **Purulent conjunctivitis** | Frank pus, gross lid oedema, systemic upset, corneal ulceration — gonococcal until proved otherwise (book p.85's N.B.). Not a community-wide epidemic of red watery eyes. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line — and this is the part that matters in an outbreak.** Book p.90's treatment is: \"**Spontaneous resolution**… within **2-3 weeks** with no specific treatment necessary\", \"**Strict hygienic measures to restrict transmission**\", topical steroids with gradual tapering only for severe symptoms, topical antibiotics only if secondary bacterial infection is suspected, and antihistamines/vasoconstrictors for itch. There is **no effective antiviral** for adenovirus — `opqb-t6-251` keys that too. What stops an epidemic is hand hygiene, separate towels, no sharing of eye drops, and keeping infectious patients out of crowded waiting rooms.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An epidemic of bilateral watery red eyes with LOWER tarsal follicles and preauricular nodes is adenoviral epidemic keratoconjunctivitis (serovars 8, 19, 37) — self-limiting in 2–3 weeks, controlled by hygiene, not by drugs.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q273), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-274', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'An important complication of **trachoma** is **dry eye** due to:',
    options:['Fibrosis of the palpebral conjunctiva affecting the Meibomian glands.','Healing of the pannus affects the Goblet cells.','Fibrosis of the upper fornix affects the accessory lacrimal glands.','Atrophy of the main lacrimal gland.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The accessory lacrimal glands live in the fornix, and trachoma scars the fornix — so the fibrosis destroys the glands and the ductules that drain them, and the aqueous layer fails.**\n\n**Where the glands are.** Book ch.6's own anatomy figure on p.83 carries a key whose first entry is \"**Accessory lacrimal glands: Glands of Krause, Glands of Wolfring**\", drawn in the region of the fornix and upper tarsal border, and book ch.4 groups them with the main gland as the source of the **aqueous** layer of the tear film. Book ch.6 notes that this is figure-only teaching — the two glands \"exist ONLY in this key\" and are not in the prose.\n\n**What trachoma does to them.** Book p.92: \"**Scarring is most prominent on the upper tarsal plate**\", progressing from linear and stellate scars to the broad confluent **Arlt's line**. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 33 shows the end point — a tarsal conjunctiva \"almost entirely replaced by **DENSE WHITE SCAR**\". Contracting scar in the upper fornix obliterates the glands themselves and, just as importantly, the fine ductules that carry their secretion to the surface: a gland whose duct is scarred shut is a gland that has stopped working. The result is an **aqueous-deficient** dry eye superimposed on an eye that already has trichiasis and corneal opacity.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What is wrong with it |\n|---|---|\n| **Fibrosis of the palpebral conjunctiva affecting the Meibomian glands** | The best distractor, because it is *partly* right: meibomian damage does occur in a scarred lid and it would cause **evaporative** dry eye by removing the lipid layer. But the meibomian glands lie **within the tarsal plate**, not in the conjunctiva overlying it, and the classical trachomatous mechanism taught for dry eye is loss of the aqueous-secreting accessory glands. |\n| **Healing of the pannus affects the Goblet cells** | Goblet cells are genuinely relevant — book p.83's histology puts them in the conjunctival epithelium, and book ch.4 makes them the source of the **mucin** layer, so conjunctival scarring does reduce them. But that is scarring of the conjunctiva at large, not \"healing of the pannus\": a pannus is a **corneal** vascular sheet advancing from the superior limbus (`L6` slide 28), and the cornea has no goblet cells to lose. |\n| **Atrophy of the main lacrimal gland** | The main gland sits in the **lacrimal fossa in the upper temporal orbit**, outside the conjunctival sac entirely. Trachoma is a disease of the conjunctival surface and never reaches it. |\n\n**⚠️ Neither conjunctiva source teaches this complication.** Book ch.6's own not-covered list records \"Complications of cicatricial trachoma beyond those listed (**dry eye from ductule destruction**, corneal ulceration, superinfection): absent\", and its p.93 list stops at trichiasis, corneal vascularization, cicatricial entropion and severe corneal opacification. `L6) Conjunctiva` returns **zero** for *dry eye*, *sicca* and *Sjögren* across all 66 slides. *The mechanism above is therefore **not taken from the conjunctiva material** — the glands' location is from book ch.6's figure key and book ch.4, and the ductule-destruction step is standard, uncontested teaching that both sources record as absent.*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Cicatricial trachoma injures the ocular surface three ways at once — **lashes rubbing it** (trichiasis), **the tear film failing** (accessory gland loss), and **the cornea already scarred** (pannus and opacification). That combination is why the corneal damage progresses even after the infection has long gone, and why the **S** and **E** of SAFE matter more than any late prescription.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Trachoma causes dry eye by scarring the upper fornix and destroying the accessory lacrimal glands of Krause and Wolfring and their ductules — the aqueous layer, not the main gland and not the pannus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q274), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-275', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A boy 11 years old developed **acute follicular** conjunctivitis. The causative organism may be one of the following **except**:',
    options:['Goncocci.','Adenovirus.','Herpes simplex.','Inclusion blenorrhoea.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Follicles are lymphoid, and gonococcus does not produce them — it produces a torrential purulent, papillary conjunctivitis.** Book p.86 lists the causes of conjunctival follicles and there is no pyogenic organism among them:\n\n1. **Viral conjunctivitis**\n2. **Chlamydia conjunctivitis (trachoma)**\n3. **Hypersensitivity to topical medications** (e.g., Brimonidine)\n4. *Folliculosis in children* (tutorial-marked)\n\nThis bank reprints the first three as its own box at `opqb-t6-268`. Meanwhile book p.91 puts \"*Conjunctival **papillae***\" first among the signs of acute bacterial conjunctivitis, and book p.94's grid gives the bacterial column *Papillae* and the viral column *Follicles + preauricular lymphadenopathy*. So the follicle/papilla split is also, in practice, a viral-and-chlamydial versus bacterial split.\n\n**Why gonococcus in particular could not be missed at the slit lamp anyway.** Book p.85's N.B.: \"Severe **purulent** discharge is suggestive of **gonococcal** conjunctivitis\", and book p.91 attaches **corneal ulceration** and pre-auricular and submandibular lymphadenopathy to gonococcal and meningococcal infection, requiring **systemic** antibiotics. `L6) Conjunctiva` slides 18–21 photograph the purulent picture and its blinding end result. That is not an \"acute follicular conjunctivitis\".\n\n**Why the other three do cause follicular conjunctivitis:**\n\n| Option | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Adenovirus** | Book p.90 — the cause of 90 % of viral conjunctivitis, with follicles mainly in the lower tarsal conjunctiva; and \"Non-specific acute follicular conjunctivitis\" is named as \"**The most common** form\". |\n| **Herpes simplex** | Book p.90 lists \"**Herpes simplex virus follicular conjunctivitis** (blepharoconjunctivitis)\" as one of the five viral presentations. Keyed again at `opqb-t6-276`. |\n| **Inclusion blenorrhoea** | The chlamydial **D→K** disease — book ch.16 names \"**Inclusion blennorrhoea**\" as an alternative name for it, and book p.91 gives adult chlamydial (inclusion) conjunctivitis \"Conjunctival **follicles** (upper and lower tarsal)\" with tender preauricular nodes. ⚠️ Note the category slip: this option names a **disease**, not an organism, under a stem that asks for an organism. The bank's own doing, kept. |\n\n**⚠️ And note the two spellings.** This bank prints \"Inclusion **blenorrhoea**\" here and \"Inclusion **blennorrhea**\" at `opqb-t6-272` — the same entity, four pages apart.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a child with an acute follicular conjunctivitis, the working diagnosis is **viral**, the treatment is hygiene and time, and the two things that change the plan are **frank pus** (swab urgently, think gonococcal, add systemic antibiotics) and **a lesion on the cornea** (stain it — see `opqb-t6-278`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Follicular conjunctivitis is viral, chlamydial or drug-induced; gonococcus produces a hyperacute purulent, papillary conjunctivitis with corneal ulceration and never a follicular one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q275), key p.42' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-276', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In the treatment of **herpes simplex conjunctivitis** we may get benefit from:',
    options:['Hot fomentations.','Cold compresses.','Lead acetate fomentations.','Boric acid lotion.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Cold compresses are the material's standard supportive measure for a viral conjunctivitis, and heat is nowhere in it.** Book p.94's viral column gives the treatment as \"**Cold compresses** · Artificial tears · ± Short course of topical steroid provides symptomatic relief\"; book p.87 gives \"**Cold compresses to relieve itching**\" for allergic disease, and book p.89 gives \"Dark glasses and **cold compresses**\" for VKC. Cold appears **three times** in the chapter as a treatment; **hot** appears **never** — this bank keys the same point as the exception at `opqb-t6-253`.\n\nCold is doing three things: vasoconstricting the injected conjunctiva, reducing lid oedema, and numbing the surface. Heat would increase the hyperaemia and the oedema, and in an infective conjunctivitis it does nothing useful at all.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Hot fomentations** | Warm compresses have a real place in ophthalmology — in **blepharitis and meibomian gland dysfunction**, where they soften inspissated meibum, and on a **stye or chalazion**. They are wrong for an acutely inflamed viral conjunctiva. *(Book ch.5 prescribes \"warm compresses and lid scrubs\" for blepharitis; this is the only warm-compress indication in the cached ophthalmology material.)* |\n| **Lead acetate fomentations** | Obsolete. Lead acetate lotion was a traditional astringent, and it is **contraindicated where the corneal epithelium is broken**, because lead precipitates in the stroma as a permanent white *lead deposit*. Since herpes simplex disease is precisely the one that ulcerates the epithelium, this is the worst option on the list. ⚠️ *Lead acetate returns **zero hits** across all 36 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 cached book chapters — **not taken from the course material**; it is standard historical pharmacology, given because the bank offers it.* |\n| **Boric acid lotion** | A mild antiseptic irrigant, and not actually harmful — this bank keys it as a **true** statement in the treatment of mucopurulent conjunctivitis at `opqb-t6-238`. It washes discharge away, which matters in a bacterial conjunctivitis; in a watery viral one there is little discharge to wash. It is a lesser answer, not a wrong principle. ⚠️ *boric* appears in the whole cached ophthalmology corpus only in `L26) Trauma II`. |\n\n**⚠️ What the question does NOT ask, and should have.** The specific treatment of herpes simplex ocular disease is a **topical antiviral** — and it is absent from both conjunctiva sources: book ch.6 names *no* antiviral anywhere, listing herpes simplex follicular conjunctivitis among the viral presentations and giving it no treatment of its own, and `L6) Conjunctiva` contains no pharmacology at all (*acyclovir / aciclovir* = 0 across its 66 slides). *That herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis is treated with topical aciclovir or ganciclovir, and that **topical steroid is dangerous** in the presence of a dendritic ulcer, is **not taken from the course material** — but it is the single most important safety point attached to this diagnosis, and book p.94's own viral column offers a \"short course of topical steroid\", which would be exactly the wrong thing here.*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Before comforting a red eye after a viral illness, **stain the cornea**. `opqb-t6-278` keys precisely that, and a dendritic ulcer changes the treatment completely — antiviral yes, steroid no.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Cold compresses are the supportive treatment of viral conjunctivitis — the book prints cold three times and hot never; lead acetate is contraindicated where the epithelium is broken, and herpes simplex needs a topical antiviral, not a steroid.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.39 (Q276), key p.42' },

  /* opqb-t6-277 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     A within-topic reprint of `opqb-t6-229` (book p.33). Same stem, same key,
     and NO OPTION IS REPLACED -- the four differ only in spelling and stops:
     "muco-purulent"/"muco purulent" and "Mucoid ropy white"/"Mucous ropy white".
     Mucoid and mucous name the same discharge, so this is a respelling, not a
     new question. A replaced distractor makes a new entry; a respelled one does
     not. Held once, no `alsoIn`, marker unaffected. */
  { id:'opqb-t6-278', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'After an attack of **influenza**, a patient came complaining of **stitching pain** in one eye with watering. A few days later he developed **photophobia**. To reach the correct diagnosis, choose one of the following:',
    options:['Conjunctival smear for culture.','Fluorescein staining of the cornea.','Measuring the ocular tension.','Syringing of the lacrimal system.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Post-viral keratitis is suggested:\nPain, photophobia, watering\nUsually epithelial involvement\n**Fluorescein staining** is the key investigation to **detect corneal epithelial defects (e.g., dendritic** lesions in herpes simplex keratitis, or superficial punctate keratitis post-viral).\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Three symptoms in this stem point away from the conjunctiva and towards the cornea, and there is one test that settles it in ten seconds.**\n\n| Symptom | Why it is not conjunctival |\n|---|---|\n| **Stitching pain** | Book p.94 defines conjunctivitis by five negatives, and \"**No photophobia**\" is one of them; conjunctivitis gives **grittiness**, book p.85's item 1 — \"Foreign body sensation with grittiness or feeling of sand in the eye\". Sharp, stabbing pain means the corneal nerves are exposed. |\n| **Unilateral** | Book p.91 records bacterial conjunctivitis as initially unilateral, so this is weak on its own — but it fits a keratitis better than an epidemic. |\n| **Photophobia** | The corneal reflex sign. Book p.90 makes it \"marked\" in epidemic keratoconjunctivitis precisely *because* of the keratitis; book p.94 excludes it from simple conjunctivitis. |\n\nBook p.94's other four negatives are the ones to check alongside it — **normal VA, normal pupil, clear cornea, normal AC** — and a fluorescein drop is what turns \"clear cornea\" from an impression into an observation.\n\n**What fluorescein shows.** It pools in any area where the epithelium is missing and fluoresces bright green under cobalt blue light. Two patterns matter here, and the box names both: a **dendritic** ulcer — a branching, linear defect with terminal end-bulbs, which is **herpes simplex** — and **superficial punctate keratitis**, a scatter of fine dots seen after viral illnesses and in dry eye. That distinction changes the treatment completely, because a dendritic ulcer needs a **topical antiviral** and must **not** be given a topical steroid, whereas book p.94's viral column otherwise offers \"± short course of topical steroid\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | When it would be right |\n|---|---|\n| **Conjunctival smear for culture** | Book p.91 reserves swabs for urgent **Gram staining** \"to exclude gonococcal and meningococcal infections\", with culture \"(if possible)\" — i.e. for a **purulent** eye. This eye is watering, not discharging, and a culture takes days while the fluorescein takes seconds. |\n| **Measuring the ocular tension** | The test for **acute angle-closure glaucoma**, which also gives a painful red eye with photophobia — but with a hazy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, haloes and markedly reduced vision, and typically severe pain with nausea rather than a stitching sensation. Worth remembering as the differential; not the first test here. |\n| **Syringing of the lacrimal system** | Localises a **drainage** obstruction in a watering eye. But this patient's watering is **reflex**, driven by a painful ocular surface, not by a blocked duct — and a blocked duct causes epiphora without pain or photophobia. |\n\n**⚠️ Fluorescein is barely in the conjunctiva material.** Book ch.6's own not-covered list records \"Fluorescein staining and slit-lamp technique: **absent** (one unlabelled fluorescein image on printed 89)\", and `L6) Conjunctiva` mentions it once only as a describer's reading of a green-stained ulcer on slide 11. The bank's box is doing the teaching here, and the cornea deck is where the technique belongs.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Cornea — the lesion being sought is corneal, but the question is printed in the conjunctiva topic and its differential is the red-eye one.)*",
    objective:'Stitching pain and photophobia after a viral illness mean the cornea, not the conjunctiva — stain with fluorescein to find a dendritic ulcer or punctate keratitis, because a dendrite forbids the steroid a conjunctivitis would tolerate.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q278), key p.43 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-279', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'**Keratoconjunctivitis** includes the following **except**:',
    options:['Spring catarrh.','Trachoma.','Phlycten.','Ptrygium.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A keratoconjunctivitis is an inflammation involving both the cornea and the conjunctiva. A pterygium is a degeneration, not an inflammation, and that is the whole distinction.**\n\n**Book p.94 files it accordingly**, under the banner \"**Conjunctival Degenerations**\", alongside pinguecula — not under conjunctivitis. Its definition contains no inflammatory word at all: \"**Fibrovascular, triangular, wing-like encroachment** of epithelial tissue onto the cornea\" (p.95). The book's objectives flowchart on p.82 draws the same line, giving \"conjunctival degeneration\" its own branch separate from \"Conjunctivitis\". A pterygium *reaches* the cornea, certainly — that is its defining feature, glossed in the book's own Arabic marginal note as \"it comes onto the cornea\" — but reaching is not inflaming. Book p.95 does allow that a pterygium may be **chronically inflamed**, and lists chronic inflammation among its indications for excision; that is a complication, not the entity.\n\n**Why the other three earn the name:**\n\n| Option | The conjunctival part | The corneal part |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Spring catarrh** | Tarsal giant papillae; limbal papillae with Tranta spots | Book p.89's own corneal-signs row: **punctate epithelial erosions**, **shield ulcer** in the upper 1/3, **pseudo-gerontoxon**, keratoconus. The book's heading for the disease is literally \"**Vernal Keratoconjunctivitis (VKC)**\". |\n| **Trachoma** | Upper tarsal follicles → scarring, Arlt's line | Book p.92: \"**Superior epithelial keratitis**\" and \"**pannus** formation (superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration)\", then \"Severe corneal opacification\". `L6` slides 28–29 photograph pannus and the nebula it leaves. |\n| **Phlycten** | A nodule on the bulbar conjunctiva with a sectoral vessel leash | The lesion migrates onto the cornea — the lecturer's own hand-drawn diagram on `L6` slide 49, captioned in his handwriting \"**Phlyctenular K.C.**\", draws \"elongated green-marked (i.e. stained / ulcerated) lesions **straddling and creeping over the limbus**, each led by its own vessel leash\". The abbreviation *K.C.* is the lecturer writing *keratoconjunctivitis*. |\n\n**⚠️ On the option spellings:** the bank prints \"**Ptrygium**\" for *pterygium*, and the stem splits \"Kerato conjunctivitis\" into two words. Both kept as printed.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The distinction is not academic — it decides what you look for and what you offer. Where the disease is a **kerato**conjunctivitis you must examine the cornea at every visit, because that is where the sight is lost: the shield ulcer of VKC, the pannus of trachoma, the migrating phlycten. Where it is a **degeneration**, the questions are different — is the visual axis threatened, is astigmatism being induced, and is excision with an autograft warranted (`opqb-t6-249`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Pterygium is a conjunctival DEGENERATION, filed by the book under degenerations, not a keratoconjunctivitis; spring catarrh, trachoma and phlyctenulosis all inflame the cornea as well as the conjunctiva.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q279), key p.43' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-280', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A 10-year-old boy complains of recurrent attacks of lacrimation, photophobia, **extreme itching** and **ropy white discharge**. On eversion of his eye lids the palpebral conjunctiva may show:',
    options:['Pink vascular tapering papillae.','Violet cobble stone papillae.','True membrane bleeds on removal.','Generalized follicular reaction.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Extreme itching plus ropy discharge in a ten-year-old is vernal keratoconjunctivitis, and what an everted lid shows in that disease is giant flat-topped cobblestone papillae.**\n\n**The symptoms are the book's own, almost word for word.** Book p.88's VKC symptom list, headed by the quotation \" The morning Misery (1988 by Buckley, R.J.) \": \"**Severe itching · Photophobia · Foreign body sensation · Mucous 'ropy' discharge**\", typically bilateral and seasonally recurrent. The age fits exactly — \"the majority of VKC occurs in patients between the **ages of 5-25 years old** with an average age of onset between **10-12 years old**\". Book p.94's grid grades allergic itching **intense** against *mild* for both viral and bacterial disease, and gives allergic discharge as **mucoid (ropy)**.\n\n**And the sign.** Book p.88's conjunctival-signs row: \"Diffuse conjunctival injection and **Tarsal giant papillae**\", illustrated by photographs captioned \"Cobblestone papillae\". Book p.86 defines them: \"**Giant papillae (Cobble stone appearance i.e., they are flat-topped)** are those **more than 1mm in diameter**.\" `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 38 photographs them \"packed edge to edge in a cobblestone mosaic… separated by pale clefts, on a deep red background\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Pink vascular tapering papillae** | Ordinary, non-giant papillae — fine and velvety, **tapering** rather than flat-topped, which book p.86 attributes to \"*bacterial conjunctivitis* and *chronic blepharitis*\". The size and the shape are both wrong for VKC: the whole point of a giant papilla is that it is **flat-topped**, its apex planed off by the opposing globe. |\n| **True membrane bleeds on removal** | The **diphtheritic** sign — book p.85's membrane table, and `opqb-t6-237`. A membrane is a sheet of necrotic tissue infiltrating the epithelium, not a bumpy tarsal surface, and it has nothing to do with itching. |\n| **Generalized follicular reaction** | **Viral or chlamydial** disease. Follicles are lymphoid, pale, rounded and maximal towards the fornix; vernal disease produces papillae, which the bank keys explicitly at `opqb-t6-268`. |\n\n**⚠️ On the word \"violet\".** No cached source assigns a **colour** to the papillae of spring catarrh — neither book ch.6 nor `L6) Conjunctiva`, whose slide 38 describes \"a deep red background\" with pale clefts. *That giant papillae may look violet or dusky in a heavily congested tarsus is **not taken from the course material**; the word appears in this bank only, and it is the option's shape and size — flat-topped, cobblestone — that make it right, not its colour.*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Two words in a child's history make the diagnosis before the lid is everted: **itching** and **ropy**. Then evert the lid to confirm it and to look for the corneal signs that decide urgency — punctate erosions, and the **shield ulcer** of book p.89, \"an oval ulcer typically in the **upper 1/3 of the cornea**… usually covered by a plaque made of fibrin and mucous\", which `opqb-t6-286` describes without naming.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Recurrent itching with ropy discharge in a child shows giant FLAT-TOPPED cobblestone papillae on the everted lid — tapering papillae are bacterial or blepharitic, and follicles are viral or chlamydial.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q280), key p.43' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-281', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A 24-year-old soldier complains of a triangular membrane encroaching from his **nasal** conjunctiva onto the cornea **just beyond the limbus**. His visual acuity is **6/12, correctable with −1.00 cylinder to 6/6**. The best management is:',
    options:['Surgical excision.','Surgical excision and irradiation.','Regular follow up.','Glasses and follow up.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**This pterygium has crossed the limbus but not the visual axis, and its whole visual cost is a −1.00 cylinder that a spectacle lens abolishes. Correct the astigmatism, watch the lesion, and do not operate yet.**\n\n**Why not surgery, on the book's own criteria.** Book p.95 prints four **indications for surgery**: chronic inflammation · threat to the visual axis · induced astigmatism · cosmesis. This patient meets none of them convincingly — nothing in the stem says the eye is inflamed, the lesion is \"just beyond the limbus\" and nowhere near the axis, and the astigmatism, though induced, is **small and fully correctable**. Set against that is the price of operating: \"**One-third recur after excision**\", with recurrences typically more aggressive than the original. A young soldier with decades of sun exposure ahead of him is exactly the patient in whom a recurrence is likely.\n\n**Why *glasses* and not merely *follow up*.** Options C and D differ by one word, and that word is the answer. The patient is functionally 6/12 — enough to matter to a soldier — and a −1.00 cylinder restores him to 6/6 today, at no risk. Choosing bare follow-up leaves a correctable visual deficit uncorrected. Book p.95's own sentence is that a pterygium \"may **decrease vision** if it induces **astigmatism**\"; if the astigmatism is correctable, correct it.\n\n**Why the astigmatism is a cylinder and not a sphere.** The fold pulls on the nasal cornea and flattens the horizontal meridian, so the induced error is **astigmatic**, not myopic. This bank offers \"induced myopia\" as a distractor at `opqb-t6-249` and does not key it. *(The flattening mechanism is **not taken from the course material** — book p.95 names \"induced astigmatism\" without explaining it, and `L6) Conjunctiva` gives pterygium eight slides with no cause, no indication and no optics.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Surgical excision** | Premature, and it is the answer to a different vignette — `opqb-t6-249`, where the lesion is **progressively enlarging** and has already put opacities on the cornea in front of its apex. There the key is excision **with a conjunctival autograft**, which drops recurrence from one-third to about **5 %**. |\n| **Surgical excision and irradiation** | Bare-sclera excision followed by **beta irradiation** is an obsolete adjunct, and its late complication is the reason: scleral necrosis and thinning at the treated site, sometimes years later. Book p.95 offers **conjunctival autograft** or **mitomycin-C** as the recurrence-reducing measures and mentions irradiation nowhere; the bank offers it again at `opqb-t6-284` and does not key it there either. *⚠️ Beta irradiation and its scleral-necrosis risk are **not taken from the course material** — \"beta irradiation\" returns zero across all 66 slides of `L6` and the book names only autograft and mitomycin-C.* |\n| **Regular follow up** | Right about the lesion, wrong about the patient — it leaves him at 6/12 when a lens would make him 6/6. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Follow-up of a pterygium is not passive: **photograph it or measure its extent onto the cornea, and refract it at each visit**. Progression towards the axis, rising astigmatism or persistent inflammation are what convert watching into operating — and when that day comes, the operation is excision with an autograft.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A pterygium short of the visual axis whose only cost is a correctable cylinder is managed with glasses and follow-up — one-third recur after excision, so operate only for the book’s four indications.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q281), key p.43' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-282', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A 16-year-old female complains of a **pigmented nodule near the limbus** that has been present **since childhood**. Recently it has **increased in size**. What would you advise?',
    options:['Regular follow up.','Immediate complete excision.','Colored contact lens.','Corticosteroids therapy.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A pigmented conjunctival lesion present since childhood, in a sixteen-year-old, that has recently enlarged, is almost always a conjunctival naevus doing what naevi do at puberty — and the correct advice is documented observation, not an operation.**\n\n**Why enlargement here is not alarming.** Conjunctival naevi are congenital or appear in the first decade, sit in the interpalpebral bulbar conjunctiva near the limbus, and are **freely mobile over the sclera**. Most are **compound or junctional naevi containing epithelial inclusion cysts**, and two things make them appear to grow around puberty: those cysts enlarge, and the naevus cells respond to hormonal change with increased pigmentation. A naevus that has been present all a patient's life, is mobile, and enlarges gradually in adolescence is behaving typically. Malignant transformation of a conjunctival naevus is rare, and rarer still at sixteen.\n\n**What \"regular follow up\" actually means**, because it is an active answer and not a shrug: **photograph the lesion with a measuring scale**, record its dimensions and its position, and review at intervals. The features that would change the advice to excision biopsy are: rapid growth, **loss of mobility over the sclera** (implying scleral invasion), **prominent feeder vessels**, extension onto the cornea, appearance in the fornix or on the palpebral conjunctiva (naevi there are unusual and suspicious), new pigmentation in a previously amelanotic lesion, or onset in **middle age** rather than childhood.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Immediate complete excision** | The instinct, and it is wrong twice. Excising a benign naevus in a young patient trades a cosmetically minor lesion for a conjunctival scar; and if the lesion were malignant, \"immediate complete excision\" without planning is the wrong operation — melanoma requires wide margins with a no-touch technique and adjunctive treatment, not an unplanned shave. Excision earns its place when the observation above turns suspicious. |\n| **Colored contact lens** | Cosmetic camouflage that treats nothing, hides the lesion from the very surveillance it needs, and — as `opqb-t6-234` keys twice in this same topic, printed on p.34 and again on p.38 — introduces a fresh problem in the form of giant papillary conjunctivitis. |\n| **Corticosteroids therapy** | There is nothing inflammatory here. Steroid would not shrink a naevus, and long-term topical steroid in a sixteen-year-old carries the cataract and pressure risks that `opqb-t6-225` keys. |\n\n**⚠️ THIS IS A GAP IN THE COURSE MATERIAL, AND IT IS COMPLETE. Everything above is *not taken from the course material*.** Measured case-insensitively across all 36 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 cached book chapters, *naevus*, *nevus*, *melanoma* and *melanosis* return **zero hits**. Book ch.6's own not-covered list is explicit: \"Conjunctival tumours of any kind — naevus, melanoma, OSSN, papilloma, lymphoma, Kaposi: **ABSENT ENTIRELY**. The objectives flowchart does not list them.\" Book ch.17, *Malignancies of the Eye and its Adnexa*, is cached and prints no conjunctival naevus either. `L6) Conjunctiva` has one slide that may be showing such a lesion — slide 59 (repeated identically as slide 61), \"a slightly RAISED, BROWNISH-TAN, irregular, coarsely textured patch of thickened conjunctiva\" in the interpalpebral zone — and the deck's own record notes it is \"**unnamed**, and there is no tumour vocabulary anywhere in the deck to attach to it\". The account above is standard, uncontested ocular oncology, given because the bank asks the question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Ocular tumours — the lesion is a conjunctival naevus, but neither the conjunctiva sources nor the malignancy chapter teach it, and the question is printed in the conjunctiva topic.)*",
    objective:'A pigmented limbal nodule present since childhood that enlarges at puberty is a conjunctival naevus with cystic and hormonal change — photograph, measure and review; excise only for loss of mobility, feeder vessels, corneal extension or adult onset.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q282), key p.43' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-283', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A patient who sustained **severe head trauma** presented with a **large triangular subconjunctival hemorrhage coming from the fornix**. The next investigation is:',
    options:['Bleeding time.','Clotting time.','X ray skull','Fluorescein angiography'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The phrase that matters is \"coming from the fornix\" — a subconjunctival haemorrhage with no visible posterior limit, after head trauma, is blood tracking forward from behind the orbital septum, and the classic source is a fracture of the anterior cranial fossa.** The eye is the messenger; the skull is the patient.\n\n**Why the posterior extent is the sign.** A spontaneous subconjunctival haemorrhage begins somewhere on the bulbar conjunctiva and you can see all four of its edges. Blood arising **behind** the orbit tracks forward under the conjunctiva and appears at the fornix with its posterior border **out of sight**, spreading anteriorly — the classical description of the haemorrhage of a basal skull fracture, often accompanied by periorbital bruising that is bilateral and confined by the orbital rim, and by rhinorrhoea, otorrhoea or Battle's sign.\n\n**The material teaches the surrounding logic even though it does not print the fracture.** Book p.95 lists **trauma** first among the associations of subconjunctival haemorrhage and prints the warning sentence in the management block: \"A **large area** of subconjunctival haemorrhage with a history of **trauma** should arouse high suspicion for **eye globe rupture**\", and its tutorial gloss adds \"that is obscured by hemorrhage → refer to ophthalmologust [sic]\". So the book's own reflex in exactly this situation is *suspect something serious behind the blood, and image it* — the bank asks for the skull, the book asks for the globe, and in a real head-injured patient **both** must be excluded. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 65's bottom-right panel photographs the traumatic version, with \"dusky **PURPLE-BROWN BRUISING** of the upper lid and periorbital skin (a black eye) together with a red patch of subconjunctival blood\" — and gives no cause and no investigation.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | When it would be right |\n|---|---|\n| **Bleeding time** · **Clotting time** | These test the two arms of haemostasis, and book p.95 does list \"**bleeding disorders**\" and \"**anticoagulation therapy**\" among the causes of subconjunctival haemorrhage — with a tutorial box advising that an anticoagulated patient's **INR** be checked and a **recurrent** haemorrhage be investigated for a bleeding disorder. But this haemorrhage has an obvious mechanical cause standing in front of it. You do not investigate coagulation in a man who has been hit on the head; you investigate the head. |\n| **Fluorescein angiography** | A **retinal and choroidal** vascular investigation — dye injected intravenously and the fundus photographed in sequence — used for diabetic retinopathy, vein occlusions and choroidal neovascularisation. It says nothing about blood under the conjunctiva, and it is an entirely inappropriate first test in an acute head injury. |\n\n*(The basal-skull-fracture mechanism, the absent posterior limit as its sign, and the accompanying signs are **not taken from the course material**: book ch.6 gives trauma as a cause of subconjunctival haemorrhage without ever mentioning skull fracture, and `L6` returns zero for every cause. They are standard, uncontested trauma teaching, and the bank's key requires them.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Blood under the conjunctiva after head trauma asks two questions, and neither is about the conjunctiva: **is the skull fractured**, and **is the globe ruptured**? A subconjunctival haemorrhage that is bright, flat, sharply bounded and complete is reassuring; one whose back edge you cannot find is not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Trauma — the investigation keyed is a head-injury one, but the presenting sign and the question's context are conjunctival.)*",
    objective:'A subconjunctival haemorrhage arising from the fornix with no visible posterior limit after head trauma means blood tracking forward from a base-of-skull fracture — image the skull, and exclude globe rupture as well.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q283), key p.43' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-284', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A male patient complains of **bilateral small yellow triangular masses on either side of the cornea**. Your decision for management should be:',
    options:['Immediate surgical excision.','Surgical excision followed by B irradiation.','Surgical excision and repair by conjunctival graft.','Artificial tears.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Pingueculum management:**\n - Irritative symptoms may be treate**d with lubricating drops**\n- Surgical excision for **cosmesis only**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, including the book's spelling \"Pingueculum\" and the bank's own bold run beginning mid-word. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Two words in the stem make this a pinguecula and not a pterygium, and the difference decides everything: *yellow*, and *on either side of the cornea*.**\n\n**Book p.94 defines it:** \"**Yellow white subepithelial** deposit of hyaline and elastic tissue adjacent to the **nasal or temporal limbus**, *sparing the cornea*. Associated with **sun and wind exposure, aging**. Benign, sometimes enlarges slowly. May be irritating due to **abnormal tear film distribution**.\" Its treatment is the box above, and nothing more. The Arabic mnemonic the book prints beside the pterygium heading on the next page — تيجي على الكورنيا, \"it comes onto the cornea\" — exists precisely to separate the two.\n\n| | **Pinguecula** | **Pterygium** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Colour | **Yellow-white** | Fleshy pink-red, vascular |\n| Relation to cornea | **Spares** it — stops at the limbus | **Encroaches onto** it |\n| Site | Nasal **or temporal** limbus, often both | Usually **nasal** |\n| Vision | Unaffected | May fall from astigmatism or axis coverage |\n| Treatment | **Lubricating drops**; excision for **cosmesis only** | Excision for four printed indications; autograft to cut recurrence |\n\n`L6) Conjunctiva` slide 58, titled \"Pingueculum\" in the lecturer's own spelling, photographs it: \"a small, **YELLOWISH-CREAM**, slightly RAISED, ill-defined patch… The patch reaches toward the limbus but **STOPS AT IT** and does not encroach on the cornea.\" Slide 56's four-panel figure is the deck's whole teaching on the distinction — two lesions crossing the limbus in the top row against two stopping at it in the bottom row — and it carries \"NO caption, no title and no label naming either\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Immediate surgical excision** | Operating on a benign, asymptomatic, vision-sparing deposit. Book p.94 restricts excision to **cosmesis only**, and \"immediate\" is nowhere in it. |\n| **Surgical excision followed by B irradiation** | Beta irradiation is an obsolete adjunct used to suppress **pterygium** recurrence, and its late complication is scleral necrosis. A pinguecula does not invade the cornea and has no comparable recurrence problem — this is a large intervention for no disease. *Not taken from the course material; the book names only autograft and mitomycin-C, and only for pterygium.* |\n| **Surgical excision and repair by conjunctival graft** | The right operation for the **wrong lesion**. Book p.95 gives the conjunctival **autograft** as the recurrence-reducing repair after **pterygium** excision, dropping recurrence from one-third to \"**only 5 %**\" (`opqb-t6-249`). A pinguecula spares the cornea and does not need it. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The patient's complaint is usually cosmetic or a dry, gritty irritation, and the mechanism is in the book's own sentence — the raised deposit disturbs tear film spreading, so a dry spot forms beside it. **Lubricants, sun protection, and reassurance** are the whole treatment. If a pinguecula ever becomes acutely red and sore, that is *pingueculitis*, and a short topical anti-inflammatory settles it — an addition **not taken from the course material**, which stops at lubricants.",
    objective:'Yellow-white deposits at the nasal and temporal limbus that spare the cornea are pingueculae: lubricating drops for irritation, excision for cosmesis only — the autograft belongs to pterygium.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q284), key p.43 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-285', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Blood vessels in a **trachomatous pannus** lie:',
    options:["Beneath the Descemet's membrane.",'In the substantia propria.',"Between Bowman's membrane & substantia propria.","Between Bowman's membrane & Epithelium."],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A trachomatous pannus is a SUPERFICIAL vascularization, and superficial in the cornea means above Bowman's layer — in the plane between Bowman's membrane and the epithelium.**\n\n**Book p.92 gives the word:** pannus is \"**superficial corneal vascularization** and cellular infiltration\", listed under the corneal signs of **active** trachoma along with superior epithelial keratitis. Everything else follows from *superficial*. The corneal layers, from `L15,16) Cornea`'s \"5 classic layers\", are **epithelium → Bowman's → stroma (substantia propria) → Descemet's → endothelium**; the vessels of a pannus creep in from the limbal arcades between the epithelium and Bowman's layer, which is why they are visible as a fine sheet lying on the surface and why the cornea beneath them can clear when they regress.\n\n**Why it comes from ABOVE, and why that is the point of the sign.** `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 28, titled \"Trachomatous Pannus\", photographs \"A sheet of fine, straight, **RADIAL** blood vessels… crossing the limbus from the **SUPERIOR** conjunctiva and invading the upper cornea, with a hazy grey infiltration of the cornea at their advancing edge\", and the deck's own note records that \"**the fact that the vessels come from ABOVE is carried by the picture alone**\". Superior pannus is the trachoma signature; vessels advancing from below or all round suggest a different cause. Slide 29, \"Trachomatous **Nebula ex Pannus**\", is the aftermath — a band of grey veil-like haze across the upper cornea, the vessels regressed. Because the vessels sit above Bowman's layer, and because Bowman's \"does **NOT** regenerate if damaged\" (`L15,16) Cornea`), what is left behind is a superficial scar rather than a deep one, and `opqb-t6-244` keys the other consequence of the plane: a pannus is continuous with the limbus and so has **no lucid interval**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What lies in that plane |\n|---|---|\n| **Beneath Descemet's membrane** | Only the **endothelium** — a single cell layer facing the anterior chamber. Nothing vascular gets there, and vessels in that position would be a retrocorneal membrane, not a pannus. |\n| **In the substantia propria** | The **stroma**, and this is the plane of **deep (interstitial) vascularization** — the vessels of interstitial keratitis, which are deep, dull, and do not regress into ghost vessels the way superficial ones do. Deep versus superficial is the whole distinction the question is testing. |\n| **Between Bowman's membrane & substantia propria** | A potential plane rather than a real one — a pannus does not dissect Bowman's off the stroma. The best distractor, because it names both correct structures in the wrong relation. |\n\n*(The corneal layers are from `L15,16) Cornea`. **\"Substantia propria\" as a synonym for the corneal stroma appears in NO cached ophthalmology source** — the cornea deck names the layer \"stroma\" throughout — so the term is the bank's own, and the superficial-versus-deep vascularization distinction is **not taken from the course material**: book ch.6 says only \"superficial\", and `L6` says nothing at all. It is standard, uncontested corneal anatomy.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** At the slit lamp, ask two questions of any corneal vessels: **which plane** and **which direction**. Fine, bright, branching vessels continuous with the conjunctival arcades and lying just under the epithelium are **superficial** — pannus, and in the superior cornea of a scarred eye, trachomatous. Dull vessels running within the stroma with no conjunctival continuity are **deep**, and point to interstitial keratitis instead.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Cornea — the plane being asked about is corneal, but the lesion is a trachomatous one.)*",
    objective:'Trachomatous pannus is a SUPERFICIAL vascularization: its vessels lie between Bowman’s membrane and the epithelium, advance from the SUPERIOR limbus, and leave a superficial nebula when they regress.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.40 (Q285), key p.43' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-286', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A patient complains of a **sandy sensation**. On examination there are areas of **epithelial oedema and subepithelial infiltration with inflammatory cells, surrounded by small blood vessels in a rosette shape, in the upper edge of the cornea**. What would you expect to find on everting the upper eyelid?',
    options:['Small expressible follicles.','Cobble stone papillae.','Pseudo membrane.','Diffuse edema with petechial hemorrhage.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Diagnosis is **Vernal keratoconjunctivitis**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim — it is the shortest box in the topic and it is the whole of what the bank prints here. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The bank states the diagnosis outright, so the question reduces to one step: what does the everted upper lid show in vernal keratoconjunctivitis? Giant flat-topped cobblestone papillae.** Book p.88's conjunctival-signs row: \"Diffuse conjunctival injection and **Tarsal giant papillae**\", illustrated by two photographs captioned \"Cobblestone papillae\"; book p.86 defines them as \"**more than 1mm in diameter**\" with a \"**Cobble stone appearance** i.e., they are flat-topped\".\n\n**And the corneal findings in the stem are VKC's own.** Book p.89's corneal-signs row lists \"**Punctate** epithelial **erosions**\" and the \"**Shield ulcer**: an oval ulcer typically in the **upper 1/3 of the cornea**… usually covered by a plaque made of fibrin and mucous\" — the *upper* cornea, exactly where this stem puts the lesion. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 44 pairs the two halves of the disease in one wordless slide: giant tarsal papillae arrowed three times above, and below them \"an oval, superficial, greyish-white **CORNEAL OPACITY / PLAQUE**… just above and slightly nasal to the pupil margin\", bracketed by four white arrows and never named. Slide 41 shows the limbal band with \"a hazy grey-white opacity spread**ing** DOWNWARD onto the upper cornea\".\n\n**⚠️ A TENSION INSIDE THIS TOPIC, RECORDED AND NOT CORRECTED.** The stem's word is **rosette**, and this same bank keys \"Corneal **Herbert's rosettes**\" to **active trachoma** at `opqb-t6-230`. Here the identical word is used of a VKC lesion and the bank's own box says so flatly. Both keys stand as printed; the entries name each other. If it helps, the discriminator is the rest of the picture:\n\n| | Trachoma | Vernal keratoconjunctivitis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Superior limbal lesion | **Herbert's rosettes** — limbal *follicles*, resolving into *pits* | Limbal *papillae* with **Trantas dots** |\n| Everted upper lid | **Follicles** (active) or **scarring**, Arlt's line (cicatricial) | **Giant cobblestone papillae** |\n| Upper corneal lesion | **Pannus** advancing from the superior limbus | **Shield ulcer** / punctate erosions |\n| Symptom | Irritation, foreign body sensation, discharge | **Intense itch**, ropy discharge |\n\nNote that option A, \"small expressible follicles\", is the trachoma answer — so the bank has built the alternative diagnosis into its own option list.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Small expressible follicles** | Viral, chlamydial or trachomatous disease — lymphoid aggregates, pale and rounded, maximal towards the fornix (`opqb-t6-268`). Not vernal disease, which gives papillae. |\n| **Pseudo membrane** | \"Coagulated exudates on the surface of the inflamed **intact** epithelium\", peeling **without** bleeding; book p.85 gives severe **gonococcal** infection as its cause, and book p.90 notes pseudomembranes in adenoviral disease. See `opqb-t6-237`. |\n| **Diffuse edema with petechial hemorrhage** | The bacterial picture — book p.85's item 4 puts conjunctival haemorrhages in \"some types of viral conjunctivitis and **severe bacterial** conjunctivitis\", and this bank attaches petechiae to the **pneumococcal** type at `opqb-t6-250`. |\n\n**⚠️ On \"sandy sensation\".** Book p.85's clinical feature 1 is \"**Foreign body sensation** with grittiness or feeling of sand in the eye\", which is non-specific across every conjunctivitis; here the diagnosis is carried by the corneal description and by the box, not by that phrase. Note that this vignette omits the symptom that would normally settle it — **itching** — which is what makes the stem harder than the disease.",
    objective:'Vernal keratoconjunctivitis pairs giant flat-topped cobblestone papillae on the everted upper lid with upper-corneal punctate erosions and the shield ulcer — this bank uses “rosette” of it here and of active trachoma at Q230.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.41 (Q286), key p.43 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-287', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A patient with **vernal keratoconjunctivitis** (the case described in `opqb-t6-286` — a sandy sensation with upper-corneal subepithelial infiltrates and giant tarsal papillae). Management must **not** include:',
    options:['Dark sun glasses.','Cold fomentation.','Systemic corticosteroids.','Topical steroids for a short period.','Mast ceil stabilizer.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Tx of VKC\n1. To avoid eye rubbing as much as possible (results in mast cell degranulation and leads to a vicious circle of more itching and eye rubbing)\n2. Dark glasses and cold compresses.\n3. Topical mast cell stabilizers 4. Topical antihistamines.\n4. Topical vasoconstrictor and anti-histaminic drops (during the attack only)\n5. Topical steroid to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use.\n6. Topical cyclosporine or topical tacrolimus may be considered for severe cases and as steroid sparing agents.\n**Systemic corticosteroids usually not indicated, reserved for severe refractory VKC or associated systemic disease**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. ⚠️ Its numbering is misprinted as it stands: item 3 reads \"Topical mast cell stabilizers 4. Topical antihistamines.\", so seven items run under six numbers and the numeral 4 appears twice. Kept exactly as printed. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ This question back-references the previous one.** \"In the previous Q\" is `opqb-t6-286`, the sandy-sensation vignette printed immediately above it in the same column of book p.41 — verified by reading that question rather than assuming, and it is the only preceding stem describing a patient. The antecedent is restated in the stem above so the question stands alone.\n\n**Every topical measure on this list is correct; the one that is not is the SYSTEMIC steroid.** The box's own last line says so, and it says why: systemic corticosteroids are \"usually **not** indicated, reserved for **severe refractory** VKC or associated systemic disease\". Book p.89's VKC treatment list — which the box otherwise reprints almost word for word — contains **no systemic drug at all**; every medication in it is prefaced \"**Medications: → Topical:**\".\n\n**Why the distinction is more than pedantry.** VKC is a **local surface allergy** in a child who will have it for years — book p.88 gives onset at 10–12 and spontaneous resolution \"in the **late second decade of life**\". Treating a decade of seasonal itch with systemic steroid would buy growth suppression, adrenal suppression, bone loss and immunosuppression for a disease confined to two conjunctival sacs. Even the **topical** steroid is hedged twice in this material — \"avoid long-term use\" (book p.89), \"**short course**\" (book p.94) — and `opqb-t6-225` keys what happens when that hedge is ignored: bilateral posterior subcapsular cataract in a twelve-year-old. That is also why book p.89 offers **cyclosporine and tacrolimus** as \"**steroid-sparing agents**\".\n\n**Why the other four are all correct management:**\n\n| Option | Where the material puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Dark sun glasses** | Box item 2 and book p.89: \"**Dark glasses** and cold compresses.\" They relieve the photophobia book p.88 lists as a cardinal symptom, and reduce the light exposure that provokes attacks. |\n| **Cold fomentation** | Box item 2 again; book p.87 \"**Cold compresses** to relieve itching\"; book p.94's allergic column. Cold is printed three times in the chapter as treatment and hot never — `opqb-t6-253` and `opqb-t6-276` key the same point. |\n| **Topical steroids for a short period** | Box item 5; book p.89 \"steroid to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use\"; book p.94 \"**Short course** of topical steroid provides symptomatic relief\". Correct, provided it is short. |\n| **Mast cell stabilizer** | Box item 3; book p.89's first topical. It is the maintenance drug — it prevents the degranulation that box item 1 blames for the itch–rub cycle — and it takes days to work, which is why an antihistamine is usually given alongside it. *(\"Mast ceil\" is the bank's misprint, kept.)* |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The shape of VKC treatment is: **stop the rubbing, cool and shade the eye, stabilise the mast cells for the season, block the histamine for breakthrough, use a short topical steroid for attacks, and reach for cyclosporine rather than a longer steroid if the attacks keep coming.** A systemic steroid enters the conversation only for severe refractory disease or when the atopy is systemic — and then it is a shared decision, not a reflex.",
    objective:'Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is treated topically — dark glasses, cold compresses, mast cell stabilizers, antihistamines, short steroid courses, cyclosporine as steroid-sparing; SYSTEMIC corticosteroids are reserved for severe refractory or systemic disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.41 (Q287), key p.43 (explanation box printed) — the stem back-references Q286 on the same page' },

  { id:'opqb-t6-288', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'What is the name of the sign shown below, and what is the disease?\n\n*(Figure: a slit-lamp photograph of the superior limbus and upper corneal margin. The bank prints no caption and no figure number.)*',
    options:['Herbet pits in trachoma','Trantas dots in trachoma','PTCS in trachoma','Stellate ulcer in trachoma'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The photograph shows a row of small, dark, rounded notches indenting the superior limbal margin — depressions, not deposits — and depressions at the superior limbus are Herbert's pits.** Every option names trachoma, so the question is purely *name the sign*.\n\n**Book p.92 defines them in one sentence:** \"**Herbert pits:** Superior limbal follicles may **resolve** to leave a **row of shallow depressions**\", filed under **cicatricial** trachoma alongside Arlt's line. Its figure prints \"a limbal close-up with black arrows pointing at a row of shallow notches, labelled **Herber't Pit**\" — the apostrophe misplaced in the in-figure label while the body text prints \"Herbert pits\". This bank's own box at `opqb-t6-230` reprints the sentence as item 2, and `opqb-t6-230` itself keys the *active* version of the same structure — **Herbert's rosettes**, the limbal follicles before they resolve.\n\n**Raised versus sunken is the whole discrimination**, and it is what separates the two chalky-looking limbal signs this topic keeps setting against each other:\n\n| | **Herbert's pits** | **Trantas dots** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Profile | **Depressions** — shallow notches in the limbal edge | **Raised** chalky deposits on the crest of a gelatinous band |\n| What they are | The scars of resolved limbal **follicles** | Collections of degenerated **eosinophils** (book p.88) |\n| Disease | **Cicatricial trachoma** | **Vernal keratoconjunctivitis** |\n| Company | Arlt's line, PTCs, trichiasis, entropion, pannus | Giant cobblestone papillae, ropy discharge, intense itch |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Trantas dots in trachoma** | The sign belongs to the wrong disease — book p.88 puts Tranta spots in VKC's limbal-signs row — and it is the wrong shape, being raised rather than sunken. ⚠️ Note the spelling: this option prints \"**Trantas**\", `opqb-t6-231` prints \"Trant's\" in its stem and \"Tranta\" in its box, and `opqb-t6-261` prints \"Taranta's\" — **four spellings of one eponym in one topic**. |\n| **PTCS in trachoma** | Post-trachomatous concretions **are** trachomatous — book p.93 gives them as a cicatricial sign causing \"chronic irritation and foreign body sensation\", and `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 15 titles a photograph \"PTCs\" without ever expanding the abbreviation. But they are **raised chalky-yellow nodules on the TARSAL conjunctiva**, not depressions at the limbus. This bank keys a photograph of them at `opqb-t6-255`. The strongest distractor, and the discriminator is **site**: tarsus versus limbus. |\n| **Stellate ulcer in trachoma** | Not a trachoma sign at all. What the book prints as *stellate* in trachoma is a pattern of **scars** — \"Linear or **stellate** conjunctival scars in mild cases\" (p.92) — on the tarsal conjunctiva, not an ulcer and not at the limbus. |\n\n**⚠️ The lecture deck photographs trachoma nine times and names none of these signs.** Measured across all 66 slides of `L6) Conjunctiva`: *Herbert* = **0**, *Arlt* = **0**, *Trantas* = **0**. Slide 15 titles \"PTCs\" and leaves the abbreviation unexpanded; slides 16, 31, 32 and 33 show the scarring progression untitled. Every name in this entry is the book's.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Herbert's pits are a **permanent scar**, so they are the sign that tells you a patient once had active trachoma even if the eye looks quiet now. Finding them means looking for what else the disease left — Arlt's line on the everted lid, PTCs, and above all **trichiasis and entropion**, because those are still doing damage and are still treatable (`opqb-t6-246`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A row of shallow depressions at the superior limbus is Herbert’s pits — the scars of resolved limbal follicles in cicatricial trachoma; Trantas dots are raised and allergic, and PTCs are raised and tarsal.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.41 (Q288), key p.43 — figure printed with the stem, no caption' }
